/* Instructor-grounded simulated differential diagnosis cases. */
window.VPQ_NECROPSY_DIFFERENTIAL_CASES={"version":13,"source":"Simulated pathology differential cases derived from instructor slide-audited Lectures 68, 69, 70, 72, 73, 74, 75, 77, 78 and 79; no actual patient records or slide images are reproduced.","cases":[{"id":"n68-brainstem","lecture":"68","system":"Neuro","title":"Circling ruminant: where is the lesion?","species":"Adult sheep","history":"An adult sheep fed poor-quality silage develops circling, head tilt, drooling and unilateral facial weakness. At necropsy, the brain has little obvious gross change; faint discoloration may be present in the brainstem.","gross":"Brainstem-focused lesion with little or no obvious gross change.","histo":"Multifocal neutrophilic microabscesses in brainstem parenchyma, sometimes mixed inflammatory cuffs around vessels.","best":"Listeriosis (Listeria monocytogenes)","morph":"Brainstem: multifocal, suppurative microabscessing encephalitis","mechanism":"Listeria can ascend via trigeminal and other cranial nerves by retrograde axonal transport, explaining the brainstem focus and asymmetric cranial nerve deficits.","tests":"Culture/PCR or other appropriate pathogen detection on relevant fresh tissues; necropsy localization and histology support but do not alone prove the agent.","ref":"Lecture 68 — Listeriosis, slide 10","options":[{"name":"Listeriosis (Listeria monocytogenes)","supports":"Silage exposure, circling, cranial nerve signs and brainstem localization all support this cause.","against":"Cannot confirm Listeria from these clinical signs alone.","hist":"Microabscessing suppurative rhombencephalitis.","role":"best"},{"name":"Thrombotic meningoencephalitis (Histophilus somni)","supports":"Ruminants may develop septic neurologic disease.","against":"Usually young feedlot cattle with random hemorrhagic foci and thrombosis/vasculitis rather than neural ascent to brainstem.","hist":"Septic vasculitis, thrombi and hemorrhagic infarction.","role":"less"},{"name":"Rabies (Lyssavirus)","supports":"Can cause cranial nerve signs and nonsuppurative brainstem inflammation.","against":"Typical rabies microscopy is nonsuppurative polioencephalomyelitis, not neutrophilic microabscesses.","hist":"Lymphocytic inflammation and possible Negri bodies (not invariably present).","role":"less"},{"name":"Equine protozoal myeloencephalitis","supports":"Asymmetric neurologic signs can occur with some CNS parasitic/protozoal conditions.","against":"Predominantly equine and frequently hemorrhagic/necrotizing spinal cord lesions; host argues strongly against.","hist":"Necrosis, hemorrhage, mixed inflammation and protozoal organisms occasionally detectable.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"n68-feedlot","lecture":"68","system":"Neuro","title":"Feedlot calf with hemorrhagic brain lesions","species":"8-month-old feedlot steer","history":"The steer develops fever and acute neurologic signs. Multiple irregular, dark-red foci are scattered at the cerebral gray–white interface.","gross":"Multifocal irregular cerebral hemorrhage and necrosis, sometimes with swelling or cloudy CSF.","histo":"Numerous small vessels show fibrinoid/necrotizing vasculitis, thrombi and adjacent infarction with hemorrhage.","best":"Thrombotic meningoencephalitis (Histophilus somni)","morph":"Brain: multifocal, hemorrhagic, necrotizing, thrombotic meningoencephalitis","mechanism":"H. somni bacteremia damages endothelium, initiating thrombosis, ischemic necrosis and leakage of blood. Distribution is vascular rather than perineural.","tests":"Bacterial testing of appropriate fresh CNS/systemic samples plus histopathology; consider systemic pneumonia, myocarditis or polyarthritis.","ref":"Lecture 68 — TME slides 11–12","options":[{"name":"Thrombotic meningoencephalitis (Histophilus somni)","supports":"Feedlot age, multifocal hemorrhage, gray–white junction lesions and thrombi/vasculitis are compelling.","against":"Agent still needs confirmatory identification.","hist":"Vasculitis, thrombosis, infarction.","role":"best"},{"name":"Listeriosis","supports":"Ruminant neurologic signs.","against":"Brainstem microabscesses and circling are more typical than multifocal cerebral vascular hemorrhage.","hist":"Neutrophilic brainstem microabscesses.","role":"less"},{"name":"Clostridium perfringens type D enterotoxemia","supports":"Can injure CNS vascular barrier and cause malacia.","against":"Often sheep/goats after ration change, preferentially bilaterally symmetric white-matter damage, rather than irregular thrombotic hemorrhages.","hist":"Edema and symmetric necrotic white matter foci.","role":"less"},{"name":"EHV-1 myeloencephalopathy","supports":"Vasculitis with thrombosis and hemorrhage can occur.","against":"Typically horses, not feedlot cattle; epidemiology argues strongly against it.","hist":"Small-vessel vasculitis, thrombosis and necrosis.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"n69-white-matter","lecture":"69","system":"Neuro","title":"Horse with acute cerebral malacia","species":"Adult horse","history":"A horse that has eaten contaminated moldy corn develops abrupt blindness, head pressing and seizures. On sectioning the brain, cerebral white matter is softened and liquefied while much of the gray matter is relatively spared.","gross":"Pale/yellow softened or cavitated regions concentrated in cerebral white matter.","histo":"Liquefactive necrosis and degeneration of white matter, with macrophages during lesion evolution.","best":"Fumonisin B1 equine leukoencephalomalacia","morph":"Cerebrum: multifocal to regionally extensive leukoencephalomalacia","mechanism":"Fusarium-associated fumonisin B1 toxicosis causes preferential severe injury to equine cerebral white matter. The professor notes its tissue selectivity is incompletely explained.","tests":"Feed/exposure history and toxin testing support the association; evaluate the lesion distribution and exclude other toxic/vascular causes.","ref":"Lecture 69 — Fumonisin B1 slide 35","options":[{"name":"Fumonisin B1 equine leukoencephalomalacia","supports":"Horse, moldy feed and white-matter liquefaction are the strongest combination.","against":"Specific fungal toxin exposure requires supporting feed/history evidence.","hist":"Severe white matter necrosis.","role":"best"},{"name":"Thiamine-deficiency polioencephalomalacia","supports":"Neurologic blindness, seizures and malacia.","against":"Classic ruminant lesion primarily affects cerebral cortical GRAY matter; species and location diverge.","hist":"Laminar cortical necrosis and softening.","role":"less"},{"name":"Centaurea-associated nigropallidal encephalomalacia","supports":"Horse toxic plant exposure and malacia possible.","against":"Preferential bilateral substantia nigra/globus pallidus lesions with chewing/prehension problems, not diffuse cerebral white matter injury.","hist":"Targeted neuronal necrosis in basal nuclei.","role":"less"},{"name":"Fibrocartilaginous embolism","supports":"Abrupt ischemic CNS malacia.","against":"Commonly spinal cord rather than this dietary-linked equine cerebral white matter syndrome.","hist":"Fibrocartilage occluding spinal vessel.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"n70-flaccid","lecture":"70","system":"PNS","title":"Acute flaccid paralysis with no gross nerve lesion","species":"Horse","history":"A horse develops progressively weak tongue, eyelids and pharyngeal muscles, then generalized flaccid paralysis after possible access to decaying carcass material. Gross sections of brain, cord and nerves appear unremarkable.","gross":"No characteristic gross CNS or PNS lesion despite severe neuromuscular dysfunction.","histo":"No specific microscopic lesion required; neurotoxin affects synaptic signaling rather than visibly destroying neurons.","best":"Botulism (Clostridium botulinum toxin)","morph":"Functional presynaptic neuromuscular blockade (often no characteristic morphologic lesion)","mechanism":"Botulinum neurotoxin blocks presynaptic acetylcholine release at neuromuscular junctions, causing flaccid paralysis.","tests":"Assess exposure history and use appropriate laboratory toxin testing in consultation with diagnostic laboratory; consider clinical differentials.","ref":"Lecture 70 — Botulism slides 18–19","options":[{"name":"Botulism (Clostridium botulinum toxin)","supports":"Flaccid generalized paralysis with cranial muscle involvement, carcass exposure and no structural lesion.","against":"Clinical presentation is not pathognomonic; toxin confirmation may be difficult.","hist":"No characteristic gross or microscopic lesion.","role":"best"},{"name":"Tetanus","supports":"Another clostridial neurologic toxicosis often without a gross lesion.","against":"Typical tetanus is SPASTIC rigidity due to loss of inhibitory motor control, not flaccid paralysis.","hist":"Often no characteristic neural lesion.","role":"less"},{"name":"Equine recurrent laryngeal neuropathy","supports":"Laryngeal dysfunction can contribute to abnormal airway signs.","against":"Chronic focal left laryngeal abductor muscle atrophy, not rapidly generalized paralysis of multiple muscle groups.","hist":"Distal axonal degeneration and denervation atrophy.","role":"less"},{"name":"Equine grass sickness","supports":"Autonomic/enteric dysfunction with minimal gross nerve lesions.","against":"Typically profound ileus, weight loss and dysautonomia rather than predominant neuromuscular junction paralysis.","hist":"Degeneration of autonomic ganglion neurons.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s72-crust","lecture":"72","system":"Skin","title":"Crusted muzzle lesions in a lamb","species":"Young lamb","history":"Several lambs develop proliferative crusts around their lips and muzzle after skin abrasions. A person handling them develops an ulcerated finger lesion. There is little respiratory illness in the affected lambs.","gross":"Thick raised proliferative crusting at mucocutaneous muzzle/lip sites.","histo":"Epidermal proliferation, ballooning degeneration and poxviral cytoplasmic inclusion bodies can occur.","best":"Contagious ecthyma (orf parapoxvirus)","morph":"Muzzle/lips: multifocal proliferative and crusting viral dermatitis","mechanism":"Parapoxvirus enters damaged skin and replicates in proliferating epidermal keratinocytes; zoonotic transmission is possible.","tests":"Clinical herd distribution and viral PCR where needed; use gloves and protective handling because lesions can be zoonotic.","ref":"Lecture 72 — Orf slide 11; sheeppox slides 6–7","options":[{"name":"Contagious ecthyma (orf parapoxvirus)","supports":"Young sheep, lip/muzzle proliferative crusts, abrasions and zoonotic human lesion.","against":"Poxvirus identity still requires testing if outbreak differential is important.","hist":"Proliferative viral epidermal injury and possible cytoplasmic inclusions.","role":"best"},{"name":"Sheeppox (capripoxvirus)","supports":"Sheep and skin papules/pustules/crusts can overlap.","against":"More generalized severe systemic disease, sparsely woolled skin and possible visceral lesions in many presentations.","hist":"Poxviral epidermal damage and inflammation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Dermatophilosis (Dermatophilus congolensis)","supports":"Crusted exudative bacterial dermatitis can resemble pox crusts.","against":"Typical rain-associated fleece/dorsal disease and bacterial branching filaments; zoonotic transmission context and oral localization fit orf better.","hist":"Segmented Gram-positive branching 'railroad-track' organisms in crust.","role":"less"},{"name":"Fungal dermatophytosis","supports":"Scaling and crusted alopecic lesions may occur.","against":"Follicular circular alopecia and fungal hairs are more characteristic than proliferative mucocutaneous crusts.","hist":"Fungal hyphae/arthroconidia associated with hair/keratin.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-pruritus","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Itchy cats sharing a home","species":"Three domestic cats","history":"Two cats become intensely pruritic and overgroom, producing symmetrical ventral hair loss; a third cat has few signs. Surface tape samples may show small mites; deep follicular scrapings are less useful.","gross":"Self-induced symmetrical alopecia with variable subtle scaling and no obligate follicular pustules.","histo":"Superficial mites in stratum corneum if captured, rather than mites within hair follicles.","best":"Demodex gatoi infestation","morph":"Skin: superficial parasitic dermatitis / alopecia caused by grooming","mechanism":"D. gatoi is a contagious surface-dwelling feline Demodex species. Asymptomatic carriers can maintain transmission.","tests":"Superficial scrapings/tape and household contact assessment; confirm mite species and exclude allergic pruritus.","ref":"Lecture 73 — Feline Demodex cati versus gatoi slide 6","options":[{"name":"Demodex gatoi infestation","supports":"Multiple cats, contagious pruritus/overgrooming and surface-dwelling mites, with asymptomatic carrier.","against":"Mites may be difficult to find due to grooming; other pruritic dermatoses can coexist.","hist":"Mites in stratum corneum.","role":"best"},{"name":"Demodex cati infestation","supports":"Feline demodicosis may cause alopecia and sometimes pruritus.","against":"D. cati lives in follicles/sebaceous glands and is often associated with immunosuppression; does not fit surface mite clue.","hist":"Mites in hair follicles.","role":"less"},{"name":"Feline atopic dermatitis","supports":"Symmetric self-induced alopecia and pruritus fit.","against":"Contagion across cats and observed surface mites are not explained by atopy alone.","hist":"Variable hypersensitivity dermatitis without Demodex.","role":"less"},{"name":"Cheyletiellosis","supports":"Contagious superficial mites may affect multiple cats.","against":"Dorsal flaky 'walking dandruff' more characteristic than this ventral self-induced alopecia pattern.","hist":"Surface mites with scale.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s74-blister","lecture":"74","system":"Skin","title":"Pustules on muzzle and pawpads","species":"Middle-aged dog","history":"The dog develops symmetrical fragile pustules and thick crusts on the nasal planum, pinnae and pawpads. Several pustules have ruptured into collarettes and crusts.","gross":"Symmetrical superficial pustules and crusts at face and pawpads.","histo":"Subcorneal pustules with acantholytic keratinocytes and inflammatory cells; deep mucosal ulceration is absent.","best":"Pemphigus foliaceus","morph":"Skin: multifocal subcorneal acantholytic pustular dermatitis","mechanism":"Autoantibodies to keratinocyte desmosomal adhesion proteins, particularly desmocollin-1 emphasized in lecture, cause superficial loss of cohesion.","tests":"Biopsy a fresh intact pustule for histology; investigate infectious mimics such as staphylococcal pyoderma and dermatophytosis.","ref":"Lecture 74 — Pemphigus foliaceus slide 11, pemphigus vulgaris slide 12, bullous pemphigoid slides 14–15","options":[{"name":"Pemphigus foliaceus","supports":"Symmetric superficial pustules, face/pawpad distribution and subcorneal acantholysis fit.","against":"Acantholysis can also accompany some infections; correlate and exclude those.","hist":"Subcorneal acantholytic pustules.","role":"best"},{"name":"Pemphigus vulgaris","supports":"Immune-mediated acantholysis and erosions.","against":"Usually deeper suprabasal separation and severe mucosal/mucocutaneous ulcers.","hist":"Suprabasal acantholysis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Bullous pemphigoid","supports":"Autoimmune blistering with erosions and crusting.","against":"Subepidermal cleavage at basement membrane, not subcorneal acantholytic pustules.","hist":"Subepidermal blister.","role":"less"},{"name":"Superficial bacterial pyoderma","supports":"Pustules, collarettes and crusts can look similar grossly.","against":"Histology plus cytology identifying cocci and bacterial follicular infection favor pyoderma; symmetric footpad distribution fits PF better.","hist":"Bacteria-associated neutrophilic folliculitis/pustules.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r75-testis","lecture":"75","system":"Repro","title":"Retained testis and feminization in a dog","species":"9-year-old intact male dog","history":"The dog has an abdominal retained testis, symmetrical alopecia and gynecomastia. At surgery, the retained testis contains a large firm white lobulated mass. CBC shows anemia and thrombocytopenia.","gross":"Large firm white fibrotic intratesticular mass in retained abdominal testis.","histo":"Neoplastic sustentacular Sertoli cells form cords/tubules, often within collagenous stroma.","best":"Sertoli cell tumor","morph":"Testis: Sertoli-cell neoplasm with suspected estrogen-associated marrow suppression","mechanism":"Some Sertoli neoplasms secrete estrogens and produce feminization; high estrogen can suppress hematopoiesis causing anemia, leukopenia or thrombocytopenia.","tests":"Testicular histopathology; evaluate cytopenias and clinical endocrine findings. Gross alone cannot prove hormone secretion.","ref":"Lecture 75 — Sertoli tumor slides 24/26 and cryptorchidism slides 13–14","options":[{"name":"Sertoli cell tumor","supports":"Abdominal cryptorchidism, firm white mass, feminization and cytopenias strongly support functional Sertoli tumor.","against":"Hormonal effects occur in only some cases and require clinical correlation.","hist":"Sertoli cell cords/tubules in firm fibrous stroma.","role":"best"},{"name":"Seminoma","supports":"Older dog and cryptorchid testis predisposition.","against":"Usually soft gray-white bulging tumor and nonfunctional; feminization and fibrotic cut surface favor Sertoli.","hist":"Sheets of neoplastic germ cells.","role":"less"},{"name":"Leydig cell tumor","supports":"Testicular tumor can sometimes have hormonal activity.","against":"Usually small yellow soft greasy nodule rather than large firm white fibrous mass.","hist":"Nests of interstitial endocrine cells.","role":"less"},{"name":"Suppurative orchitis","supports":"Testicular enlargement may arise from inflammation.","against":"Would expect inflammatory exudate and neutrophils rather than discrete white neoplastic mass with feminizing syndrome.","hist":"Neutrophilic testicular inflammation.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"r77-abortion","lecture":"77","system":"Repro","title":"Which placental lesion explains the abortion?","species":"Ewe with late-gestation abortion","history":"An ewe aborts in the last trimester. The fetus is well-preserved with few lesions. The cotyledons are dark red with dirty-red exudate; the intercotyledonary membranes are patchily thick, leathery and edematous. Another pregnant worker has helped handle abortion tissues.","gross":"Cotyledonary exudate AND extensive red-brown edematous/leathery intercotyledonary placentitis.","histo":"Necrotizing placentitis with inflammation/vasculitis can occur; intracellular organism identification requires ancillary tests.","best":"Chlamydia abortus (ovine enzootic abortion)","morph":"Placenta: multifocal to coalescing necrotizing exudative cotyledonary and intercotyledonary placentitis","mechanism":"C. abortus is an obligate intracellular zoonotic pathogen of sheep/goats; the instructor's speaker notes emphasize placental pathology in both compartments and late-pregnancy abortion.","tests":"Submit placenta and fetal samples for PCR/appropriate testing. Use zoonotic precautions, especially avoid exposure for pregnant people.","ref":"Lecture 77 Catch-Up PowerPoint speaker notes slides 40–41","options":[{"name":"Chlamydia abortus (ovine enzootic abortion)","supports":"Late ovine abortion, fetal preservation and BOTH placental compartments affected in characteristic leathery/exudative fashion.","against":"Other causes of severe placentitis overlap grossly; organism testing remains necessary.","hist":"Placental necrosis/vasculitis/inflammation; organisms can be difficult to distinguish without tests.","role":"best"},{"name":"Toxoplasma gondii","supports":"Ewe abortion and cotyledonary involvement.","against":"Classic distinctive scattered 1–3 mm white cotyledon nodules with largely normal intercotyledonary tissue, unlike broad leathery involvement.","hist":"Protozoal infection and focal placental necrosis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Coxiella burnetii (Q fever)","supports":"Late ovine abortion, thickened placenta, zoonotic risk and significant intercotyledonary exudate.","against":"Professor emphasizes abundant off-white exudate and often mineralization; can overlap heavily, so it remains a strong differential.","hist":"Marked placentitis with intracellular organisms; confirm by PCR.","role":"less"},{"name":"Campylobacter fetus subsp. fetus / C. jejuni","supports":"Sheep abortion and placentitis.","against":"More characteristic fetal targetoid liver necrosis when lesions are present; current description highlights placenta.","hist":"Fetal necrotizing hepatitis can occur.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"e78-corneal","lecture":"78","system":"Eye","title":"Dark corneal lesion in a cat","species":"Adult cat","history":"A cat has a history of recurrent corneal surface disease. A sharply defined brown-black plaque is present centrally on a cloudy cornea, with blood vessels extending toward the lesion.","gross":"Focal dark brown/black plaque surrounded by stromal opacity and corneal neovascularization.","histo":"Well-demarcated devitalized, acellular corneal stroma rather than intralesional hyphae or eosinophil-rich plaque.","best":"Feline corneal sequestrum","morph":"Cornea: focal stromal necrosis / sequestrum","mechanism":"Corneal sequestra represent focal stromal devitalization, often after chronic ulceration; precise initiating pathogenesis remains uncertain.","tests":"Ophthalmic exam and assessment for ulceration, depth and concurrent ocular disease; histopathology when tissue removed.","ref":"Lecture 78 — Corneal sequestrum slide 38; fungal and eosinophilic keratitis slides 34–35","options":[{"name":"Feline corneal sequestrum","supports":"Cat, focal dark devitalized plaque, surrounding opacity and neovascularization are characteristic.","against":"Concurrent corneal infection/ulceration may still require evaluation.","hist":"Acellular necrotic corneal stromal focus.","role":"best"},{"name":"Equine fungal keratitis","supports":"Corneal ulceration and severe necrosis can occur.","against":"More characteristic of horses; fungal hyphae and necrosuppurative keratitis versus focal black plaque in a cat.","hist":"Necrosuppurative keratitis with fungal hyphae.","role":"less"},{"name":"Feline eosinophilic keratitis","supports":"Cats may develop proliferative corneal plaques.","against":"Typically white-pink lateral corneal/conjunctival plaques, not a focal black necrotic sequestrum.","hist":"Predominantly eosinophilic infiltrates.","role":"less"},{"name":"Corneal dermoid","supports":"A congenital focal corneal lesion may resemble a mass.","against":"Dermoid contains hair follicles/adnexal glands rather than an acquired necrotic pigmented plaque.","hist":"Ectopic skin appendages in cornea.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"ear79-bulla","lecture":"79","system":"Ear","title":"Older Cocker spaniel with a keratin-filled bulla","species":"Older Cocker Spaniel","history":"The dog has a long history of recurrent otitis externa/media and new head tilt. Necropsy reveals an expansile cyst-like lesion in the tympanic bulla packed with pale flaky lamellated material.","gross":"Expansile middle-ear cyst with keratinous contents, rather than free liquid pus.","histo":"Keratin within an epidermal-lined lesion with fibrovascular stroma, granulomatous inflammation and cholesterol clefts/crystals.","best":"Tympanokeratoma (aural cholesteatoma)","morph":"Middle ear: keratinizing epithelial cyst / tympanokeratoma","mechanism":"Epidermal cyst associated with tympanic membrane/chronic otitis expands with retained keratin, compressing/eroding surrounding structures.","tests":"Histology of the middle-ear lesion, culture if infection coexists, and imaging as appropriate.","ref":"Lecture 79 — Tympanokeratoma slide 20, otitis media slide 18, inflammatory polyp slide 21","options":[{"name":"Tympanokeratoma (aural cholesteatoma)","supports":"Older Cocker Spaniel, chronic otitis and keratin-filled bulla with cholesterol granulomatous reaction.","against":"Concurrent bacterial otitis media is possible and should not be missed.","hist":"Keratin debris with fibrovascular/granulomatous stroma and cholesterol crystals.","role":"best"},{"name":"Suppurative otitis media","supports":"Chronic history, head tilt and middle-ear expansion.","against":"Would expect tan-yellow mucoid/purulent inflammatory exudate rather than an organized keratinous cyst.","hist":"Neutrophilic exudate ± bacteria.","role":"less"},{"name":"Aural inflammatory polyp","supports":"Middle-ear mass can protrude toward canal or nasopharynx.","against":"Most often young cats with smooth off-white pedunculated fibrovascular mass, not an older dog with packed keratin cyst.","hist":"Inflamed fibrovascular core lined by respiratory/squamous epithelium.","role":"unlikely"},{"name":"Ceruminous gland adenocarcinoma","supports":"Neoplastic ear masses may be locally invasive.","against":"Ceruminous gland tumors primarily external ear canal, cats overrepresented; not a typical lamellated keratin bulla cyst.","hist":"Invasive glandular neoplasia.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"n69-pem","lecture":"69","system":"Neuro","title":"Blind feedlot lamb: gray versus white matter","species":"6-month-old feedlot lamb","history":"After a rapid shift to a high-concentrate ration, a lamb becomes dull, develops cortical blindness and head-pressing, and then has seizures. No obvious ocular abnormality explains the blindness. The cerebral cortex is subtly swollen and yellow-gray; some gyri are flattened.","gross":"Bilaterally distributed cortical swelling, yellow discoloration and softening; chronic lesions may become cavitated. A Wood's lamp may demonstrate cortical autofluorescence, but absence does not exclude the disease.","histo":"Laminar neuronal necrosis in the cerebral gray matter, with neuronal eosinophilia and nuclear pyknosis, neuropil vacuolation, edema and variable macrophage reaction depending on duration.","best":"Polioencephalomalacia (cerebrocortical necrosis)","morph":"Cerebrum: bilateral, laminar, cortical neuronal necrosis (polioencephalomalacia)","mechanism":"Polioencephalomalacia describes the cortical lesion pattern, not one uniquely identified agent. In ruminants it can follow disruption of thiamine availability/metabolism or high sulfur exposure. Energy failure preferentially injures metabolically active cortical neurons.","tests":"Assess diet, ration transition, sulfur in water/feed and possible thiamine-related risk factors; evaluate response to treatment clinically. Histology establishes the lesion pattern but cannot distinguish sulfur-associated from thiamine-associated injury alone.","ref":"Lecture 69 — cortical necrosis/polioencephalomalacia topic; external veterinary diagnostic literature (confirm exact slide against lecture source)","options":[{"name":"Polioencephalomalacia (cerebrocortical necrosis)","supports":"Young ruminant, sudden blindness, ration change and bilaterally affected cerebral cortex strongly fit.","against":"Histology alone does not establish whether sulfur excess or thiamine disturbance was responsible.","hist":"Laminar cerebral cortical neuronal necrosis; gray-matter predominant.","role":"best"},{"name":"Fumonisin-associated equine leukoencephalomalacia","supports":"Seizures, blindness and cerebral malacia overlap.","against":"Equine disease with preferential cerebral white-matter damage; wrong species and tissue compartment.","hist":"Severe liquefactive WHITE-matter necrosis.","role":"unlikely"},{"name":"Lead toxicosis","supports":"Can cause acute cortical neurologic disease and blindness in ruminants.","against":"Requires a credible lead source and compatible systemic findings; cortical damage can overlap, so gross lesions alone are not decisive.","hist":"Cortical neuronal degeneration, edema and sometimes vascular lesions; lead confirmation relies on tissue/blood analysis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Salt poisoning / water deprivation","supports":"Can cause neurologic signs and cortical injury in some species.","against":"Strongly associated with pigs and relevant water/salt history; characteristic eosinophilic perivascular cuffing is more associated with porcine cases.","hist":"Laminar cortical neuronal injury, sometimes eosinophilic meningoencephalitis in pigs.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"n68-bse","lecture":"68","system":"Neuro","title":"Adult bovine: microscopic vacuoles and behavioral change","species":"5-year-old cow","history":"An adult cow develops a progressively abnormal temperament, hypersensitivity to sound and touch, incoordination and loss of condition. The brain appears grossly unremarkable; the teaching slide shows neuropil and neuronal vacuoles within selected brainstem nuclei.","gross":"No characteristic macroscopic CNS lesions despite progressive neurologic disease.","histo":"Bilateral symmetric spongiform vacuolation of neuropil, sometimes intraneuronal vacuoles, with neuronal loss and gliosis in characteristic brainstem regions. Distribution and validated testing are essential.","best":"Bovine spongiform encephalopathy (BSE)","morph":"Brainstem: spongiform encephalopathy (microscopic vacuolation)","mechanism":"Abnormally folded prion protein promotes misfolding and accumulation of host prion protein, leading to progressive neurodegeneration with little conventional inflammatory response.","tests":"Treat as a reportable prion-disease concern: contact the responsible regulatory veterinarian and diagnostic laboratory for approved collection and testing. Histologic vacuoles alone are not BSE-specific. Avoid unvalidated handling or testing claims.","ref":"Lecture 68 — spongiform encephalopathy/prion differential; USDA public-domain BSE histology reference (verify exact lecture slide alignment)","options":[{"name":"Bovine spongiform encephalopathy (BSE)","supports":"Adult bovine, progressive behavioral changes, ataxia and characteristic brainstem spongiform change support consideration.","against":"Cannot diagnose BSE on history or vacuolation alone; laboratory confirmation and exclusion of other causes are necessary.","hist":"Characteristic spongiform change involving selected brainstem nuclei with little inflammation.","role":"best"},{"name":"Rabies","supports":"Behavior change, hypersensitivity and neurologic dysfunction require consideration.","against":"Rabies generally features nonsuppurative encephalitis rather than the characteristic spongiform distribution.","hist":"Lymphocytic polioencephalitis, possible intracytoplasmic Negri bodies; confirmation uses validated assays.","role":"less"},{"name":"Toxic/metabolic vacuolar encephalopathy","supports":"Some toxicoses and metabolic conditions can cause neuropil/myelin vacuolation.","against":"Distribution, clinical course and ancillary evidence determine cause; vacuoles are not prion-specific.","hist":"Variable white matter or neuropil vacuolation and lesion distribution.","role":"less"},{"name":"Listeriosis","supports":"Common ruminant CNS infection can produce ataxia and cranial nerve deficits.","against":"Usually focal/asymmetric brainstem signs with suppurative microabscesses rather than noninflammatory spongiform change.","hist":"Suppurative microabscessing rhombencephalitis.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r76-granulosa","lecture":"76","system":"Female repro","title":"Mare with behavior change and one enlarged ovary","species":"11-year-old mare","history":"A mare has intermittent stallion-like behavior and failure to exhibit regular estrus. Rectal palpation and ultrasound identify one markedly enlarged multicystic ovary and a small, relatively inactive contralateral ovary. The ovarian cut surface is pale yellow-white with numerous cystic spaces.","gross":"Unilateral enlarged, smooth, solid-to-multicystic ovarian mass, yellow-white cut surface; contralateral ovarian atrophy.","histo":"Neoplastic granulosa cells in follicle-like microstructures (Call-Exner bodies may be present), cords or sheets, often with theca-cell proliferation; histologic architecture varies.","best":"Granulosa-theca cell tumor","morph":"Ovary: unilateral sex cord–stromal neoplasm (granulosa-theca cell tumor)","mechanism":"Functional neoplastic sex cord–stromal cells may produce inhibin and androgenic or estrogenic steroids, inhibiting follicle development in the opposite ovary and altering estrous or stallion-like behavior. Hormone patterns vary.","tests":"Ultrasonography, anti-Müllerian hormone and inhibin/testosterone where indicated; histopathology of removed ovary establishes subtype. Hormone concentrations are supportive, not substitutes for tissue diagnosis.","ref":"Lecture 76 catch-up PowerPoint, slides 3–4 speaker notes (ovarian tumor and granulosa-theca cell tumor)","options":[{"name":"Granulosa-theca cell tumor","supports":"Unilateral multicystic mass, contralateral atrophy and hormone-associated behavioral change fit.","against":"Some tumors are solid and not hormonally active; ultrasound alone cannot classify every ovarian mass.","hist":"Sex cord–stromal cords/follicular patterns; possible Call-Exner bodies.","role":"best"},{"name":"Persistent anovulatory follicle / follicular cyst","supports":"Ovarian cystic enlargement and abnormal cycling may overlap.","against":"Less convincing for a large mixed solid multicystic tumor with marked contralateral suppression and prolonged behavioral changes.","hist":"Cystic follicle lined by granulosa cells, without neoplastic sex cord proliferation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Ovarian epithelial cystadenoma","supports":"Ovarian epithelial tumors may form large multicystic masses.","against":"Typically lack the functional endocrine and contralateral atrophy pattern.","hist":"Cysts lined by neoplastic epithelial cells, sometimes papillary.","role":"less"},{"name":"Ovarian teratoma","supports":"Germ-cell neoplasms may enlarge an ovary.","against":"Expected heterogeneous tissue such as hair, bone or cartilage; endocrine effects less characteristic.","hist":"Differentiated tissues from multiple germ layers.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"r76-pyometra","lecture":"76","system":"Female repro","title":"Intact dog with distended uterine horns","species":"9-year-old intact female dog","history":"Several weeks after estrus, a dog develops lethargy, polyuria/polydipsia and anorexia without visible vaginal discharge. Both uterine horns are markedly enlarged and fluid-filled. The endometrium bears numerous small cystic glands.","gross":"Tensely distended uterine horns containing thick yellow-green suppurative material; cystic endometrial glandular change. An apparently closed cervix can prevent vaginal discharge.","histo":"Neutrophilic luminal exudate, endometrial glandular cystic hyperplasia, variable mucosal necrosis and inflammatory extension depending on severity.","best":"Canine pyometra associated with cystic endometrial hyperplasia","morph":"Uterus: diffuse suppurative endometritis with luminal pus (pyometra), associated with cystic endometrial hyperplasia","mechanism":"Progesterone-dominant diestrus increases glandular secretion and reduces uterine contractility/clearance; ascending opportunistic bacteria, frequently E. coli, can establish infection and trigger endotoxemia.","tests":"Urgent systemic evaluation, imaging, CBC/chemistry and bacterial culture where appropriate; histopathology to assess inflammation and CEH. Clinical suspicion warrants timely stabilization and treatment.","ref":"Lecture 76 slides 17 and 21–24: CEH, metritis, canine pyometra and species distinctions","options":[{"name":"Canine pyometra associated with cystic endometrial hyperplasia","supports":"Diestrus, PU/PD, systemic illness, luminal pus, and neutrophilic endometritis.","against":"Gross purulence alone cannot identify bacterial species or degree of systemic compromise.","hist":"Suppurative endometritis with or without CEH.","role":"best"},{"name":"Mucometra","supports":"Distended uterus containing viscous material can mimic a fluid-filled pyometra on imaging.","against":"Sterile mucus without marked neutrophilic exudate or illness does not explain the suppurative findings.","hist":"Mucus-filled lumen with little/no suppurative inflammation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Hydrometra","supports":"Fluid-distended uterus could be similar grossly.","against":"Thin watery nonpurulent fluid and minimal uterine inflammation would favor hydrometra.","hist":"Watery luminal fluid without a major neutrophilic exudate.","role":"less"},{"name":"Postpartum metritis","supports":"Fetid uterine exudate and systemic bacterial illness are possible.","against":"Recent parturition or retained fetal membranes would usually be expected; metritis affects multiple uterine-wall layers.","hist":"Often more diffuse full-thickness uterine-wall inflammation.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r77-campy","lecture":"77","system":"Abortion","title":"Aborted lamb with distinctive liver lesions","species":"Late-gestation ewe and aborted fetus","history":"Several ewes abort late in gestation. One fetus has multiple approximately round pale target-like foci in the liver; the placenta is thickened and exudative. Other aborted fetuses have no obvious characteristic hepatic lesions.","gross":"Fetal liver: multifocal targetoid pale necrotic foci, sometimes with red or raised margins. Placenta may be edematous, necrotic and covered in exudate.","histo":"Suppurative to necrotizing placentitis, placental bacterial microcolonies, sometimes vasculitis, fetal pneumonia and random necrosuppurative hepatitis.","best":"Ovine Campylobacter-associated abortion (C. jejuni or C. fetus subsp. fetus)","morph":"Placenta: necrosuppurative placentitis; fetus: multifocal necrotizing hepatitis, consistent with bacterial abortion","mechanism":"Maternal intestinal exposure and bacteremia permit placental colonization and fetal infection; a compromised exchange surface and direct fetal infection lead to abortion. Target-like hepatic lesions are strongly suggestive but not pathognomonic.","tests":"Submit fresh placenta, fetal abomasal contents, lung and liver for appropriate culture/PCR (laboratory guidance); fixed placenta and fetal organs for histology. Observe zoonotic abortion-tissue precautions.","ref":"Lecture 77 PowerPoint slides 36–38 speaker notes (Campylobacter species and target lesions)","options":[{"name":"Ovine Campylobacter-associated abortion (C. jejuni or C. fetus subsp. fetus)","supports":"Late abortion with multifocal targetoid fetal liver necrosis and necrosuppurative placentitis is a high-yield pattern.","against":"Characteristic liver foci are inconsistent among cases and are not sufficient to identify the species.","hist":"Bacterial placentitis with microcolonies; fetal necrosuppurative hepatitis and pneumonia.","role":"best"},{"name":"Chlamydia abortus","supports":"Late ewe abortion with severe placentitis and little fetal pathology is common.","against":"Typically striking intercotyledonary leathery change and dirty red-brown exudate rather than targetoid fetal liver lesions.","hist":"Necrotizing placentitis, sometimes vasculitis; organism requires specific testing.","role":"less"},{"name":"Coxiella burnetii","supports":"Ruminant abortion with severe placentitis, possibly with few fetal lesions.","against":"Fetal targetoid hepatic foci are less characteristic, and many cases have limited fetal changes.","hist":"Severe trophoblast-rich necrotizing placentitis; intracellular bacteria may be abundant.","role":"less"},{"name":"Brucella melitensis","supports":"Zoonotic ovine/caprine late abortion with placentitis and fetal pneumonia.","against":"No unique liver target signature; laboratory confirmation and public health precautions are necessary.","hist":"Necrotizing placentitis and fetal bronchopneumonia.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r77-ehv","lecture":"77","system":"Abortion","title":"Sudden third-trimester abortion in a mare","species":"Pregnant mare, approximately month 10","history":"A mare with no obvious preceding fever or reproductive discharge aborts a relatively fresh fetus. The fetal lungs do not fully collapse, are heavy and edematous; the liver has multiple small pale foci, and the thymus may show petechiae. The placenta has no dominant cervical-star exudative lesion.","gross":"Fresh fetus with pulmonary edema and small pale foci in liver and/or lungs, possible body-cavity fluid and petechiae; gross findings may be mild or absent.","histo":"Multifocal hepatic necrosis with intranuclear viral inclusions in hepatocytes and sometimes other affected cells; pulmonary and lymphoid tissue injury can occur.","best":"Equine herpesvirus 1 abortion","morph":"Fetus: multifocal hepatic necrosis and pulmonary edema, consistent with viral fetal infection","mechanism":"Maternal EHV-1 infection can produce cell-associated viremia and fetal/placental infection late in gestation; fetal systemic infection and tissue necrosis follow, often without warning to the dam.","tests":"PCR on appropriate fetal lung, liver, thymus and placenta; histopathology and diagnostic laboratory guidance. Apply farm biosecurity during abortion investigation.","ref":"Lecture 76 slide 34 (EHV-1 abortion); Lecture 77 fetal abortion investigation slides 26–29","options":[{"name":"Equine herpesvirus 1 abortion","supports":"Abrupt late abortion, fresh fetus, pulmonary edema and multifocal hepatic necrosis strongly support.","against":"Some EHV-1 abortions lack distinct lesions; PCR is needed to confirm.","hist":"Hepatic necrosis with possible eosinophilic intranuclear inclusions.","role":"best"},{"name":"Ascending bacterial placentitis","supports":"Common cause of equine abortion and fetal infection.","against":"Usually suppurative placentitis prominent at cervical star; fetal liver viral-type inclusions would not fit.","hist":"Neutrophilic chorioallantoitis with bacteria; fetal bronchopneumonia possible.","role":"less"},{"name":"Leptospiral abortion","supports":"Can cause late equine abortion with placental edema and fetal liver lesions.","against":"Often requires renal/placental pathogen evidence; typical viral intranuclear inclusions not explained.","hist":"Variable nephritis, hepatitis and placentitis without viral inclusions.","role":"less"},{"name":"Umbilical cord torsion","supports":"May cause sudden noninfectious fetal hypoxia and death.","against":"Cord twisting/vascular compromise should be apparent; does not explain viral inclusion-bearing hepatic necrosis.","hist":"Hypoxic/vascular tissue injury, no infectious cytopathic inclusions.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r77-mummy","lecture":"77","system":"Abortion","title":"Small retained fetal remains: mummy or maceration?","species":"Multiparous sow","history":"A sow farrows several healthy piglets with two much smaller dark, desiccated, intact fetal forms. These have leathery skin and are not foul-smelling. No purulent uterine fluid is evident. Determine the process before assuming an infectious cause.","gross":"Shrunken dry brown-black fetal remnants maintaining fetal outline, without gas, pus or marked decomposition.","histo":"Extensive postmortem dehydration and autolysis; little diagnostic intact fetal histology is expected. Absence of purulent uterine inflammation supports an aseptic retention process.","best":"Fetal mummification","morph":"Uterine contents: retained desiccated fetuses (mummification)","mechanism":"Fetal death after sufficient skin development and retention in a relatively sterile uterine environment permits water resorption while a continuing pregnancy or luteal function prevents immediate expulsion. The morphology is an outcome, not an etiologic diagnosis.","tests":"Assess gestational stage, sizes and count of affected fetuses, placenta, sow reproductive history and herd pattern. In swine consider porcine parvovirus among causes of variable-size mummies; determine etiology separately by appropriate fetal/placental testing.","ref":"Lecture 77 catch-up PowerPoint slides 16–20 speaker notes (mummification, maceration, emphysematous fetus); Lecture 76 slide 29 PPV/SMEDI","options":[{"name":"Fetal mummification","supports":"Sterile-appearing, dark leathery, intact dry fetus in mixed-size litter.","against":"Cannot establish which infection or noninfectious event caused fetal death.","hist":"Desiccated autolyzed retained fetal tissues.","role":"best"},{"name":"Fetal maceration","supports":"Retained dead fetal tissue could also break down over time.","against":"Maceration generally involves bacterial infection, fetal tissue disintegration and pus, often with retained bones.","hist":"Suppurative uterine inflammation and decomposed fetal remains.","role":"less"},{"name":"Emphysematous fetus","supports":"Dead retained fetus may become infected.","against":"Would expect gas-producing putrefaction, swelling, crepitation and malodor, not dry intact tissues.","hist":"Putrefactive tissue changes with bacterial overgrowth.","role":"unlikely"},{"name":"Normal stillbirth","supports":"Perinatal fetal death can accompany a live-born litter.","against":"Normal-size fresh stillborn fetus does not explain chronically retained, small dry fetal remnants.","hist":"Variable findings depending on death timing; not characteristic dry mummification.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r77-toxo","lecture":"77","system":"Abortion","title":"White cotyledonary foci and fetal brain necrosis","species":"Ewe with late-pregnancy loss","history":"An ewe aborts in late gestation. Several cotyledons have small well-demarcated pale spots; intercotyledonary membranes are not uniformly leathery. Histology has yet to be interpreted. A regional veterinarian is investigating the flock's fetal losses.","gross":"Multiple pale pinpoint-to-millimeter necrotic/mineralized foci centered on placental cotyledons; fetal gross lesions may be absent.","histo":"Multifocal necrotizing placentitis with protozoal organisms in trophoblasts; fetal nonsuppurative necrotizing encephalitis can be present. Demonstration of parasite antigen/DNA strengthens etiologic attribution.","best":"Toxoplasma gondii-associated ovine abortion","morph":"Placenta: multifocal necrotizing cotyledonary placentitis; fetus: multifocal necrotizing encephalitis","mechanism":"Maternal ingestion of sporulated feline-origin oocysts permits parasitemia and transplacental infection; infected trophoblast and fetal tissues develop necrosis. Fetal outcome depends in part on gestation stage.","tests":"Placental and fetal brain PCR plus histology and immunohistochemistry where available; maternal serology alone does not prove abortion etiology. Protect pregnant/immunocompromised handlers from fetal and placental tissues.","ref":"Lecture 77 abortion and placentitis differential; Dorsch et al. 2022, Front Vet Sci Figure 3 (open-access CC BY diagnostic case)","options":[{"name":"Toxoplasma gondii-associated ovine abortion","supports":"Cotyledonary white foci and fetal necrotizing encephalitis fit; placental histology can demonstrate protozoa.","against":"Cotyledonary pallor alone is not diagnostic; need specific tissue confirmation.","hist":"Focal cotyledonary trophoblast necrosis with protozoal organisms; fetal necrotizing encephalitis.","role":"best"},{"name":"Campylobacter-associated ovine abortion","supports":"Late abortion and necrotizing placentitis are compatible.","against":"More characteristic fetal targetoid liver foci and bacterial placental microcolonies; do not confuse these with white cotyledon foci.","hist":"Necrosuppurative placentitis with bacterial microcolonies; fetal hepatitis/pneumonia.","role":"less"},{"name":"Chlamydia abortus","supports":"Late ewe abortion with placentitis.","against":"More typically extensive dirty red-brown leathery intercotyledonary chorioallantoitis.","hist":"Necrotizing placentitis, with specific agent demonstrated by PCR/IHC.","role":"less"},{"name":"Coxiella burnetii","supports":"Marked ruminant placentitis with little fetal injury can occur.","against":"Cotyledonary pale foci plus fetal necrotizing encephalitis are not its strongest pattern.","hist":"Severe necrotizing placentitis, often numerous intracellular bacteria in trophoblasts.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r75-ram-epididymis","lecture":"75","system":"Male repro","title":"Ram with a firm unilateral epididymal tail","species":"3-year-old breeding ram","history":"At breeding soundness examination the ram has decreased fertility and a hard enlarged cauda epididymidis on one side. A cut section shows fibrous thickening, focal creamy debris and distended ducts containing inspissated sperm. Some affected rams in the flock have no palpable changes.","gross":"Firm enlarged epididymal tail, fibrosis and multifocal abscess-like or sperm-granulomatous foci; testis may be atrophic.","histo":"Chronic interstitial lymphoplasmacytic and suppurative epididymitis, duct obstruction, sperm leakage and foreign-body granulomatous reaction; bacterial identification requires ancillary testing.","best":"Brucella ovis-associated epididymitis","morph":"Epididymis: chronic active, fibrosing, sperm-granulomatous epididymitis","mechanism":"B. ovis establishes chronic reproductive-tract infection; duct inflammation and obstruction rupture sperm-containing ducts, exposing sperm antigens to the interstitium and generating granulomatous inflammation, fibrosis and impaired sperm transport.","tests":"Brucella ovis flock testing/serology and organism-specific PCR or culture under diagnostic laboratory guidance. Palpation alone misses subclinical carriers; local reporting and herd policies vary.","ref":"Lecture 75 slides 19–20 (ram epididymitis: Brucella ovis versus Actinobacillus seminis)","options":[{"name":"Brucella ovis-associated epididymitis","supports":"Sexually mature ram, characteristic cauda epididymis involvement, flock infertility and possible subclinical carriers.","against":"Clinical and gross appearance alone cannot distinguish all bacterial causes.","hist":"Chronic active epididymitis with periductal fibrosis and sperm granulomas.","role":"best"},{"name":"Actinobacillus seminis epididymitis","supports":"Can cause pyogranulomatous epididymitis and infertility in rams.","against":"More often affects younger rams; significant overlap means culture/PCR is decisive.","hist":"Suppurative/pyogranulomatous epididymitis, not etiologically distinct by H&E alone.","role":"less"},{"name":"Noninfectious sperm granuloma after trauma","supports":"Ruptured ducts release sperm and generate granulomatous reaction.","against":"Flock-associated epididymitis and infectious inflammation increase suspicion of infection.","hist":"Foreign-body giant-cell inflammation around extravasated sperm, with little bacterial evidence.","role":"less"},{"name":"Testicular seminoma","supports":"May cause a firm gonadal mass and infertility.","against":"Primary lesion is epididymal rather than a discrete intratesticular germ-cell tumor.","hist":"Sheets of neoplastic germ cells within testis.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"r75-prostate","lecture":"75","system":"Male repro","title":"Older dog with pelvic outlet obstruction and bone pain","species":"11-year-old neutered male dog","history":"A dog has tenesmus, dysuria, progressive weight loss, and reluctance to walk. Rectal examination reveals an irregular firm fixed prostate; imaging shows mineralization, sublumbar lymphadenopathy and new proliferative lesions of lumbar vertebrae.","gross":"Irregular infiltrative mineralized prostate, sometimes asymmetrical, with firm adjacent tissue and regional lymph node enlargement.","histo":"Infiltrative malignant epithelial cells in irregular tubules, nests or solid sheets, marked atypia and stromal desmoplasia. Tumor type and origin may require immunohistochemistry.","best":"Canine prostatic carcinoma","morph":"Prostate: infiltrative malignant epithelial neoplasm (prostatic carcinoma)","mechanism":"Malignant prostatic epithelium or urothelial-type carcinoma infiltrates local structures, obstructs urinary/fecal passage and may metastasize to nodes, lungs and axial skeleton.","tests":"Imaging and tissue/cytology assessment with histopathology and immunohistochemistry as needed; distinguish primary prostatic from urothelial carcinoma. Metastasis staging is clinically important.","ref":"Lecture 75 slides 28–31 (prostatic cysts, prostatitis, benign hyperplasia and carcinoma)","options":[{"name":"Canine prostatic carcinoma","supports":"Firm irregular mineralized prostate, neutered status and possible axial skeletal metastases.","against":"Prostatic mineralization and inflammation can overlap; obtain tissue characterization.","hist":"Infiltrating atypical glandular/urothelial-like epithelial cells.","role":"best"},{"name":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia","supports":"Older male dog with prostatomegaly and straining.","against":"Usually intact, smoother and symmetric enlargement; infiltrative fixed lesion plus metastases does not fit.","hist":"Orderly hyperplastic glands with epithelial proliferation and minimal invasion.","role":"less"},{"name":"Prostatic abscess / bacterial prostatitis","supports":"Can cause painful enlargement, focal cavities and dysuria.","against":"Fever, infection evidence and purulent exudate would support infection; osseous lesions are not typical metastases from abscess.","hist":"Suppurative inflammation and destruction, sometimes bacterial colonies.","role":"less"},{"name":"Paraprostatic cyst","supports":"May cause marked pelvic mass effect and straining.","against":"Usually an extraprostatic cystic structure, rather than fixed mineralized infiltrative gland.","hist":"Cyst wall with variable lining, not infiltrative malignant glands.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"r76-mastitis","lecture":"76","system":"Mammary","title":"Sudden agalactia versus gangrenous mastitis","species":"Postpartum dairy cow","history":"A cow has sudden severe depression, fever, and greatly reduced milk production in one quarter. The secretion is thin, watery and blood tinged. The affected gland is markedly swollen and edematous, with sharply demarcated dusky necrotic areas and systemic signs of endotoxemia.","gross":"Marked edema and hemorrhage, necrotic mammary tissue and watery abnormal milk from one involved quarter.","histo":"Severe neutrophilic and necrotizing mastitis, with vascular injury and edema; histology alone does not reliably identify an individual coliform species.","best":"Acute severe coliform (E. coli) mastitis","morph":"Mammary gland: acute severe necrosuppurative and hemorrhagic mastitis","mechanism":"Ascending intramammary Gram-negative bacterial infection activates powerful innate inflammatory responses, including LPS-driven systemic inflammatory and vascular effects that damage the gland and cause shock.","tests":"Aseptically collected milk culture/PCR as appropriate, assessment for systemic inflammatory response and shock, fluid/electrolyte status, and clinical management. Necrosis pattern is not organism-specific.","ref":"Lecture 76 slides 37–42 (coliform, staphylococcal, streptococcal, and Mycoplasma mastitis)","options":[{"name":"Acute severe coliform (E. coli) mastitis","supports":"Peracute severe systemic illness, one affected quarter, watery/bloody secretion and necrotizing edema.","against":"Staphylococcal gangrene can look very similar; culture needed.","hist":"Acute necrotizing neutrophilic mastitis and marked edema/vascular injury.","role":"best"},{"name":"Staphylococcus aureus gangrenous mastitis","supports":"Can cause gangrenous blue-black mammary necrosis and severe toxemia.","against":"Clinical pattern and histology overlap; culture rather than gross color proves agent.","hist":"Necrosuppurative mastitis and often coccal aggregates.","role":"less"},{"name":"Mycoplasma bovis mastitis","supports":"Agalactia and rapidly affected herd mates are typical.","against":"Often multiple quarters, firm swollen udder and floccular milk with less overt severe endotoxemic crisis.","hist":"Variable suppurative mastitis; confirmation with specific molecular testing/culture.","role":"less"},{"name":"Streptococcal mastitis","supports":"Milk quality changes and inflamed quarter are plausible.","against":"Often less striking vascular necrosis and shock in comparison; overlap possible.","hist":"Neutrophilic mastitis with bacteria, requiring organism testing.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r77-mycotic","lecture":"77","system":"Abortion","title":"Leathery bovine placenta with fetal skin plaques","species":"Dairy cow, last third of pregnancy","history":"A cow aborts a late-gestation fetus. The placenta has thick, opaque, leathery intercotyledonary membranes and enlarged necrotic cotyledons. Raised gray-white crusting lesions are present on the fetal head and neck. A section of placenta reveals abundant branching hyphae invading chorionic tissue.","gross":"Necrotizing and thickened leathery placenta, sometimes adventitial placentation, with focal gray-white raised fetal skin plaques and possible pneumonia.","histo":"Necrotizing placentitis with invading fungal hyphae; septate acute-angle branching hyphae favor Aspergillus, whereas broader pauciseptate hyphae favor certain Mucorales. Morphology should be confirmed by culture or molecular methods.","best":"Mycotic abortion, most suggestive of Aspergillus spp.","morph":"Placenta: severe necrotizing fungal placentitis with fetal mycotic dermatitis","mechanism":"Environmental fungi enter maternal circulation and colonize placentomes, spread through fetal membranes and impair placental exchange; placental and fetal infection cause fetal death. Exact fungal species cannot be established by gross morphology alone.","tests":"Submit fresh and fixed placenta, fetal skin lesions, lung and stomach contents; perform fungal culture/PCR, H&E, PAS and GMS stains. Species identification requires laboratory support.","ref":"Lecture 77 slide 44 speaker notes (Aspergillus-associated bovine abortion); veterinary diagnostic pathology literature","options":[{"name":"Mycotic abortion, most suggestive of Aspergillus spp.","supports":"Leathery intercotyledonary placentitis plus fetal fungal dermatitis and invading septate hyphae.","against":"A mold species must not be named from gross appearance alone; Mucorales differ microscopically.","hist":"Fungal invasion of placental stroma/vessels; septate hyphae on special stain.","role":"best"},{"name":"Brucella abortus abortion","supports":"Necrotizing placentitis and fetal pneumonia occur.","against":"Fungal hyphae in placenta and fetal skin favor mycotic etiology; brucellosis requires specific testing.","hist":"Necrosuppurative placentitis with bacteria rather than true tissue-invasive fungal hyphae.","role":"less"},{"name":"Neospora caninum abortion","supports":"Important bovine transplacental cause of late abortion.","against":"Usually fetal nonsuppurative encephalitis and myocarditis, not dense fungal placentitis and cutaneous plaques.","hist":"Necrotizing nonsuppurative encephalitis and myocarditis with protozoal organisms.","role":"less"},{"name":"Chlamydial placentitis","supports":"Necrotizing placentitis can occur in ruminants.","against":"Does not produce tissue-invasive branching hyphae.","hist":"Trophoblast necrosis/inflammation with intracellular bacteria, not fungi.","role":"unlikely"}]},{"id":"r77-neospora","lecture":"77","system":"Abortion","title":"Bovine fetus with encephalitis but few gross lesions","species":"Cow aborting at 6 months gestation","history":"A beef herd experiences several mid-gestation abortions and repeat losses among certain dam lines. The fetuses are often autolyzed with no dramatic external lesions. Fetal brain and heart histology shows scattered areas of necrosis and mononuclear inflammation.","gross":"Often no distinctive gross fetal lesion; autolysis can obscure findings. Occasionally focal pale tissue changes.","histo":"Multifocal necrotizing nonsuppurative encephalitis and myocarditis, often with glial response; intralesional protozoal cysts/tachyzoites may be sparse.","best":"Neospora caninum-associated bovine abortion","morph":"Fetus: multifocal necrotizing nonsuppurative encephalitis and myocarditis","mechanism":"Transplacental Neospora infection, including endogenous reactivation in persistently infected dams, injures fetal neural and cardiac tissues; vertical transmission explains recurrence in dam lines.","tests":"Fetal brain/heart histopathology with Neospora PCR and immunohistochemistry where available; dam serology informs herd risk but does not alone establish causation.","ref":"Lecture 77 slide 46 speaker notes (Neospora caninum bovine abortion)","options":[{"name":"Neospora caninum-associated bovine abortion","supports":"Mid-gestation cattle abortions, recurrence in dam lines, fetal nonsuppurative encephalitis and myocarditis.","against":"Protozoal morphology may be sparse and Toxoplasma/other etiologies can overlap; confirm by specific tests.","hist":"Multifocal necrotizing nonsuppurative encephalitis and myocarditis.","role":"best"},{"name":"Bovine viral diarrhea virus fetal infection","supports":"Can cause fetal loss and neurologic malformations.","against":"Hydranencephaly, cerebellar hypoplasia or ocular lesions depend on infection timing; necrotizing myocarditis plus protozoal encephalitis is more suggestive of Neospora.","hist":"Developmental CNS abnormalities or nonspecific inflammatory/necrotic changes.","role":"less"},{"name":"Bovine herpesvirus 1 abortion","supports":"Can produce fetal hepatic necrosis and abortion.","against":"Hepatic multifocal necrosis, sometimes viral intranuclear inclusions, more typical than neural/cardiac protozoal pattern.","hist":"Fetal hepatocellular necrosis with possible intranuclear inclusions.","role":"less"},{"name":"Toxoplasma gondii","supports":"Related protozoan can cause fetal necrotizing encephalitis.","against":"Ovine/caprine abortion more characteristic; bovine abortion due to Toxoplasma is uncommon, species testing essential.","hist":"Protozoal-associated necrotizing encephalitis; immunostains/PCR differentiate.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r76-teratoma","lecture":"76","system":"Female repro","title":"Ovarian mass with hair and mineralized material","species":"4-year-old intact female dog","history":"Exploratory surgery for abdominal distention reveals one enlarged ovarian mass. Its cut surface contains cysts filled with greasy keratinous material and hair, interspersed with firm white mineralized fragments. The other ovary is grossly normal.","gross":"Heterogeneous unilateral ovarian mass with hair, sebum, cysts and bone/cartilage-like material, a strong but not pathognomonic gross pattern.","histo":"Well-differentiated tissue derivatives from at least two germ layers, potentially cutaneous adnexa, cartilage, bone, adipose, neural, or glandular/respiratory-like epithelium.","best":"Mature ovarian teratoma","morph":"Ovary: mature germ-cell teratoma with multiple differentiated tissue types","mechanism":"A totipotent ovarian germ cell differentiates aberrantly into multiple somatic tissue lineages. A mature teratoma may be benign yet can exert mass effects; malignant potential requires histopathologic assessment.","tests":"Submit representative sections of solid and cystic regions for histopathology; inspect for immature or malignant components and evaluate local extension. Gross tissue diversity strongly directs the differential but alone does not grade behavior.","ref":"Lecture 76 catch-up PPT slide 3 and slide 6 speaker notes; original lecture 76 slide 52 (ovarian teratoma)","options":[{"name":"Mature ovarian teratoma","supports":"Hair, sebum and cartilage/bone within an ovarian tumor strongly favor divergent germ-layer tissue differentiation.","against":"Gross findings cannot exclude an immature/malignant component; adequate histologic sampling is essential.","hist":"Differentiated tissues representing at least two embryonic germ layers.","role":"best"},{"name":"Granulosa-theca cell tumor","supports":"Ovarian mass may contain many cysts and appear yellow-white.","against":"Hair and cartilage are not explained by sex cord–stromal neoplasia; associated endocrine effects may favor granulosa-theca.","hist":"Granulosa-cell rosettes/cords and variable thecal cells, no ectopic mature cartilage/hair.","role":"less"},{"name":"Ovarian epithelial cystadenoma","supports":"Cystic epithelial tumor may become large and multilocular.","against":"Cyst walls are epithelial rather than containing hair follicles and bone.","hist":"Cysts lined by relatively orderly epithelial cells with possible papillae.","role":"less"},{"name":"Ovarian dysgerminoma","supports":"Germ-cell origin and unilateral ovarian enlargement overlap.","against":"Usually soft solid gray-white mass rather than differentiated hair/bone-containing cysts.","hist":"Sheets of primitive germ cells, lacking mature mixed tissue types.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r76-ovarian-epithelial","lecture":"76","system":"Female repro","title":"Ovarian papillary mass with abdominal implants","species":"8-year-old intact female dog","history":"An intact female dog has progressive abdominal distention and ascites. A complex ovarian mass has numerous delicate papillary projections extending through a disrupted bursal surface, with small similar nodules on peritoneal surfaces.","gross":"Irregular ovarian papillary/cystic mass with peritoneal implants and effusion; assess the ovarian bursa and serosal distribution.","histo":"Malignant ovarian epithelial cells line complex papillary fronds, with pleomorphism, mitoses, stromal invasion and possible peritoneal implants.","best":"Ovarian papillary cystadenocarcinoma","morph":"Ovary: malignant papillary epithelial neoplasm with transcoelomic spread","mechanism":"Malignant surface/subsurface epithelial neoplasia invades the ovarian capsule/bursa and sheds cells throughout the peritoneal cavity, causing implants and sometimes effusion.","tests":"Histopathology of ovarian mass and peritoneal deposits; sample effusion and stage involvement of abdomen. Papillary gross morphology alone does not establish invasion.","ref":"Lecture 76 catch-up PPT slide 5 speaker notes; original Lecture 76 slide 51","options":[{"name":"Ovarian papillary cystadenocarcinoma","supports":"Papillary epithelial mass, disrupted bursal boundary and peritoneal implants support malignant epithelial spread.","against":"Determine exact epithelial origin and invasion histologically; other peritoneal malignancies are possible.","hist":"Invasive atypical epithelial papillae with metastatic implants.","role":"best"},{"name":"Ovarian papillary cystadenoma","supports":"Papillary cystic architecture is compatible.","against":"Benign adenoma should not invade or produce malignant serosal implants.","hist":"Orderly papillae without stromal invasion.","role":"less"},{"name":"Granulosa-theca cell tumor","supports":"Large ovarian neoplasm may have cystic spaces and ascites.","against":"Papillary epithelial lining and peritoneal epithelial implants favor carcinoma.","hist":"Sex-cord stromal cellular patterns, sometimes Call-Exner bodies.","role":"less"},{"name":"Primary peritoneal mesothelioma","supports":"Serosal nodules and effusion may mimic disseminated ovarian carcinoma.","against":"Dominant ovarian primary and epithelial ovarian architecture support ovarian origin.","hist":"Mesothelial growth patterns; immunohistochemistry may be necessary.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r75-equine-scc","lecture":"75","system":"Male repro","title":"Older gelding with ulcerated penile mass","species":"18-year-old gelding","history":"An older gelding has recurrent blood-tinged preputial discharge and difficulty retracting the penis. A firm ulcerated irregular plaque and nodular mass occupy the glans; regional inguinal nodes are mildly enlarged.","gross":"Firm infiltrative ulcerated proliferative penile/preputial mass, potentially extending into deeper tissues.","histo":"Invasive islands and cords of atypical squamous epithelium penetrate the stroma; variable keratin pearls, intercellular bridges, desmoplasia and mitoses.","best":"Equine penile squamous cell carcinoma","morph":"Penis: infiltrative keratinizing squamous cell carcinoma","mechanism":"Malignant transformation of squamous epithelium produces local invasion and may involve equine papillomavirus 2; persistent genital papillomatous lesions can be a risk context, but viral DNA does not establish causation for an individual tumor.","tests":"Multiple biopsies including lesion edges and invasive base; assess penile depth, inguinal lymph nodes and metastatic spread. Histology determines invasive versus in situ disease.","ref":"Lecture 75 slides 35–37 (penile tumors and EcPV2-associated equine SCC)","options":[{"name":"Equine penile squamous cell carcinoma","supports":"Older horse with firm ulcerative invasive glans lesion and enlarged nodes.","against":"Biopsy needed to show invasion; papilloma and carcinoma in situ may look similar grossly.","hist":"Invasive atypical squamous nests with keratinization/desmoplasia.","role":"best"},{"name":"Squamous papilloma","supports":"Exophytic genital squamous growth can occur and may be EcPV associated.","against":"Typical benign papilloma remains exophytic without invasive nests in deeper connective tissue.","hist":"Papillary fronds with fibrovascular cores and orderly squamous epithelium.","role":"less"},{"name":"Bovine papillavirus-type fibropapilloma","supports":"Cauliflower-like genital masses can be confusing.","against":"Classically young bulls rather than aged geldings, with a prominent fibrous stromal core.","hist":"Papillomatous epithelial hyperplasia over fibroblastic stroma.","role":"unlikely"},{"name":"Equine sarcoid","supports":"Fibroblastic cutaneous mass can involve preputial skin.","against":"Histology dominated by dermal spindle-cell fibroblastic proliferation without invasive malignant squamous islands.","hist":"Dermal fibroblast proliferation with overlying epidermal changes.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r75-tvt","lecture":"75","system":"Male repro","title":"Bleeding cauliflower-like penile mass in a dog","species":"3-year-old intact male free-roaming dog","history":"The dog has sanguineous preputial discharge and a friable red lobulated mass at the cranial glans. Cytology shows many discrete round cells with pale cytoplasmic vacuoles, prominent nuclei and mitotic figures.","gross":"Soft pink-red multilobulated friable mucosal mass that bleeds readily; may have ulceration.","histo":"Sheets of round neoplastic cells with distinct cell borders, round nuclei, variably vacuolated cytoplasm, high cellularity and frequent mitoses, with thin fibrovascular septa.","best":"Canine transmissible venereal tumor (TVT)","morph":"Penis: transmissible round-cell neoplasm (TVT)","mechanism":"TVT is a clonally transmissible cancer in which living tumor cells implant during genital contact or close contact with affected lesions; it is not a conventional viral papilloma.","tests":"Fine-needle aspiration or impression cytology often diagnostic; histopathology when needed and assess extragenital lesions and lymph nodes. Distinguish from lymphoma and other round-cell tumors using cytology and appropriate testing.","ref":"Lecture 75 slide 38 (canine TVT); diagnostic textbook TVT histology/cytology references","options":[{"name":"Canine transmissible venereal tumor (TVT)","supports":"Sexual/contact history, friable genital mass and vacuolated round cells match TVT.","against":"Round-cell cytology can overlap with other tumors; verify representative features.","hist":"Round neoplastic cells with cytoplasmic vacuoles and mitoses.","role":"best"},{"name":"Canine genital papilloma","supports":"Genital exophytic mass can have cauliflower appearance.","against":"Papilloma is an epithelial frond rather than sheets of discrete vacuolated round cells.","hist":"Papillary stratified squamous epithelium over fibrovascular cores.","role":"less"},{"name":"Lymphoma","supports":"A genital extranodal round-cell tumor is possible.","against":"TVT-type cytoplasmic vacuolation and typical location strongly favor TVT; lymphocyte phenotype requires testing.","hist":"Monomorphic atypical lymphoid cells without classic TVT vacuolation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Squamous cell carcinoma","supports":"Ulcerated bleeding penile mass is possible.","against":"Cohesive malignant squamous cells/keratinization rather than discrete vacuolated round cells.","hist":"Invasive squamous epithelial nests and keratinization.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r77-coxiella","lecture":"77","system":"Abortion","title":"Abortion storm with leathery placenta and minimal fetal lesions","species":"Goat herd in late gestation","history":"Several does abort late in gestation. Fetuses are fresh or mildly autolyzed with no distinctive lesions. The placenta is thick, leathery, and coated by abundant off-white exudate, especially between placentomes. Laboratory microscopy shows numerous small intracellular coccobacilli in distended trophoblasts.","gross":"Thickened opaque chorioallantois with abundant cream/off-white intercotyledonary exudate and possible placental mineralization, sometimes without specific fetal lesions.","histo":"Severe necrotizing placentitis, with trophoblasts swollen by abundant intracellular small Gram-negative coccobacilli; confirm with immunohistochemistry/PCR due to overlapping agents.","best":"Coxiella burnetii-associated placentitis (Q fever)","morph":"Placenta: severe diffuse necrotizing placentitis with intracellular bacteria","mechanism":"C. burnetii replicates in placental trophoblasts, leading to high organism burden, necrosis and abortion. Infected placentas and aerosols create a substantial zoonotic risk, including for pregnant people.","tests":"Coordinate diagnostic sampling with the laboratory; placental PCR/IHC plus histology and herd epidemiology. Avoid aerosols, use appropriate protective measures and notify public/animal health authorities as required.","ref":"Lecture 77 slide 42 speaker notes (Q fever); published bovine and caprine pathology literature","options":[{"name":"Coxiella burnetii-associated placentitis (Q fever)","supports":"Placenta-predominant late abortion with profound trophoblast infection and thick exudate fits.","against":"Similar gross placentitis occurs with Chlamydia and others; confirm organism by specific tests.","hist":"Necrotizing placentitis with numerous intracellular organisms in trophoblasts.","role":"best"},{"name":"Chlamydia abortus (enzootic abortion)","supports":"Late small-ruminant abortion with leathery intercotyledonary necrotizing placentitis strongly overlaps.","against":"Gross alone usually cannot separate these; PCR/IHC and microbe-specific testing is needed.","hist":"Necrotizing placentitis with intracellular obligate bacteria; histology can overlap.","role":"less"},{"name":"Brucella melitensis","supports":"Caprine late abortion with necrotizing placentitis and zoonotic hazard.","against":"Fetal pneumonia and different organism testing may favor brucellosis; gross overlap remains substantial.","hist":"Necrosuppurative placentitis with bacteria, possibly fetal bronchopneumonia.","role":"less"},{"name":"Toxoplasma gondii","supports":"Small-ruminant abortion and placental necrosis can occur.","against":"More typical multifocal pale cotyledon foci and fetal encephalitis than extensive bacteria-laden intercotyledonary placentitis.","hist":"Protozoal-associated cotyledonary necrosis and fetal necrotizing encephalitis.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r77-trich","lecture":"77","system":"Abortion","title":"Repeat-breeder cattle herd with infected bulls","species":"Beef cattle herd, natural service","history":"Several cows repeatedly return to estrus after breeding and a minority develop pyometra or early abortion. Older herd bulls appear clinically normal, but preputial samples from one bull contain motile flagellated protozoa by a laboratory-validated assay.","gross":"Usually minimal specific gross lesions; cows may have mild cervicitis, endometritis, uterine pus or variable placental thickening. Bulls often have no visible changes.","histo":"Mild-to-moderate lymphoplasmacytic or mixed inflammatory endometritis/placentitis; organisms may be difficult to detect histologically and microscopic findings are nonspecific.","best":"Tritrichomonas foetus-associated bovine trichomoniasis","morph":"Uterus: variable endometritis/pyometra secondary to venereally transmitted protozoal infection","mechanism":"T. foetus colonizes the preputial crypts of carrier bulls and spreads during mating. In cows genital infection causes early embryonic death, repeat breeding and occasionally pyometra or abortion.","tests":"Validated PCR/culture on appropriately collected bull preputial material or female genital samples; follow state herd disease-control and testing requirements. Microscopic motility alone should not stand in for species identification.","ref":"Lecture 77 slides 47–48 speaker notes (Tritrichomonas foetus cattle trichomoniasis)","options":[{"name":"Tritrichomonas foetus-associated bovine trichomoniasis","supports":"Natural service, older carrier bulls, early reproductive loss and protozoa in preputial material.","against":"Requires validated test for T. foetus; organisms with similar morphology can be confused.","hist":"Often nonspecific endometritis, diagnosis supported by organism PCR/culture.","role":"best"},{"name":"Campylobacter fetus subsp. venerealis","supports":"Also causes venereal infertility and repeat breeding with apparently normal carrier bulls.","against":"Bacterial rather than protozoal organism; needs Campylobacter-specific testing.","hist":"Variable mild genital inflammation; histology is not specific.","role":"less"},{"name":"Bovine viral diarrhea virus","supports":"Can cause embryonic/fetal loss and poor reproductive performance.","against":"Bull preputial protozoa and venereal herd pattern favor trichomoniasis, not enough alone without testing.","hist":"Fetal infection may produce variable developmental or inflammatory lesions.","role":"less"},{"name":"Brucella abortus","supports":"Infectious infertility and abortion in cattle require consideration.","against":"More characteristically later abortion, necrotizing placentitis and fetal pneumonia rather than prominent early repeat-breeding pattern.","hist":"Placental necrosuppurative lesions and fetal pneumonia.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s71-hepatocutaneous","lecture":"71","system":"Skin","title":"Painful crusting footpads with a honeycomb liver","species":"Older diabetic dog","history":"An older dog with weight loss and poor body condition develops painful hyperkeratotic fissured footpads, crusts around mucocutaneous junctions, and recurrent superficial bacterial infection. Liver ultrasound reveals a distinctive honeycomb-like pattern.","gross":"Bilaterally symmetric fissured and crusting footpads plus periocular/perioral and pressure-point erosions; hepatic nodular regenerative/degenerative pattern.","histo":"Epidermal parakeratosis above vacuolated/edematous pale spinous cells and basal hyperplasia, giving the classic red-white-blue layering.","best":"Superficial necrolytic dermatitis (hepatocutaneous syndrome)","morph":"Skin: multifocal superficial necrolytic dermatitis; liver: associated metabolic/hepatocellular disorder","mechanism":"Severe metabolic derangements in amino acid availability and chronic hepatic dysfunction contribute to ineffective epidermal maintenance and superficial epidermal necrosis; this is a clinical histopathologic syndrome rather than a specific infectious agent.","tests":"Skin biopsies from suitable lesion edges, serum chemistry/amino acid assessment, hepatic imaging and workup for hepatic disease or rarer glucagonoma; culture secondary infection when needed.","ref":"Lecture 71 slide 39 — superficial necrolytic dermatitis","options":[{"name":"Superficial necrolytic dermatitis (hepatocutaneous syndrome)","supports":"Signalment, lesion distribution and microscopic pattern together provide the strongest match; agent or syndrome confirmation still requires the listed tests.","against":"Pattern recognition is suggestive rather than a stand-alone etiologic confirmation.","hist":"Epidermal parakeratosis above vacuolated/edematous pale spinous cells and basal hyperplasia, giving the classic red-white-blue layering.","role":"best"},{"name":"Zinc-responsive dermatosis","supports":"Footpad crusting and parakeratotic scale can overlap.","against":"Hepatic honeycomb pattern plus red-white-blue necrolytic histology favor hepatocutaneous syndrome.","hist":"Parakeratotic hyperkeratosis and epidermal hyperplasia without the diagnostic pale laminar epidermal necrolysis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Pemphigus foliaceus","supports":"Crusting pawpads and facial areas can occur.","against":"Subcorneal acantholytic pustules differ from broad laminar necrolytic epidermal change.","hist":"Subcorneal pustules with acantholytic keratinocytes.","role":"less"},{"name":"Canine hypothyroidism","supports":"Alopecia and recurrent bacterial dermatitis may occur.","against":"Painful fissured footpads and characteristic epidermal necrolysis are atypical.","hist":"Follicular atrophy/telogenization and variable hyperkeratosis, not red-white-blue epidermal layering.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s71-photosense","lecture":"71","system":"Skin","title":"White-skinned sheep with sun-exposed crusts and icterus","species":"Adult sheep","history":"A light-skinned sheep develops painful crusted erosions of ear tips, muzzle and unpigmented dorsal skin after turnout. Pigmented skin remains relatively spared; sclerae are icteric and the liver has cholestatic disease.","gross":"Ulcerative/crusted dermatitis of UV-exposed, sparsely haired unpigmented sites with evidence of hepatobiliary disease.","histo":"Epidermal necrosis, edema and variably neutrophilic interface/superficial inflammation; histology alone does not identify the photosensitizing compound.","best":"Hepatogenous (type III) photosensitization","morph":"Skin: photodistributed epidermal necrosis and ulceration associated with cholestasis","mechanism":"Chlorophyll metabolism produces phylloerythrin; impaired biliary clearance causes its accumulation in skin, where ultraviolet light generates reactive tissue injury.","tests":"Investigate hepatic/biliary injury, relevant plants/mycotoxins, liver enzymes and bilirubin; differentiate primary photosensitization by lack of hepatobiliary involvement.","ref":"Lecture 71 slides 25–26 — types I, II and III photosensitization","options":[{"name":"Hepatogenous (type III) photosensitization","supports":"Signalment, lesion distribution and microscopic pattern together provide the strongest match; agent or syndrome confirmation still requires the listed tests.","against":"Pattern recognition is suggestive rather than a stand-alone etiologic confirmation.","hist":"Epidermal necrosis, edema and variably neutrophilic interface/superficial inflammation; histology alone does not identify the photosensitizing compound.","role":"best"},{"name":"Primary type I photosensitization","supports":"Photodistributed unpigmented-skin injury looks similar.","against":"Usually direct ingestion/contact with photodynamic agent without required cholestasis.","hist":"Photo-induced epidermal necrosis; histology usually cannot identify the exposure class.","role":"less"},{"name":"Dermatophilosis","supports":"Crusts can form on the face and dorsum of sheep.","against":"Wet-weather bacterial crusts are not obligatorily limited to unpigmented UV-exposed skin or associated with icterus.","hist":"Neutrophilic exudative epidermitis containing branching segmented bacterial filaments.","role":"less"},{"name":"Cutaneous lupus erythematosus","supports":"Sun-exacerbated muzzle/ear lesions may occur.","against":"Underlying cholestasis and strict depigmented photodistribution favor photosensitization.","hist":"Lymphocytic interface dermatitis and basal keratinocyte injury.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s72-greasy-pig","lecture":"72","system":"Skin","title":"Piglets covered in dark greasy exudate","species":"2-week-old nursing piglets","history":"Several piglets in a litter develop erythema around face and ears followed by rapidly spreading sticky brown exudate and dehydration. The epidermis sloughs superficially and deeper subcutaneous abscesses are absent.","gross":"Diffuse superficial greasy brown crusts and exudate, initially facial/aural then generalizing.","histo":"Superficial/subcorneal acantholysis or pustules with crusting, bacterial cocci and limited deep dermal invasion; toxin-mediated epidermal exfoliation.","best":"Exudative epidermitis (greasy pig disease; toxigenic Staphylococcus hyicus)","morph":"Skin: diffuse superficial exfoliative and exudative bacterial epidermitis","mechanism":"Exfoliative toxins produced by certain S. hyicus strains disrupt keratinocyte adhesion, allowing marked superficial exudation; dehydration and electrolyte disturbance can become severe in small piglets.","tests":"Skin lesion culture and confirmation of relevant toxin-producing staphylococcal infection when needed; assess hydration and hygiene/predisposing skin injury.","ref":"Lecture 72 slide 38 — greasy pig disease","options":[{"name":"Exudative epidermitis (greasy pig disease; toxigenic Staphylococcus hyicus)","supports":"Signalment, lesion distribution and microscopic pattern together provide the strongest match; agent or syndrome confirmation still requires the listed tests.","against":"Pattern recognition is suggestive rather than a stand-alone etiologic confirmation.","hist":"Superficial/subcorneal acantholysis or pustules with crusting, bacterial cocci and limited deep dermal invasion; toxin-mediated epidermal exfoliation.","role":"best"},{"name":"Porcine juvenile pustular psoriasiform dermatitis","supports":"Young pigs can have crusting and scaling.","against":"Noninfectious psoriasiform lesions do not usually produce overwhelming sticky toxigenic exudate and dehydration.","hist":"Psoriasiform epidermal hyperplasia and pustulation rather than typical staphylococcal exfoliation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Swinepox","supports":"Piglets can develop generalized papules, pustules and crusts.","against":"Discrete pox papules and epidermal viral inclusions differ from spreading greasy exfoliative dermatitis.","hist":"Ballooning degeneration and intracytoplasmic poxviral inclusions.","role":"less"},{"name":"Porcine erysipelas","supports":"Can produce erythematous skin lesions and systemic illness.","against":"Diamond-shaped vasculitic infarcts rather than diffuse greasy superficial epidermal crusts.","hist":"Dermal vascular injury, thrombosis and necrosis.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s72-dermatophilus","lecture":"72","system":"Skin","title":"Rain-soaked horse with paintbrush crusts","species":"Adult horse","history":"After persistent rain, a horse develops tightly adherent crusts over the back and rump. Hair tufts lift with crusts, leaving a moist erythematous surface. Impression cytology from the underside of a fresh crust reveals branching rows of small coccoid forms.","gross":"Tufted or paintbrush-like exudative crusts in chronically wet hair-bearing skin.","histo":"Neutrophilic exudative epidermitis with segmented branching gram-positive organisms forming transverse and longitudinal rows (railroad-track appearance).","best":"Dermatophilosis (Dermatophilus congolensis)","morph":"Skin: multifocal exudative neutrophilic bacterial dermatitis with crusting","mechanism":"Moisture and epidermal microtrauma permit zoospores to infect superficial skin; cycles of bacterial multiplication and epidermal inflammation produce laminated crusts.","tests":"Fresh-crust impression cytology/Gram stain; culture or PCR where indicated. Avoid using unrelated fungal branching hyphae as substitutes for dermatophilic filament morphology.","ref":"Lecture 72 slides 36–37 — dermatophilosis","options":[{"name":"Dermatophilosis (Dermatophilus congolensis)","supports":"Signalment, lesion distribution and microscopic pattern together provide the strongest match; agent or syndrome confirmation still requires the listed tests.","against":"Pattern recognition is suggestive rather than a stand-alone etiologic confirmation.","hist":"Neutrophilic exudative epidermitis with segmented branching gram-positive organisms forming transverse and longitudinal rows (railroad-track appearance).","role":"best"},{"name":"Dermatophytosis","supports":"Alopecia, scale and broken hairs may resemble some cases.","against":"Fungal arthroconidia on hair shafts rather than segmented branching bacteria in exudative crust.","hist":"Fungal hyphae in keratin and hair follicles.","role":"less"},{"name":"Staphylococcal superficial pyoderma","supports":"Crusting and follicular pustules can occur.","against":"Cocci lack characteristic longitudinal/transverse branching filaments.","hist":"Follicular pustules with cocci and neutrophils.","role":"less"},{"name":"Cutaneous habronemiasis","supports":"Horse with summer skin erosions and crusting.","against":"Habronema lesions are typically exuberant pruritic granulomatous wounds at fly-exposed sites, not rain-associated paintbrush crusts.","hist":"Eosinophilic granulomatous dermatitis and parasite fragments.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-habronema","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Horse summer sore that will not heal","species":"Adult horse","history":"In summer a horse develops a persistently pruritic nonhealing ulcerated exuberant granulation lesion near the medial canthus and distal limb. Several hard yellow granules are found in the lesion. Biopsy reveals abundant eosinophils.","gross":"Ulcerated, proliferative, pruritic nodules or plaques with yellow gritty necrotic granules at fly-accessible sites.","histo":"Marked eosinophilic granulomatous dermatitis, collagen flame figures and occasional degenerating spirurid larvae within eosinophilic foci.","best":"Cutaneous habronemiasis (summer sores)","morph":"Skin: multifocal eosinophilic granulomatous dermatitis with ulceration","mechanism":"Flies carrying third-stage Habronema/Draschia larvae deposit them on wounds; larvae cannot complete their gastric life cycle in skin and provoke a strong hypersensitivity response.","tests":"Biopsy with serial levels and parasite search, epidemiologic context and exclusion of sarcoid or pythiosis; larvae are often absent from sections.","ref":"Lecture 73 slide 24 — summer sores","options":[{"name":"Cutaneous habronemiasis (summer sores)","supports":"Signalment, lesion distribution and microscopic pattern together provide the strongest match; agent or syndrome confirmation still requires the listed tests.","against":"Pattern recognition is suggestive rather than a stand-alone etiologic confirmation.","hist":"Marked eosinophilic granulomatous dermatitis, collagen flame figures and occasional degenerating spirurid larvae within eosinophilic foci.","role":"best"},{"name":"Pythiosis (Pythium insidiosum)","supports":"Equine proliferative ulcerating lesions can contain eosinophilic kunkers.","against":"Broad sparsely septate oomycete hyphae in kunkers and strongly progressive invasive granulomas favor pythiosis.","hist":"Eosinophilic granulomas with broad hyphae demonstrable by GMS/PAS or immunostaining.","role":"less"},{"name":"Equine sarcoid","supports":"Persistent nodular ulcerated lesion is possible.","against":"Eosinophilic granules and larvae-associated inflammatory pattern favor habronemiasis.","hist":"Fibroblastic spindle-cell dermal tumor with epidermal hyperplasia.","role":"less"},{"name":"Proud flesh / exuberant granulation tissue","supports":"Chronic equine wounds may form exuberant pink tissue.","against":"Pronounced eosinophilia and yellow gritty foci suggest a specific parasitic/oomycete process.","hist":"Capillary-rich fibrovascular granulation tissue rather than eosinophilic granulomas.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-pythium","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Wetland horse with destructive ulcer and kunkers","species":"Horse with access to warm standing water","history":"A horse develops a rapidly progressive ulcerative granulomatous distal limb lesion after frequent contact with stagnant water. Irregular hard yellow kunkers protrude from sinuses. Biopsy contains extensive eosinophilic necrosis with broad sparsely septate hyphae.","gross":"Severe proliferative ulcerated cutaneous mass, sinus tracts and gritty necrotic kunkers.","histo":"Pyogranulomatous and eosinophilic inflammation with broad sparsely septate oomycete hyphae surrounded by eosinophilic necrotic debris; immunohistochemistry/PCR aid identification.","best":"Cutaneous pythiosis (Pythium insidiosum)","morph":"Skin/subcutis: severe eosinophilic necrotizing pyogranulomatous dermatitis with oomycete hyphae","mechanism":"Aquatic Pythium zoospores encyst in damaged skin and invade tissue, triggering eosinophilic granulomatous inflammation and progressive tissue destruction.","tests":"Biopsy lesion margin/kunkers for histology, immunohistochemistry, PCR or culture by qualified lab. Pythium is an oomycete, not a true fungus.","ref":"Lecture 73 slide 31 — Pythium insidiosum","options":[{"name":"Cutaneous pythiosis (Pythium insidiosum)","supports":"Signalment, lesion distribution and microscopic pattern together provide the strongest match; agent or syndrome confirmation still requires the listed tests.","against":"Pattern recognition is suggestive rather than a stand-alone etiologic confirmation.","hist":"Pyogranulomatous and eosinophilic inflammation with broad sparsely septate oomycete hyphae surrounded by eosinophilic necrotic debris; immunohistochemistry/PCR aid identification.","role":"best"},{"name":"Cutaneous habronemiasis","supports":"Pruritic summer sores and yellow granules can closely resemble pythiosis.","against":"Parasite-associated lesion often less deeply invasive; finding spirurid larvae rather than hyphae confirms.","hist":"Eosinophilic granulomatous dermatitis with rare larval profiles.","role":"less"},{"name":"Basidiobolomycosis","supports":"Oomycete/fungal-like destructive eosinophilic granulomas may overlap in some regions.","against":"Different organisms and geographic context require identification.","hist":"Eosinophilic granulomas with fungal elements of different morphology.","role":"less"},{"name":"Equine sarcoid","supports":"Proliferative ulcerated dermal mass may mimic.","against":"Hyphae within destructive eosinophilic lesions exclude a purely fibrous neoplasm.","hist":"Dermal spindle-cell fibroblastic neoplasia without hyphae.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s74-pemphigus-vulgaris","lecture":"74","system":"Skin","title":"Dog with painful oral ulcers and skin blisters","species":"Adult dog","history":"A dog has painful erosions of the oral mucosa, mucocutaneous junctions and groin. Intact blisters are rare. Biopsy from an early lesion edge shows epidermal separation just above the basal keratinocyte layer.","gross":"Fragile bullae or erosions especially at mucosa/mucocutaneous surfaces, potentially generalized.","histo":"Suprabasal acantholysis with a row of basal keratinocytes remaining on basement membrane (tombstones); variable secondary inflammation.","best":"Pemphigus vulgaris","morph":"Skin and oral mucosa: suprabasal acantholytic vesiculobullous dermatitis","mechanism":"Autoantibodies to desmosomal proteins disrupt keratinocyte cohesion at the suprabasal epidermal level, producing deep blistering and erosion.","tests":"Biopsy intact edge of early vesicle (not an old ulcer) for histology and correlate with mucosal distribution; rule out infectious acantholytic mimics.","ref":"Lecture 74 slide 12 — pemphigus vulgaris","options":[{"name":"Pemphigus vulgaris","supports":"Signalment, lesion distribution and microscopic pattern together provide the strongest match; agent or syndrome confirmation still requires the listed tests.","against":"Pattern recognition is suggestive rather than a stand-alone etiologic confirmation.","hist":"Suprabasal acantholysis with a row of basal keratinocytes remaining on basement membrane (tombstones); variable secondary inflammation.","role":"best"},{"name":"Pemphigus foliaceus","supports":"Can cause facial crusts, pustules, acantholysis.","against":"Subcorneal rather than suprabasal splitting; oral mucosal ulceration much less characteristic.","hist":"Subcorneal neutrophilic pustules containing acantholytic keratinocytes.","role":"less"},{"name":"Bullous pemphigoid","supports":"Oral and skin blisters may be painful.","against":"Subepidermal (basement membrane zone) split rather than suprabasal tombstoning.","hist":"Subepidermal vesicles with eosinophils/neutrophils.","role":"less"},{"name":"Toxic epidermal necrolysis","supports":"Widespread mucosal erosion and skin slough may mimic severe pemphigus.","against":"TEN is extensive full-thickness epidermal necrosis, not primarily suprabasal acantholysis.","hist":"Confluent keratinocyte apoptosis and full-thickness epidermal necrosis.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s74-eosinophilic-plaque","lecture":"74","system":"Skin","title":"Cat with pruritic weeping abdominal plaques","species":"Adult cat","history":"A cat develops intensely pruritic raised red moist sharply demarcated plaques on the ventral abdomen and inner thighs. Cytology has abundant eosinophils. Flea exposure and allergy history are possible.","gross":"Raised erythematous alopecic eroded moist plaques in ventral abdominal/medial thigh region.","histo":"Eosinophilic superficial-to-deep dermatitis with epidermal hyperplasia, spongiosis, eosinophilic exocytosis and possible eosinophilic collagen degeneration.","best":"Feline eosinophilic plaque (eosinophilic granuloma complex reaction pattern)","morph":"Skin: eosinophilic hyperplastic and erosive dermatitis (eosinophilic plaque)","mechanism":"Hypersensitivity-driven eosinophil recruitment causes epidermal and dermal inflammation; fleas, food allergy or other allergens may contribute. The plaque describes a reaction pattern, not a definitive underlying allergen.","tests":"Skin cytology and biopsy if atypical, rigorous flea control, investigation of food/environmental hypersensitivity and infection; avoid claiming cytology alone identifies etiology.","ref":"Lecture 74 slides 33–36 — feline eosinophilic granuloma complex","options":[{"name":"Feline eosinophilic plaque (eosinophilic granuloma complex reaction pattern)","supports":"Signalment, lesion distribution and microscopic pattern together provide the strongest match; agent or syndrome confirmation still requires the listed tests.","against":"Pattern recognition is suggestive rather than a stand-alone etiologic confirmation.","hist":"Eosinophilic superficial-to-deep dermatitis with epidermal hyperplasia, spongiosis, eosinophilic exocytosis and possible eosinophilic collagen degeneration.","role":"best"},{"name":"Indolent ulcer","supports":"Eosinophilic lesion of feline mucocutaneous tissue is related.","against":"Typically upper-lip ulcer, often minimally pruritic, rather than ventral moist plaque.","hist":"Ulcerated eosinophilic dermatitis of upper lip with variable collagen change.","role":"less"},{"name":"Eosinophilic granuloma","supports":"Can be linear thigh lesion or raised lip/chin plaque.","against":"Linear raised firm granuloma rather than large erythematous oozing plaque.","hist":"Dermal eosinophilic granulomatous reaction with collagen flame figures.","role":"less"},{"name":"Superficial bacterial pyoderma","supports":"Red moist erosive plaques and pruritus overlap.","against":"Heavy eosinophils with typical feline plaque distribution favor hypersensitivity pattern; bacteria should be checked.","hist":"Neutrophilic folliculitis/pustules with bacteria rather than eosinophilic-dominant dermatitis.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s71-cushing","lecture":"71","system":"Skin","title":"Endocrine skin: alopecia with comedones","species":"9-year-old dog","history":"An older dog develops progressive symmetrical truncal alopecia, thin skin, comedones and abdominal enlargement with polyuria and polydipsia. There is no primary severe itch.","gross":"Bilaterally symmetrical truncal alopecia, thin skin, comedones, possible calcinosis cutis.","histo":"Follicular and epidermal atrophy with telogenization and sometimes dermal collagen mineralization.","best":"Canine hyperadrenocorticism","morph":"Skin: chronic endocrine atrophy and follicular arrest","mechanism":"Excess glucocorticoid suppresses fibroblasts, collagen synthesis and hair follicle cycling, with muscle wasting and redistribution of fat.","tests":"Endocrine testing guided by clinical likelihood, urinalysis/culture, chemistry and evaluation for infection or hypothyroidism.","ref":"Lecture 71 slide 31","options":[{"name":"Canine hyperadrenocorticism","supports":"Lesion distribution, signalment, clinical context and the stated microscopic pattern together make this the strongest diagnosis.","against":"The gross pattern alone does not confirm the etiology; correlate appropriate testing and sampling.","hist":"Follicular and epidermal atrophy with telogenization and sometimes dermal collagen mineralization.","role":"best"},{"name":"Hypothyroidism","supports":"Symmetrical alopecia and seborrhea can overlap.","against":"PU/PD, potbelly, very thin skin and calcinosis are more consistent with glucocorticoid excess.","hist":"Telogenization, follicular atrophy; generally without calcinosis cutis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Seasonal flank alopecia","supports":"Symmetrical well-demarcated truncal alopecia occurs.","against":"Usually seasonal flank localization and no systemic PU/PD or skin thinning.","hist":"Follicular dysplasia and melanin clumping.","role":"less"},{"name":"Demodicosis","supports":"Alopecia and comedones can appear with adult-onset disease.","against":"Mites and folliculitis on deep skin scrapings/histology identify infestation.","hist":"Follicular mites, perifolliculitis, rupture and pyogranulomas.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s72-sheep-pox","lecture":"72","system":"Skin","title":"Sheep with generalized papules and systemic illness","species":"Young lamb","history":"Following fever, lymph node enlargement and respiratory signs, multiple lambs develop firm raised papules and crusted nodules over hairless skin; necropsy also shows nodular pulmonary lesions.","gross":"Generalized firm papules and nodules with crusting in sparsely wooled sites; occasional lung and internal nodules.","histo":"Epidermal hyperplasia/ballooning and dermal inflammatory infiltrates, with characteristic poxviral cytoplasmic inclusions in suitable lesions.","best":"Sheeppox (capripoxvirus)","morph":"Skin: multifocal proliferative viral dermatitis with systemic lesions","mechanism":"Capripoxvirus causes systemic infection and epithelial/dermal injury; unlike localized orf, susceptible sheep may show severe generalized and visceral disease.","tests":"Official laboratory PCR for capripoxvirus and applicable reportable-disease response; avoid assuming lesion type identifies virus without testing.","ref":"Lecture 72 slides 6–7","options":[{"name":"Sheeppox (capripoxvirus)","supports":"Lesion distribution, signalment, clinical context and the stated microscopic pattern together make this the strongest diagnosis.","against":"The gross pattern alone does not confirm the etiology; correlate appropriate testing and sampling.","hist":"Epidermal hyperplasia/ballooning and dermal inflammatory infiltrates, with characteristic poxviral cytoplasmic inclusions in suitable lesions.","role":"best"},{"name":"Contagious ecthyma (orf)","supports":"Young sheep develop crusted poxviral lesions.","against":"Orf typically targets lips/muzzle/teats and is less likely to cause systemic pulmonary nodules.","hist":"Proliferative epidermitis with ballooning degeneration and pox inclusions.","role":"less"},{"name":"Dermatophilosis","supports":"Crusts may occur over skin.","against":"Typically exudative paintbrush crusts with branching segmented bacterial organisms, not systemic pox nodules.","hist":"Neutrophilic crusts containing railroad-track bacterial filaments.","role":"less"},{"name":"Lumpy skin disease","supports":"Capripoxvirus skin nodules may look similar.","against":"Predominantly cattle rather than sheep; must verify host and organism.","hist":"Deep dermal vasculitis and necrotizing nodules with possible pox inclusions.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s72-fowlpox","lecture":"72","system":"Skin","title":"Chicken with comb crusts and oral plaques","species":"Young flock chicken","history":"Several chickens have raised crusted lesions on combs and wattles; some others show adherent diphtheritic plaques in the oral cavity and difficult breathing.","gross":"Dry form: proliferative crusting featherless skin; wet form: necrotic diphtheritic oral/upper respiratory plaques.","histo":"Hyperplastic epithelium with ballooning keratinocytes and large eosinophilic intracytoplasmic Bollinger bodies.","best":"Fowlpox (avipoxvirus), mixed dry and wet forms","morph":"Skin/oral mucosa: proliferative poxviral dermatitis and diphtheritic stomatitis","mechanism":"Avipoxvirus replication in epithelium creates ballooning and inclusions; mucosal plaques can physically obstruct airflow.","tests":"Histopathology showing typical inclusion bodies, virus PCR when confirmation needed; assess flock mosquito exposure and vaccination history.","ref":"Lecture 72 slide 10","options":[{"name":"Fowlpox (avipoxvirus), mixed dry and wet forms","supports":"Lesion distribution, signalment, clinical context and the stated microscopic pattern together make this the strongest diagnosis.","against":"The gross pattern alone does not confirm the etiology; correlate appropriate testing and sampling.","hist":"Hyperplastic epithelium with ballooning keratinocytes and large eosinophilic intracytoplasmic Bollinger bodies.","role":"best"},{"name":"Infectious laryngotracheitis","supports":"Birds may develop dyspnea and upper airway exudate.","against":"ILT causes respiratory mucosal lesions with intranuclear herpesviral inclusions rather than characteristic cutaneous fowlpox.","hist":"Necrotizing tracheitis with intranuclear inclusions early.","role":"less"},{"name":"Vitamin A deficiency","supports":"Oral white plaques and keratinization may occur in birds.","against":"No typical poxvirus intracytoplasmic Bollinger bodies or comb scabs.","hist":"Squamous metaplasia of respiratory/oral epithelium.","role":"less"},{"name":"Bacterial dermatitis","supports":"Crusts and necrotic skin plaques could appear.","against":"Bacteria do not produce large viral Bollinger bodies.","hist":"Suppurative/necrotizing dermatitis with bacterial colonies.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s72-erysipelas","lecture":"72","system":"Skin","title":"Pig with geometric purple skin patches","species":"6-month-old grower pig","history":"A febrile pig is reluctant to rise and has multiple raised sharply demarcated rhomboid pink-purple skin plaques. Another herd mate has swollen joints.","gross":"Multifocal rhomboid or diamond-shaped erythematous to purple skin lesions, occasionally progressing to necrosis.","histo":"Small-vessel vasculitis, thrombosis and dermal ischemic necrosis; possible septic thrombi.","best":"Porcine erysipelas (Erysipelothrix rhusiopathiae)","morph":"Skin: multifocal dermal vascular infarction, consistent with septicemia","mechanism":"Erysipelothrix bacteremia and vascular injury impair dermal perfusion producing the diamond pattern; chronic forms may feature arthritis or vegetative endocarditis.","tests":"Culture/PCR from appropriate tissues/blood in consultation with laboratory, assess joints/heart and zoonotic exposure precautions.","ref":"Lecture 72 slide 39","options":[{"name":"Porcine erysipelas (Erysipelothrix rhusiopathiae)","supports":"Lesion distribution, signalment, clinical context and the stated microscopic pattern together make this the strongest diagnosis.","against":"The gross pattern alone does not confirm the etiology; correlate appropriate testing and sampling.","hist":"Small-vessel vasculitis, thrombosis and dermal ischemic necrosis; possible septic thrombi.","role":"best"},{"name":"Classical swine fever or African swine fever","supports":"Both can cause fever and hemorrhagic/purple skin.","against":"Diamond-shaped urticarial infarcts are suggestive of erysipelas but hemorrhagic systemic diseases need regulatory testing when plausible.","hist":"Severe systemic hemorrhage, lymphoid necrosis or vascular injury depending agent.","role":"less"},{"name":"Porcine dermatitis and nephropathy syndrome","supports":"Purple dermal lesions and systemic illness possible.","against":"Typically necrotizing vasculitis with renal lesions in older pigs; investigate renal changes and PCV2 context.","hist":"Systemic immune-complex vasculitis and glomerulonephritis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Greasy pig disease","supports":"Can affect young pigs with exudative skin lesions.","against":"Diffuse greasy brown crusts rather than sharply demarcated purple vascular infarcts.","hist":"Superficial exfoliative epidermitis with staphylococci.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s72-sarcoid","lecture":"72","system":"Skin","title":"Horse with recurring fibroblastic skin nodule","species":"6-year-old horse","history":"A persistent nodular mass over the girth area enlarges after previous trauma and regrowth following removal. Grossly the lesion is firm to fleshy, ulcerated in part, but there is no evidence of distant metastasis.","gross":"Nodular/fibroblastic proliferative cutaneous mass with variable verrucous surface or ulceration.","histo":"Dermal interlacing spindle-shaped fibroblastic cells with whorled bundles and epidermal hyperplasia; invasive local pattern without typical hematogenous metastasis.","best":"Equine sarcoid (BPV-associated dermal fibroblastic tumor)","morph":"Skin: locally invasive nonmetastasizing fibroblastic neoplasm","mechanism":"Bovine papillomavirus-associated transformation of dermal fibroblasts leads to locally invasive tumor growth and recurrence after incomplete removal.","tests":"Histopathology and clinical context; handle biopsy/treatment planning carefully because trauma may exacerbate some lesions.","ref":"Lecture 72 slide 16","options":[{"name":"Equine sarcoid (BPV-associated dermal fibroblastic tumor)","supports":"Lesion distribution, signalment, clinical context and the stated microscopic pattern together make this the strongest diagnosis.","against":"The gross pattern alone does not confirm the etiology; correlate appropriate testing and sampling.","hist":"Dermal interlacing spindle-shaped fibroblastic cells with whorled bundles and epidermal hyperplasia; invasive local pattern without typical hematogenous metastasis.","role":"best"},{"name":"Exuberant granulation tissue","supports":"Pink proliferative wound tissue can resemble fibroblastic sarcoid.","against":"Usually associated with injury and histologically abundant perpendicular capillaries and organized healing tissue.","hist":"Vascular granulation tissue with fibroblasts, no characteristic sarcoid architecture.","role":"less"},{"name":"Squamous cell carcinoma","supports":"Ulcerated locally invasive skin mass can occur.","against":"Neoplastic squamous epithelial islands and keratin pearls, not primarily dermal fibroblasts.","hist":"Invasive atypical squamous cell epithelium.","role":"less"},{"name":"Fibrosarcoma","supports":"Dermal spindle cell malignant tumor can resemble sarcoid.","against":"May show more marked cellular atypia and metastatic potential; histology and clinical setting distinguish.","hist":"Infiltrative malignant spindle-cell tumor with variable atypia.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-sarcoptes","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Itchy dog with crusted ear margins","species":"Young dog from crowded kennel","history":"Several dogs are severely pruritic with papules and crusts on pinnal margins, elbows and hocks; people handling them develop transient itchy papules. A superficial scrape may be negative.","gross":"Severe itchy papular and crusting dermatitis on ear edges, ventral body and pressure areas, with excoriations.","histo":"Superficial perivascular hypersensitivity dermatitis, epidermal hyperplasia and hyperkeratosis; burrowing mites and eggs may be seen in stratum corneum but are often missed.","best":"Sarcoptic mange (Sarcoptes scabiei)","morph":"Skin: crusting hypersensitivity dermatitis with surface/epidermal mites","mechanism":"Burrowing mites stimulate a strong hypersensitivity response; mite burden may be low despite intense itch and zoonotic transient papules.","tests":"Multiple superficial scrapings, compatible signs, contact history and response to appropriate veterinary treatment; consider flea allergy and atopy.","ref":"Lecture 73 slide 8","options":[{"name":"Sarcoptic mange (Sarcoptes scabiei)","supports":"Lesion distribution, signalment, clinical context and the stated microscopic pattern together make this the strongest diagnosis.","against":"The gross pattern alone does not confirm the etiology; correlate appropriate testing and sampling.","hist":"Superficial perivascular hypersensitivity dermatitis, epidermal hyperplasia and hyperkeratosis; burrowing mites and eggs may be seen in stratum corneum but are often missed.","role":"best"},{"name":"Canine generalized demodicosis","supports":"Mites and follicular dermatitis can cause alopecia and scaling.","against":"Demodex resides in hair follicles and often has folliculitis; scabies is highly contagious and intensely pruritic.","hist":"Follicular mites, folliculitis/furunculosis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Flea allergy dermatitis","supports":"Severe itch and papules common.","against":"More strongly lumbosacral/tail base and flea evidence; ear margins characteristic of scabies.","hist":"Perivascular eosinophilic dermatitis without burrowing mites.","role":"less"},{"name":"Canine atopic dermatitis","supports":"Chronic intensely pruritic dermatitis common.","against":"Onset and distribution plus contagious kennel cluster favor scabies; mites must be ruled out.","hist":"Spongiotic/perivascular hypersensitivity dermatitis, no mites.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-ringworm","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Shelter kitten with circular scaling alopecia","species":"4-month-old kitten","history":"A kitten develops expanding circular alopecic scaly plaques on the face and forelimbs. A foster caregiver has a round itchy patch; a sampled broken hair contains fungal arthroconidia.","gross":"Circular alopecia, scale, broken hairs and variable follicular papules, sometimes little itch.","histo":"Dermatophytic infection of hair shaft and follicular keratin with hyphae and arthroconidia; perifolliculitis may be present.","best":"Dermatophytosis (Microsporum canis)","morph":"Skin/hair: fungal folliculitis with ectothrix arthroconidial hair infection","mechanism":"Dermatophyte infects keratinized hair and stratum corneum; contaminated hairs/spores transmit infection to humans and animals.","tests":"Fungal culture, PCR interpreted with examination, trichography and Wood's lamp screening where appropriate; examine contacts and environment.","ref":"Lecture 73 slide 27","options":[{"name":"Dermatophytosis (Microsporum canis)","supports":"Lesion distribution, signalment, clinical context and the stated microscopic pattern together make this the strongest diagnosis.","against":"The gross pattern alone does not confirm the etiology; correlate appropriate testing and sampling.","hist":"Dermatophytic infection of hair shaft and follicular keratin with hyphae and arthroconidia; perifolliculitis may be present.","role":"best"},{"name":"Demodicosis","supports":"Juvenile patchy alopecia and follicular scale may overlap.","against":"Deep scrapings/trichograms reveal mites rather than ectothrix fungal arthroconidia.","hist":"Intra-follicular mites, folliculitis/furunculosis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Superficial bacterial folliculitis","supports":"Alopecic patches and epidermal collarettes possible.","against":"Cocci/neutrophils and follicular pustules without fungal hairs support pyoderma.","hist":"Neutrophilic folliculitis with bacterial cocci.","role":"less"},{"name":"Malassezia dermatitis","supports":"Greasy scaling and hair loss can occur.","against":"Yeast on surface cytology and often malodor/erythema differ from dermatophyte-infected hairs.","hist":"Surface budding yeast with variable superficial inflammation.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-malassezia","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Malodorous dog with greasy skin folds","species":"Adult allergic dog","history":"A dog with chronic pruritus develops greasy erythematous darkened interdigital and axillary skin with a musty odor. Tape cytology shows multiple peanut-shaped budding yeasts.","gross":"Erythematous greasy malodorous folds, lichenification/hyperpigmentation and variable alopecia.","histo":"Superficial perivascular dermatitis, acanthosis and hyperkeratosis; budding yeast in surface keratin; organism number and clinical context matter.","best":"Malassezia pachydermatis dermatitis","morph":"Skin: chronic superficial yeast-associated dermatitis with lichenification","mechanism":"Commensal yeast overproliferation or hypersensitivity occurs when skin barrier and local environment change, often secondary to atopy or endocrinopathy.","tests":"Tape strip or impression cytology plus underlying allergy/endocrine investigation; culture rarely necessary for straightforward cases.","ref":"Lecture 73 slide 28","options":[{"name":"Malassezia pachydermatis dermatitis","supports":"Lesion distribution, signalment, clinical context and the stated microscopic pattern together make this the strongest diagnosis.","against":"The gross pattern alone does not confirm the etiology; correlate appropriate testing and sampling.","hist":"Superficial perivascular dermatitis, acanthosis and hyperkeratosis; budding yeast in surface keratin; organism number and clinical context matter.","role":"best"},{"name":"Staphylococcal superficial pyoderma","supports":"Red pruritic skin with scale, collarettes or odor can overlap.","against":"Neutrophils with cocci and pustular folliculitis point to bacterial infection; coinfection is common.","hist":"Suppurative folliculitis containing cocci.","role":"less"},{"name":"Canine atopic dermatitis without yeast overgrowth","supports":"Interdigital and axillary pruritus common.","against":"Cytology demonstrating increased yeast in a compatible lesion supports yeast involvement, although allergy may be the root cause.","hist":"Spongiotic/perivascular dermatitis; no characteristic yeast abundance.","role":"less"},{"name":"Dermatophytosis","supports":"Scaling alopecia and fungal infection overlap.","against":"Dermatophyte invades hair shafts; yeast resides on skin surface.","hist":"Hyphae or arthroconidia within/in association with hair.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s74-dle","lecture":"74","system":"Skin","title":"Depigmenting nasal planum in a collie","species":"7-year-old collie","history":"A dog develops progressive loss of normal cobblestone nasal planum texture, depigmentation, erythema and superficial erosions exacerbated by sun exposure, without systemic illness.","gross":"Symmetric nasal depigmentation and smooth loss of cobblestone surface with crusts and scarring.","histo":"Lymphocytic interface dermatitis, basal keratinocyte apoptosis/vacuolation and pigmentary incontinence, sometimes thickened basement membrane.","best":"Canine discoid lupus erythematosus (cutaneous lupus)","morph":"Nasal planum: chronic lymphocytic interface dermatitis","mechanism":"Localized immune attack on basal epidermal keratinocytes promotes interface damage and pigment loss; UV light can exacerbate lesions.","tests":"Biopsy active depigmented/erythematous lesion edge for histology; exclude nasal pyoderma, dermatophytosis and systemic lupus signs.","ref":"Lecture 74 slide 19","options":[{"name":"Canine discoid lupus erythematosus (cutaneous lupus)","supports":"Lesion distribution, signalment, clinical context and the stated microscopic pattern together make this the strongest diagnosis.","against":"The gross pattern alone does not confirm the etiology; correlate appropriate testing and sampling.","hist":"Lymphocytic interface dermatitis, basal keratinocyte apoptosis/vacuolation and pigmentary incontinence, sometimes thickened basement membrane.","role":"best"},{"name":"Mucocutaneous pyoderma","supports":"Crusts and erosions at mucocutaneous junction can resemble DLE.","against":"Bacteria/neutrophils and response to therapy may distinguish; histology can overlap.","hist":"Neutrophilic interface and superficial bacterial inflammation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Vitiligo","supports":"Symmetrical nasal depigmentation possible.","against":"Usually depigmentation without erosions or interface ulceration.","hist":"Melanocyte loss with minimal interface inflammation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Pemphigus foliaceus","supports":"Facial crusting and depigmentation occur.","against":"Subcorneal pustules/acantholysis differ from lymphocytic interface dermatitis.","hist":"Subcorneal acantholytic pustules.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-demodex-cati","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Cat with follicular facial mites","species":"Adult immunosuppressed cat","history":"An adult cat with a history of systemic illness has patchy facial and periocular alopecia, follicular scaling and occasional comedones. Deep scrapings reveal elongated mites deep inside follicles.","gross":"Patchy scaling alopecia with follicular plugs on head and neck.","histo":"Numerous slender mites within hair follicles/sebaceous structures, with perifolliculitis and sometimes follicular rupture.","best":"Feline Demodex cati","morph":"Skin: follicular parasitic dermatitis with Demodex mites","mechanism":"D. cati is follicle-dwelling; overgrowth is associated with altered host defenses and differs from contagious surface-dwelling D. gatoi.","tests":"Deep skin scrapings/trichograms plus evaluation for predisposing illnesses; species identification by mite morphology and location.","ref":"Lecture 73 slide 6","options":[{"name":"Feline Demodex cati","supports":"The specific host, distribution, exposure and microscopic lesion pattern provide the best combined evidence.","against":"The morphology still requires appropriate confirmation and cannot establish all etiologic details independently.","hist":"Numerous slender mites within hair follicles/sebaceous structures, with perifolliculitis and sometimes follicular rupture.","role":"best"},{"name":"Demodex gatoi","supports":"Feline demodicosis and overgrooming can resemble it.","against":"Superficial and contagious with short-bodied mites; deep follicular mites fit D. cati.","hist":"Short surface mites within stratum corneum, not elongated follicular mites.","role":"less"},{"name":"Dermatophytosis","supports":"Facial alopecia, scale, contagious risk.","against":"Fungal infection of hair shafts rather than follicular mite bodies.","hist":"Hyphae and arthroconidia around/in affected hairs.","role":"less"},{"name":"Feline acne","supports":"Comedones and follicular plugging can occur.","against":"Usually chin-centered keratinization, not numerous follicular mites.","hist":"Follicular keratin plugs and secondary inflammation, no mites.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-chorioptes","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Draft horse stamping with pastern crusts","species":"Draft horse with feathered fetlocks","history":"Several horses in a barn stamp and rub their hind fetlocks. There is scaling, alopecia and crusting over pasterns and distal limbs; superficial scrapings reveal nonburrowing mites.","gross":"Distal limb papules, scale, crusts, chronic thickening in feathered pasterns.","histo":"Superficial eosinophilic/perivascular dermatitis with epidermal hyperplasia and mites on keratin surface rather than deep within follicles.","best":"Chorioptic mange (Chorioptes spp.)","morph":"Distal-limb parasitic crusting dermatitis","mechanism":"Chorioptes feed on skin debris and are surface dwellers, causing irritant/hypersensitivity responses at characteristic distal limb sites.","tests":"Superficial skin scrapings or tape from crust margins; differentiate from pastern dermatitis, dermatophilosis and mites of other genera.","ref":"Lecture 73 slide 12","options":[{"name":"Chorioptic mange (Chorioptes spp.)","supports":"The specific host, distribution, exposure and microscopic lesion pattern provide the best combined evidence.","against":"The morphology still requires appropriate confirmation and cannot establish all etiologic details independently.","hist":"Superficial eosinophilic/perivascular dermatitis with epidermal hyperplasia and mites on keratin surface rather than deep within follicles.","role":"best"},{"name":"Psoroptic mange","supports":"Nonburrowing mites, pruritus and crusts in horses.","against":"Psoroptes more often involve mane/ear canal depending species; morphology confirms genus.","hist":"Mites on skin/ear debris, superficial dermatitis; histology rarely genus-specific.","role":"less"},{"name":"Equine pastern dermatitis","supports":"Crusting and swelling over pasterns match.","against":"A multifactorial syndrome; demonstrable Chorioptes supports mite component.","hist":"Variable neutrophilic/eosinophilic dermatitis, with or without mites.","role":"less"},{"name":"Dermatophilosis","supports":"Wet crusting on lower legs can occur.","against":"Segmented branching bacteria are seen in crust cytology rather than mites.","hist":"Neutrophilic crusts with Dermatophilus filaments.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-psoroptes","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Rabbit shaking head with thick ear crusts","species":"Adult rabbit","history":"A rabbit develops marked head shaking and scratching, with thick tan-brown laminated crust filling the external auditory canal and pinna. Debris contains large nonburrowing mites.","gross":"Severe crusting otitis and adherent ear exudate with marked discomfort.","histo":"Hyperkeratosis and exudative superficial dermatitis with mites in ear debris; histology alone is not needed for definitive mite identification.","best":"Psoroptes cuniculi-associated ear mange","morph":"Ear canal: severe parasitic crusting otitis externa","mechanism":"Surface-feeding psoroptic mites induce intense irritation and exudation; mites remain at skin surface and form heavy crusts.","tests":"Microscopic examination of a safely obtained ear crust/debris sample, mite morphological confirmation, assessment of contacts.","ref":"Lecture 73 slide 11","options":[{"name":"Psoroptes cuniculi-associated ear mange","supports":"The specific host, distribution, exposure and microscopic lesion pattern provide the best combined evidence.","against":"The morphology still requires appropriate confirmation and cannot establish all etiologic details independently.","hist":"Hyperkeratosis and exudative superficial dermatitis with mites in ear debris; histology alone is not needed for definitive mite identification.","role":"best"},{"name":"Otodectes cynotis","supports":"Ear mites can cause head shaking and otitis.","against":"Different mite genus/host pattern; large psoroptic mites and thick crusts are classic for rabbit ear canker.","hist":"Surface mites and hyperkeratotic crust; genus determined on direct examination.","role":"less"},{"name":"Bacterial otitis externa","supports":"Crusting, pain and exudate occur.","against":"Mites visible within crust rather than bacteria alone.","hist":"Neutrophilic exudate/bacteria without characteristic mites.","role":"less"},{"name":"Chorioptic mange","supports":"Surface mite causing pruritus and scaling.","against":"Usually distal limbs or perineal body sites rather than heavy rabbit ear canker.","hist":"Surface mites with superficial hyperkeratosis; mite morphology needed.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-cheyletiella","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Walking dandruff in a multi-pet household","species":"Young rabbit","history":"A rabbit and another household pet develop dorsal white flaky scale and variable pruritus. Brushing collects large superficial mites with prominent palpal claws; a caregiver develops transient itchy papules.","gross":"Prominent dry dorsal midline scale, variable erythema and alopecia.","histo":"Surface-located mites with mild superficial perivascular dermatitis and hyperkeratosis; often not seen on routine biopsy.","best":"Cheyletiellosis (walking dandruff)","morph":"Skin: superficial parasitic scaling dermatitis","mechanism":"Cheyletiella mites live on the surface; their feeding creates scale and hypersensitivity and can cause temporary human skin lesions.","tests":"Coat brushing, acetate tape or superficial scrapings and microscopic mite identification; treat in-contact animals under veterinarian guidance.","ref":"Lecture 73 slide 13","options":[{"name":"Cheyletiellosis (walking dandruff)","supports":"The specific host, distribution, exposure and microscopic lesion pattern provide the best combined evidence.","against":"The morphology still requires appropriate confirmation and cannot establish all etiologic details independently.","hist":"Surface-located mites with mild superficial perivascular dermatitis and hyperkeratosis; often not seen on routine biopsy.","role":"best"},{"name":"Sarcoptic mange","supports":"Contagious pruritic mite infestation and human transient lesions overlap.","against":"Sarcoptes burrows in superficial epidermis, usually intense itch and ear/elbow lesions in dogs.","hist":"Burrowing mites/eggs in stratum corneum and hypersensitivity dermatitis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Psoroptic mange","supports":"Surface mite with scale and itch.","against":"Often heavy ear crust in rabbits and different mite morphology.","hist":"Superficial mites within exudative ear crust.","role":"less"},{"name":"Dermatophytosis","supports":"Zoonotic scaling/alopecia can occur.","against":"Fungal infected hairs, not walking surface mites.","hist":"Arthroconidia/hyphae involving hair shafts.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-sporothrix","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Cat with ulcerated nodules tracking up limb","species":"Adult outdoor cat","history":"An outdoor cat has multiple draining ulcers and subcutaneous nodules on a forelimb with new nodules ascending proximally along lymphatic drainage. The handler reports a scratch and a new skin lesion.","gross":"Multiple ulcerating nodules and draining tracts, sometimes along lymphatics; cats may have extensive organism-rich wounds.","histo":"Pyogranulomatous dermatitis with round-to-oval/cigar-shaped yeast forms; organisms may be numerous in feline lesions.","best":"Feline sporotrichosis (Sporothrix spp.)","morph":"Skin/subcutis: nodular ulcerating pyogranulomatous fungal dermatitis","mechanism":"Sporothrix inoculated through trauma causes local and lymphocutaneous infection; infected cats can transmit viable organisms by scratches/bites and secretions.","tests":"Cytology/histopathology plus fungal culture or PCR with appropriate laboratory biosafety and zoonotic precautions; distinguish from atypical mycobacterial infection.","ref":"Lecture 73 slide 30","options":[{"name":"Feline sporotrichosis (Sporothrix spp.)","supports":"The specific host, distribution, exposure and microscopic lesion pattern provide the best combined evidence.","against":"The morphology still requires appropriate confirmation and cannot establish all etiologic details independently.","hist":"Pyogranulomatous dermatitis with round-to-oval/cigar-shaped yeast forms; organisms may be numerous in feline lesions.","role":"best"},{"name":"Atypical mycobacterial panniculitis","supports":"Nodules, draining tracts and pyogranulomatous dermatitis possible.","against":"Acid-fast bacilli rather than budding yeasts; special stains and PCR needed.","hist":"Pyogranulomatous panniculitis with acid-fast bacilli in some cases.","role":"less"},{"name":"Phaeohyphomycosis","supports":"Subcutaneous fungal nodules and ulceration may overlap.","against":"Dematiaceous pigmented hyphae and yeast-like bodies, not typical Sporothrix morphology.","hist":"Brown pigmented fungal cells/hyphae, highlighted by special stains.","role":"less"},{"name":"Cutaneous cryptococcosis","supports":"Cats can have ulcerative fungal nodules, often facial.","against":"Encapsulated narrow-based budding yeasts with mucicarmine-positive capsule.","hist":"Granulomatous inflammation with large encapsulated yeasts.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-zinc","lecture":"73","system":"Skin","title":"Husky with crusting around eyes and muzzle","species":"2-year-old Siberian husky","history":"A young husky eating a commercially complete diet develops symmetric adherent scale and crust around eyelids, lips, pinnae and elbows without major systemic abnormalities.","gross":"Symmetrical parakeratotic crusting at periorificial and pressure sites.","histo":"Marked parakeratotic hyperkeratosis with epidermal hyperplasia; inflammation varies and is not pathognomonic.","best":"Zinc-responsive dermatosis type I","morph":"Skin: parakeratotic hyperkeratotic dermatitis","mechanism":"In susceptible northern breeds decreased zinc absorption/utilization can impair epithelial cornification despite adequate dietary zinc; histology supports but does not uniquely prove etiology.","tests":"Dietary review and serum zinc interpretation with caution, skin biopsy, exclusion of other causes; veterinary-supervised zinc supplementation response supports diagnosis.","ref":"Lecture 73 slides 34–35","options":[{"name":"Zinc-responsive dermatosis type I","supports":"The specific host, distribution, exposure and microscopic lesion pattern provide the best combined evidence.","against":"The morphology still requires appropriate confirmation and cannot establish all etiologic details independently.","hist":"Marked parakeratotic hyperkeratosis with epidermal hyperplasia; inflammation varies and is not pathognomonic.","role":"best"},{"name":"Zinc-responsive dermatosis type II","supports":"Similar symmetric parakeratotic crusts.","against":"More often rapidly growing large-breed pups with deficient or antagonistic diets, not an adult northern breed on adequate diet.","hist":"Parakeratosis and epidermal hyperplasia similar; history decisive.","role":"less"},{"name":"Superficial necrolytic dermatitis","supports":"Periorificial and footpad crusts overlap.","against":"Usually older dogs with liver disease, painful pads and red-white-blue epidermal change.","hist":"Parakeratosis with pale upper spinous epidermis and basal proliferation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Pemphigus foliaceus","supports":"Crusts on muzzle and pinnae common.","against":"Acantholytic subcorneal pustules differ from primarily parakeratotic hyperkeratosis.","hist":"Neutrophilic subcorneal pustules and acantholytic cells.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s74-pemphigoid","lecture":"74","system":"Skin","title":"Dog with tense blisters and mucosal erosions","species":"Adult collie","history":"A dog has painful relatively tense blisters on skin and oral mucosa, with erosions after rupture. A fresh-edge biopsy shows the entire epidermis lifted cleanly from dermis.","gross":"Vesicles/bullae and erosions on skin and mucosa, sometimes extensive.","histo":"Subepidermal separation at the basement membrane zone with eosinophils and neutrophils and an intact overlying epidermis.","best":"Bullous pemphigoid","morph":"Skin: subepidermal vesiculobullous autoimmune dermatitis","mechanism":"Autoantibodies targeting hemidesmosomal/basement membrane zone components disrupt dermal-epidermal attachment, unlike desmosomal disruption in pemphigus.","tests":"Biopsy intact bullae or fresh edge for H&E; immunofluorescence/serologic testing where available; avoid ulcer center with lost junction.","ref":"Lecture 74 slides 14–15","options":[{"name":"Bullous pemphigoid","supports":"The specific host, distribution, exposure and microscopic lesion pattern provide the best combined evidence.","against":"The morphology still requires appropriate confirmation and cannot establish all etiologic details independently.","hist":"Subepidermal separation at the basement membrane zone with eosinophils and neutrophils and an intact overlying epidermis.","role":"best"},{"name":"Pemphigus vulgaris","supports":"Oral erosions and fragile bullae overlap.","against":"Split is suprabasal intraepidermal with tombstone basal cells.","hist":"Suprabasal acantholysis retaining basal keratinocytes.","role":"less"},{"name":"Pemphigus foliaceus","supports":"Facial crusting, fragile pustules possible.","against":"Split is very superficial and intraepidermal rather than junctional.","hist":"Subcorneal acantholytic pustules.","role":"less"},{"name":"Epidermolysis bullosa","supports":"Junctional separation and bullae can resemble it.","against":"Usually inherited and begins young, rather than acquired adult immune disease.","hist":"Blister level depends on inherited structural protein defect.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s74-flea","lecture":"74","system":"Skin","title":"Dog chewing tail base with papular crusts","species":"Adult dog without reliable flea prevention","history":"The dog bites the rump and tail base, developing papules, crusts and traumatic alopecia over the lumbosacral region. Only one flea is found despite severe itch.","gross":"Lumbosacral and tail-base excoriations, erythematous papules, crusts and secondary alopecia.","histo":"Superficial perivascular eosinophilic hypersensitivity dermatitis; lesions are not specific for flea saliva.","best":"Flea allergy dermatitis","morph":"Skin: pruritic eosinophilic hypersensitivity dermatitis, flea-associated pattern","mechanism":"Hypersensitivity to flea saliva can cause profound inflammation from minimal flea exposure, so a low observed flea count does not rule out flea allergy.","tests":"Flea combing, environmental and all-pet flea control trial, exclusion of secondary pyoderma and other allergic disease.","ref":"Lecture 74 slide 32","options":[{"name":"Flea allergy dermatitis","supports":"The specific host, distribution, exposure and microscopic lesion pattern provide the best combined evidence.","against":"The morphology still requires appropriate confirmation and cannot establish all etiologic details independently.","hist":"Superficial perivascular eosinophilic hypersensitivity dermatitis; lesions are not specific for flea saliva.","role":"best"},{"name":"Canine atopic dermatitis","supports":"Pruritus and recurrent erythematous skin can overlap.","against":"Atopy commonly involves paws, ears, flexures, face; tail-base concentration and fleas support FAD.","hist":"Nonspecific perivascular allergic dermatitis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Sarcoptic mange","supports":"Severe itch and crusts, may have few mites.","against":"Ear margins, elbows, hocks, contagion and human exposure favor Sarcoptes.","hist":"Superficial burrowing mites with hypersensitivity if detected.","role":"less"},{"name":"Malassezia dermatitis","supports":"Can produce intense itch, greasy odor and erythema.","against":"Yeast surface cytology plus fold distribution and smell favor yeast over primary flea reaction.","hist":"Surface budding yeast with chronic superficial dermatitis.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s74-culicoides","lecture":"74","system":"Skin","title":"Horse rubbing mane and tail every summer","species":"Adult horse living near wetlands","history":"Each warm season the horse rubs its mane and tail until hair breaks and the skin becomes lichenified and crusted. Lesions lessen in cold months and cluster near midges.","gross":"Excoriation, alopecia, crusting and lichenification along mane and tail head with summer recurrence.","histo":"Superficial and deep perivascular eosinophilic dermatitis, epidermal hyperplasia; histology is a hypersensitivity pattern, not proof of insect type.","best":"Equine Culicoides hypersensitivity (sweet itch)","morph":"Skin: chronic pruritic eosinophilic hypersensitivity dermatitis","mechanism":"IgE-mediated and other hypersensitivity to salivary antigens from biting Culicoides midges leads to seasonal self-trauma and chronic secondary changes.","tests":"Seasonality, lesion pattern and midge exposure plus exclusion of ectoparasites; avoid treating biopsy alone as Culicoides-specific.","ref":"Lecture 74 slide 31","options":[{"name":"Equine Culicoides hypersensitivity (sweet itch)","supports":"The specific host, distribution, exposure and microscopic lesion pattern provide the best combined evidence.","against":"The morphology still requires appropriate confirmation and cannot establish all etiologic details independently.","hist":"Superficial and deep perivascular eosinophilic dermatitis, epidermal hyperplasia; histology is a hypersensitivity pattern, not proof of insect type.","role":"best"},{"name":"Chorioptic mange","supports":"Pruritic crusts and rubbing possible.","against":"Commonly distal limbs and feathered pasterns, with surface mites identified.","hist":"Superficial parasitic dermatitis with mites.","role":"less"},{"name":"Onchocerca dermatitis","supports":"Equine vector-associated itch and pigment changes possible.","against":"Onchocerca microfilariae may be detected in skin and distribution can involve ventral midline/eyes.","hist":"Eosinophilic dermatitis sometimes with filarial microfilariae.","role":"less"},{"name":"Atopic dermatitis","supports":"Seasonal allergy and pruritus also possible.","against":"Mane/tail distribution and midge contact suggest insect-specific hypersensitivity but do not prove it.","hist":"Perivascular eosinophilic dermatitis indistinguishable on H&E.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s74-em","lecture":"74","system":"Skin","title":"Dog with target lesions and keratinocyte apoptosis","species":"Adult dog after recent medication change","history":"A dog develops acute symmetric erythematous annular and target-like skin lesions, with limited mucocutaneous erosion. Early biopsy demonstrates scattered necrotic keratinocytes and lymphocytes surrounding individual dying cells.","gross":"Erythematous annular target-like plaques, variable erosions; severity and mucosal involvement vary.","histo":"Lymphocytic interface dermatitis with single-cell keratinocyte apoptosis/satellitosis rather than full-thickness sheet necrosis.","best":"Erythema multiforme","morph":"Skin: acute apoptotic lymphocytic interface dermatitis","mechanism":"A dysregulated cell-mediated cytotoxic response against keratinocytes follows possible drug, infectious or neoplastic triggers; EM is distinct in the lecture from severe SJS/TEN patterns.","tests":"Biopsy fresh active lesions and careful drug/infection history; evaluate extent of detachment, mucosal injury and systemic instability.","ref":"Lecture 74 slide 20","options":[{"name":"Erythema multiforme","supports":"The specific host, distribution, exposure and microscopic lesion pattern provide the best combined evidence.","against":"The morphology still requires appropriate confirmation and cannot establish all etiologic details independently.","hist":"Lymphocytic interface dermatitis with single-cell keratinocyte apoptosis/satellitosis rather than full-thickness sheet necrosis.","role":"best"},{"name":"Toxic epidermal necrolysis","supports":"Severe acute epidermal injury and mucosal erosions can occur.","against":"TEN features widespread confluent full-thickness epidermal necrosis and detachment; do not equate targetoid EM with TEN.","hist":"Sheetlike epidermal necrosis with sparse inflammation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Discoid lupus erythematosus","supports":"Interface keratinocyte damage and facial erosions overlap.","against":"Chronic nasal-specific UV-associated lesions and basement membrane/pigment changes differ from widespread acute targets.","hist":"Chronic lymphocytic interface damage and pigmentary incontinence.","role":"less"},{"name":"Pemphigus vulgaris","supports":"Mucosal erosions and skin blisters overlap.","against":"Primarily suprabasal acantholysis rather than scattered apoptotic keratinocytes.","hist":"Suprabasal acantholytic intraepidermal clefts.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s74-mosquito","lecture":"74","system":"Skin","title":"Cat with seasonal pinna and nasal bridge ulcers","species":"Young outdoor cat","history":"During mosquito season, a cat develops recurrent crusted erosions on ear tips, dorsal nose and periocular skin. Cytology and biopsy show many eosinophils without organisms.","gross":"Symmetric papules, erosions and crusts of sparsely haired facial skin and ears.","histo":"Superficial/deep perivascular to interstitial eosinophilic dermatitis and epidermal erosion, sometimes eosinophilic collagen degeneration.","best":"Feline mosquito-bite hypersensitivity","morph":"Skin: eosinophilic allergic ulcerative dermatitis of pinnae and nasal bridge","mechanism":"Local hypersensitivity to mosquito salivary antigens drives eosinophil infiltration in exposed hairless skin.","tests":"Seasonal history and environmental insect exposure, elimination of dermatophytosis, mites, autoimmune/infectious disease and response to preventing bites.","ref":"Lecture 74 slide 40","options":[{"name":"Feline mosquito-bite hypersensitivity","supports":"The specific host, distribution, exposure and microscopic lesion pattern provide the best combined evidence.","against":"The morphology still requires appropriate confirmation and cannot establish all etiologic details independently.","hist":"Superficial/deep perivascular to interstitial eosinophilic dermatitis and epidermal erosion, sometimes eosinophilic collagen degeneration.","role":"best"},{"name":"Pemphigus foliaceus","supports":"Facial/pinna crusts and pustules can be similar.","against":"Subcorneal acantholytic pustules rather than dominant eosinophilic dermal infiltrates.","hist":"Acantholytic subcorneal pustular dermatitis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Notoedric mange","supports":"Cats can have intensely itchy facial and ear-edge crusts.","against":"Burrowing mites on scrapings and contact spread favor Notoedres.","hist":"Mites/eggs in cornified layer with crusting hypersensitivity.","role":"less"},{"name":"Feline eosinophilic granuloma complex","supports":"Allergic eosinophilic lesions may overlap histologically.","against":"Distribution and bite seasonality, not histology alone, implicate mosquitoes.","hist":"Eosinophilic granulomatous or plaque-form dermatitis depending syndrome.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"n69-hydro","lecture":"69","system":"Neuro","title":"Newborn puppy with enlarged ventricles","species":"Toy-breed puppy","history":"A juvenile dog has a domed skull and delayed neurologic development. Sectioning reveals markedly enlarged CSF-filled lateral ventricles, with a thin rim of remaining cerebral parenchyma.","gross":"Marked lateral ventricular enlargement and thinning of surrounding cerebral mantle; brain tissue persists rather than being entirely absent.","histo":"Ependymal flattening/disruption, periventricular edema and variable white-matter loss from chronic pressure.","best":"Congenital hydrocephalus","morph":"Brain: severe internal communicating or obstructive hydrocephalus","mechanism":"CSF accumulation due to impaired flow/resorption increases ventricular pressure, progressively distending ventricles and compressing the surrounding cerebrum.","tests":"Assess ventricles and CSF pathways by imaging and necropsy; distinguish congenital from acquired causes and examine for obstructive lesions.","ref":"Lecture 69 slides 5–6","options":[{"name":"Congenital hydrocephalus","supports":"The species, location, lesion morphology and histologic pattern align best with this diagnosis.","against":"Confirm the clinical and ancillary evidence, especially when histology represents a reaction pattern.","hist":"Ependymal flattening/disruption, periventricular edema and variable white-matter loss from chronic pressure.","role":"best"},{"name":"Hydranencephaly","supports":"Very thin or absent cerebral mantle can give a fluid-dominant cranial cavity.","against":"Hydranencephaly is extensive destruction/absence of cerebral hemispheres, not simply expanded ventricles compressing preserved cortex.","hist":"Absent cerebral hemispheres replaced by fluid-filled sacs.","role":"less"},{"name":"Porencephaly","supports":"Fluid-filled cavitation in brain parenchyma occurs.","against":"Focal destructive cerebral cavities rather than diffuse ventricular dilation.","hist":"Focal parenchymal tissue loss communicating with CSF spaces or not.","role":"less"},{"name":"Meningoencephalocele","supports":"Congenital CSF-filled cranial abnormalities occur.","against":"External herniation of meninges and brain through bony defect, not isolated ventricular enlargement.","hist":"Herniation through cranial defect with meningeal/brain tissue.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"n69-salt","lecture":"69","system":"Neuro","title":"Water-deprived pig with laminar cortical damage","species":"Grower pigs","history":"A group of pigs has restricted water access, then drinks freely after the supply is restored. Several develop blindness, seizures and abnormal pacing. Grossly the cerebral cortex is swollen; histology reveals laminar neuronal necrosis and prominent perivascular eosinophils.","gross":"Cerebral edema with possible laminar cortical discoloration; gross findings may be minimal.","histo":"Laminar cerebrocortical neuronal necrosis and distinctive perivascular eosinophilic cuffs, which are particularly associated with porcine salt poisoning.","best":"Salt toxicosis / water-deprivation sodium toxicosis","morph":"Cerebrum: laminar polioencephalomalacia with eosinophilic meningoencephalitis","mechanism":"Hypernatremia and rapid osmotic shifts during rehydration damage cerebral cells and blood vessels; species, exposure history and characteristic eosinophilia matter.","tests":"Check water access history, sodium levels if clinically available, and differentials for cortical malacia; sample brain for histology.","ref":"Lecture 69 slide 29","options":[{"name":"Salt toxicosis / water-deprivation sodium toxicosis","supports":"The species, location, lesion morphology and histologic pattern align best with this diagnosis.","against":"Confirm the clinical and ancillary evidence, especially when histology represents a reaction pattern.","hist":"Laminar cerebrocortical neuronal necrosis and distinctive perivascular eosinophilic cuffs, which are particularly associated with porcine salt poisoning.","role":"best"},{"name":"Thiamine-associated polioencephalomalacia","supports":"Cortical blindness and laminar necrosis are similar.","against":"Classic in ruminants; pig water restriction and eosinophilic cuffs support sodium toxicosis.","hist":"Laminar cortical necrosis without characteristic marked eosinophilic cuffing.","role":"less"},{"name":"Lead toxicosis","supports":"Neurologic signs and cortical injury can overlap.","against":"Lead exposure evidence/tissue concentrations required; eosinophilic meningoencephalitis and water history argue against.","hist":"Cortical neuronal injury, edema and vascular pathology.","role":"less"},{"name":"Aujeszky disease (pseudorabies)","supports":"Pigs can develop acute neurologic signs.","against":"Nonsuppurative encephalitis with viral lesions, not classic perivascular eosinophilic sodium-associated pattern.","hist":"Lymphocytic encephalitis with possible herpesviral inclusions.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"n70-dysautonomia","lecture":"70","system":"Neuro","title":"Cat with profound autonomic failure","species":"Young cat","history":"A cat presents with mydriasis, third-eyelid protrusion, reduced tear production, constipation, urine retention and regurgitation. Necropsy may reveal megaesophagus and distended bowel. Sympathetic and parasympathetic ganglia have degenerating neurons.","gross":"Often no definitive neural gross lesion; megaesophagus, dry mucosa and dilated gastrointestinal segments may be secondary.","histo":"Chromatolysis and degeneration of neurons in autonomic ganglia with variable gliosis and inflammatory change.","best":"Feline dysautonomia (Key-Gaskell syndrome)","morph":"Autonomic ganglia: widespread neuronal degeneration","mechanism":"Degeneration of autonomic neurons produces combined sympathetic, parasympathetic and enteric dysfunction, so multiple organ systems lose coordinated control.","tests":"Autonomic function testing, clinical distribution and postmortem ganglion histology; underlying cause often remains uncertain.","ref":"Lecture 70 slide 12","options":[{"name":"Feline dysautonomia (Key-Gaskell syndrome)","supports":"The species, location, lesion morphology and histologic pattern align best with this diagnosis.","against":"Confirm the clinical and ancillary evidence, especially when histology represents a reaction pattern.","hist":"Chromatolysis and degeneration of neurons in autonomic ganglia with variable gliosis and inflammatory change.","role":"best"},{"name":"Botulism","supports":"Weakness, swallowing impairment and reduced tone can occur.","against":"Neuromuscular junction acetylcholine-release blockade causes flaccid paralysis, not the full pupillary and visceral ganglion degeneration pattern.","hist":"No typical ganglion neuronal degeneration.","role":"less"},{"name":"Equine grass sickness","supports":"Autonomic dysfunction, ileus and ganglion neuronal loss overlap.","against":"Strongly equine rather than feline; comparable mechanism but species guides ranking.","hist":"Autonomic/enteric ganglion neuronal degeneration.","role":"less"},{"name":"Mechanical megacolon","supports":"Severe constipation and distension possible.","against":"Does not explain mydriasis, reduced lacrimation or diffuse autonomic ganglion changes.","hist":"Colon dilation without multisystem ganglion neuronopathy.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"n70-larynx","lecture":"70","system":"Neuro","title":"Horse with inspiratory roar and muscle atrophy","species":"Adult performance horse","history":"A horse develops progressive inspiratory roaring during exertion. Endoscopy shows poor abduction of the left arytenoid. At necropsy the left cricoarytenoideus dorsalis muscle is pale and atrophied.","gross":"Marked unilateral atrophy of left intrinsic laryngeal muscles, especially cricoarytenoideus dorsalis.","histo":"Neurogenic muscle atrophy with angular small fibers and degenerative changes in recurrent laryngeal nerve axons/myelin.","best":"Equine recurrent laryngeal neuropathy","morph":"Left recurrent laryngeal nerve: chronic axonal neuropathy with denervation atrophy","mechanism":"Length-dependent peripheral axonal degeneration preferentially injures the longer left recurrent laryngeal nerve, impairing arytenoid abduction.","tests":"Endoscopic assessment of arytenoid motion, muscle/nerve histology and exclusion of local structural laryngeal injury.","ref":"Lecture 70 slide 26","options":[{"name":"Equine recurrent laryngeal neuropathy","supports":"The species, location, lesion morphology and histologic pattern align best with this diagnosis.","against":"Confirm the clinical and ancillary evidence, especially when histology represents a reaction pattern.","hist":"Neurogenic muscle atrophy with angular small fibers and degenerative changes in recurrent laryngeal nerve axons/myelin.","role":"best"},{"name":"Laryngeal trauma","supports":"Can produce asymmetric movement and airway noise.","against":"Trauma should show local injury/scarring rather than typical distal axonal degeneration.","hist":"Fibrosis or inflammatory injury at site of trauma.","role":"less"},{"name":"Botulism","supports":"Can cause dysphagia and generalized flaccid weakness.","against":"Rapid generalized failure without chronic unilateral denervation atrophy.","hist":"No characteristic nerve degeneration required.","role":"less"},{"name":"Polyneuritis equi","supports":"Peripheral nerve inflammation can cause cranial/cauda equina deficits.","against":"Often multifocal peripheral nerve signs with inflammatory pathology rather than isolated left recurrent laryngeal nerve.","hist":"Lymphoplasmacytic/granulomatous peripheral neuritis.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"e78-melting","lecture":"78","system":"Eye","title":"Dog with rapidly deepening gelatinous corneal ulcer","species":"Brachycephalic dog","history":"A dog with prominent eyes develops a painful superficial corneal ulcer that becomes rapidly deeper and gelatinous over two days, with stromal liquefaction and impending perforation.","gross":"Soft gelatinous melting corneal stroma, craterlike deep ulcer and variable hypopyon.","histo":"Corneal epithelial loss with marked stromal collagenolysis, neutrophils, bacteria in some cases and variable stromal edema.","best":"Keratomalacia (melting corneal ulcer)","morph":"Cornea: severe ulcerative collagenolytic keratitis","mechanism":"Proteases from microbes and host inflammatory cells digest stromal collagen; compromised tear film or exposure can exacerbate rapid tissue loss.","tests":"Emergency slit-lamp/fluorescein examination, appropriate corneal cytology/culture before antimicrobial therapy when feasible, urgent ophthalmic care.","ref":"Lecture 78 slide 37","options":[{"name":"Keratomalacia (melting corneal ulcer)","supports":"The species, location, lesion morphology and histologic pattern align best with this diagnosis.","against":"Confirm the clinical and ancillary evidence, especially when histology represents a reaction pattern.","hist":"Corneal epithelial loss with marked stromal collagenolysis, neutrophils, bacteria in some cases and variable stromal edema.","role":"best"},{"name":"Simple superficial corneal ulcer","supports":"Epithelial defect causes pain and fluorescein uptake.","against":"Superficial ulcers lack active stromal liquefaction and rapid progressive thinning.","hist":"Epithelial discontinuity with limited stromal damage.","role":"less"},{"name":"Corneal sequestrum","supports":"A corneal focus can become discolored and painful.","against":"Typically feline pigmented brown-black necrotic plaque rather than translucent gelatinous collagenolysis.","hist":"Dense eosinophilic degenerative stromal collagen, pigment.","role":"less"},{"name":"Immune-mediated superficial keratitis","supports":"Corneal inflammation and edema occur.","against":"Not characterized by rapidly liquefying infected stroma.","hist":"Superficial vascular/inflammatory corneal infiltration without collagenolytic ulcer.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"e78-eru","lecture":"78","system":"Eye","title":"Horse with recurrent painful red eye","species":"Adult horse","history":"A horse has repeated episodes of blepharospasm, miosis, aqueous flare and corneal edema months apart. Progressive posterior synechiae and cataract form after repeated attacks.","gross":"Anterior uveitis with possible synechiae, lens changes and vitreous/retinal injury over time.","histo":"Lymphoplasmacytic and sometimes mixed uveal inflammation, iris/ciliary body damage and later fibrous sequelae.","best":"Equine recurrent uveitis (ERU)","morph":"Eye: chronic recurrent lymphoplasmacytic uveitis with secondary cataract/synechiae","mechanism":"Recurring immune-mediated ocular inflammation can follow or accompany infectious triggers such as Leptospira in some regions; repeated injury leads to permanent sequelae and vision loss.","tests":"Full ophthalmic examination, intraocular pressure, ocular ultrasound where indicated and targeted infectious investigation; no one gross finding alone proves etiology.","ref":"Lecture 78 slide 41","options":[{"name":"Equine recurrent uveitis (ERU)","supports":"The species, location, lesion morphology and histologic pattern align best with this diagnosis.","against":"Confirm the clinical and ancillary evidence, especially when histology represents a reaction pattern.","hist":"Lymphoplasmacytic and sometimes mixed uveal inflammation, iris/ciliary body damage and later fibrous sequelae.","role":"best"},{"name":"Corneal ulcer","supports":"Ocular pain, edema and tearing can look similar.","against":"Primary epithelial defect fluorescein-positive; ERU primarily affects uveal tract with miosis and aqueous flare.","hist":"Ulcerative keratitis rather than primary uveitis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Primary glaucoma","supports":"Can produce red painful enlarged eye and corneal edema.","against":"Glaucoma has increased pressure and typically mydriasis rather than miosis during uveitis.","hist":"Optic nerve/retinal damage and angle changes with elevated pressure.","role":"less"},{"name":"Lens-induced uveitis","supports":"Inflammation, synechiae and cataract overlap.","against":"Lens capsule rupture or protein leakage in setting of lens disease points to lens-derived inflammation.","hist":"Granulomatous or lymphoplasmacytic inflammation centered on damaged lens.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"ear79-hematoma","lecture":"79","system":"Ear","title":"Dog with a swollen fluctuant pinna","species":"Adult dog with chronic ear scratching","history":"A dog vigorously shakes its head with concurrent otitis externa. The ear flap becomes acutely thick and fluctuant with a blood-filled cavity between cartilage and skin.","gross":"Fluctuant blood-filled swelling of pinna, sometimes involving much of the concave ear surface.","histo":"Separation and hemorrhage between auricular cartilage and skin, progressing to fibrovascular organization, fibrosis and cartilage deformity if chronic.","best":"Aural hematoma","morph":"Pinna: acute dissecting hemorrhage between cartilage and overlying tissues","mechanism":"Self-trauma from head-shaking ruptures pinna vessels, creating a hematoma; persistent fluid and organization can cause cauliflower-ear deformity.","tests":"Examine and treat primary otitis or other itch/pain trigger, inspect hematoma and evaluate potential bleeding disorders when indicated.","ref":"Lecture 79 slide 10","options":[{"name":"Aural hematoma","supports":"The species, location, lesion morphology and histologic pattern align best with this diagnosis.","against":"Confirm the clinical and ancillary evidence, especially when histology represents a reaction pattern.","hist":"Separation and hemorrhage between auricular cartilage and skin, progressing to fibrovascular organization, fibrosis and cartilage deformity if chronic.","role":"best"},{"name":"Auricular abscess","supports":"Painful swollen ear flap might contain fluid.","against":"Purulent exudate and neutrophilic infection, not primarily blood.","hist":"Suppurative inflammation with bacterial colonies.","role":"less"},{"name":"Auricular chondritis","supports":"Pinna thickens, becomes painful or deformed.","against":"Primarily cartilage-targeting inflammation rather than acute blood cavity.","hist":"Cartilage inflammation/necrosis and perichondritis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Auricular infarction","supports":"Discolored necrotic pinna can swell or ulcerate.","against":"Vascular ischemic necrosis typically affects ear margins rather than a fluctuant blood-filled cavity.","hist":"Thrombi and ischemic necrosis of ear tissues.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"ear79-polyp","lecture":"79","system":"Ear","title":"Young cat with nasopharyngeal mass and head tilt","species":"Young cat","history":"A young cat presents with chronic unilateral otitis, head tilt and intermittent stertor. A smooth pink pedunculated mass arises near the auditory tube/tympanic cavity and extends toward the nasopharynx.","gross":"Smooth pink-red stalked inflammatory mass from middle ear or auditory tube, often projecting into external canal or nasopharynx.","histo":"Polypoid edematous fibrovascular stroma with lymphoplasmacytic inflammation beneath respiratory or squamous epithelium.","best":"Feline inflammatory aural/nasopharyngeal polyp","morph":"Middle ear/nasopharynx: benign inflammatory polypoid proliferation","mechanism":"Inflammatory mucosal proliferation and obstruction around auditory tube/middle ear can cause chronic otitis and vestibular signs.","tests":"Otoscopy and imaging when needed; histopathology after removal; distinguish from neoplasia and address residual stalk recurrence.","ref":"Lecture 79 slide 21","options":[{"name":"Feline inflammatory aural/nasopharyngeal polyp","supports":"The species, location, lesion morphology and histologic pattern align best with this diagnosis.","against":"Confirm the clinical and ancillary evidence, especially when histology represents a reaction pattern.","hist":"Polypoid edematous fibrovascular stroma with lymphoplasmacytic inflammation beneath respiratory or squamous epithelium.","role":"best"},{"name":"Ceruminous gland adenocarcinoma","supports":"May obstruct ear canal and create chronic otitis.","against":"Often older cats/dogs with infiltrative glandular neoplasia rather than benign stalked juvenile polyp.","hist":"Atypical infiltrative ceruminous epithelial tubules and solid growth.","role":"less"},{"name":"Aural cholesteatoma / tympanokeratoma","supports":"Middle ear mass and vestibular signs overlap.","against":"Keratin-filled epithelial cyst and bone destruction rather than fibrovascular inflammatory polyp.","hist":"Keratinizing squamous epithelial lining with laminated keratin and adjacent chronic inflammation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Lymphoma","supports":"Aural/nasopharyngeal mass and obstruction possible.","against":"Monomorphic malignant lymphocytes rather than mixed inflammatory polyp stroma.","hist":"Sheets of atypical lymphoid cells.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s71-catfrag","lecture":"71","title":"Cat with skin that tears during ordinary handling","species":"Older diabetic cat","history":"An older cat with difficult-to-control diabetes has muscle wasting, a potbellied appearance, and dorsal trunk skin that tears when gently restrained. There is little primary pruritus and wounds heal slowly.","gross":"Extremely thin translucent skin with large irregular tears after minimal handling, alopecia and delayed healing.","histo":"Severely thinned dermis with decreased collagen bundles and atrophic adnexa, sometimes epidermal atrophy; lesions may be subtle in small biopsies.","best":"Feline hyperadrenocorticism with acquired skin fragility","morph":"Skin: severe dermal atrophy and acquired cutaneous fragility","mechanism":"Excess glucocorticoid inhibits dermal fibroblast activity and collagen production, undermining tensile strength; diabetes commonly coexists with feline hyperadrenocorticism.","tests":"Investigate cortisol excess using appropriate feline endocrine tests, imaging and concurrent disorders; exclude iatrogenic steroid exposure and other causes of acquired skin fragility.","ref":"Lecture 71 slide 32 (feline hyperadrenocorticism)","system":"Skin","options":[{"name":"Feline hyperadrenocorticism with acquired skin fragility","supports":"The specified host, lesion distribution, history and diagnostic histopathologic pattern make this the leading diagnosis.","against":"Gross appearances alone are not definitive; the listed ancillary workup and competing conditions must be considered.","hist":"Severely thinned dermis with decreased collagen bundles and atrophic adnexa, sometimes epidermal atrophy; lesions may be subtle in small biopsies.","role":"best"},{"name":"Cutaneous asthenia / Ehlers–Danlos-like disorder","supports":"Skin tears with minor trauma.","against":"Usually inherited and recognized younger; excessive stretchability rather than acquired severe dermal thinning in a diabetic senior cat.","hist":"Disorganized collagen and variable abnormal dermal collagen ultrastructure.","role":"less"},{"name":"Iatrogenic glucocorticoid excess","supports":"Steroid exposure can induce nearly identical thin fragile skin.","against":"No exogenous steroid history was elicited; medication review still essential.","hist":"Dermal atrophy similar to endogenous hyperadrenocorticism.","role":"less"},{"name":"Feline dermatophytosis","supports":"Alopecia and crusting can be present.","against":"Does not explain extreme mechanical skin tearing and dermal collagen atrophy.","hist":"Fungal arthroconidia/hyphae in hairs.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s71-digital","lecture":"71","title":"Cow with strawberry-red lesion above the heel bulbs","species":"Lactating dairy cow","history":"An adult dairy cow is lame in a wet, contaminated housing environment. At the plantar aspect of a hind foot, just proximal to the interdigital cleft and heel bulbs, is a sharply demarcated painful moist red lesion; chronic areas form elongated papillary projections.","gross":"Circumscribed raw granular red ulcerative plaque near the plantar heel, later papillomatous filiform gray/brown projections ('hairy heel wart').","histo":"Papillomatous epidermal hyperplasia, erosive to ulcerative neutrophilic dermatitis, superficial necrosis and spirochetal organisms in affected epidermis.","best":"Bovine digital dermatitis","morph":"Distal limb: ulcerative to papillomatous dermatitis associated with spirochetes","mechanism":"Polymicrobial disease involving Treponema spp. in a favorable wet environment impairs epidermal integrity and produces painful proliferative inflammation.","tests":"Characteristic lesion site and stage, herd examination and hygiene review; histopathology and treponemal testing for atypical lesions. Assess other claw causes of lameness.","ref":"Lecture 71 slide 41 (bovine papillomatous digital dermatitis)","system":"Skin","options":[{"name":"Bovine digital dermatitis","supports":"The specified host, lesion distribution, history and diagnostic histopathologic pattern make this the leading diagnosis.","against":"Gross appearances alone are not definitive; the listed ancillary workup and competing conditions must be considered.","hist":"Papillomatous epidermal hyperplasia, erosive to ulcerative neutrophilic dermatitis, superficial necrosis and spirochetal organisms in affected epidermis.","role":"best"},{"name":"Interdigital foot rot","supports":"Severe lameness, painful inflamed foot and foul odor overlap.","against":"Foot rot chiefly produces necrotizing interdigital phlegmon/swelling rather than characteristic heel papillomatous surface lesions.","hist":"Acute necrotizing suppurative interdigital dermatitis and subcutitis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Heel horn erosion","supports":"Caudal heel damage can occur in unhygienic barns.","against":"Primary horn degradation, not proliferative epidermal papillae and strawberry-like ulcerated skin.","hist":"Horn erosion and variable superficial inflammation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Bovine papilloma","supports":"Warty mass may resemble chronic digital dermatitis.","against":"BPV papillary fibroepithelial growth typically not a painful wet erosive heel plaque linked to Treponema.","hist":"Papillary epithelial hyperplasia over fibrovascular cores, usually without treponemal ulcerative lesions.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s72-lumpy","lecture":"72","title":"Cow with deep dermal nodules and central sitfasts","species":"Adult cow in a geographic outbreak","history":"A febrile cow in an area experiencing spread of biting insects develops numerous firm full-thickness skin nodules, swelling of superficial nodes and variable respiratory disease. Some nodules become hard central necrotic plugs ('sitfasts').","gross":"Multiple firm deep dermal nodules including skin, subcutis, sometimes muscle, with necrotic cores and skin sloughing.","histo":"Multifocal necrotizing dermal vasculitis and perivascular inflammation, lymphohistiocytic infiltrates and possible intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies.","best":"Lumpy skin disease (capripoxvirus)","morph":"Skin: multifocal nodular necrotizing dermatitis and vasculitis consistent with capripoxviral infection","mechanism":"Systemic lumpy skin disease virus infection targets dermal tissues and vessels, provoking vascular injury, deep necrosis and the characteristic separated necrotic plugs.","tests":"Immediately implement jurisdiction-specific reportable/exotic disease procedures, biosecurity and official capripoxvirus PCR. Lesions alone cannot distinguish this from other nodular diseases.","ref":"Lecture 72 slides 5–6 (lumpy skin disease named within capripoxvirus family chart); external veterinary reference adds detailed gross findings","system":"Skin","options":[{"name":"Lumpy skin disease (capripoxvirus)","supports":"The specified host, lesion distribution, history and diagnostic histopathologic pattern make this the leading diagnosis.","against":"Gross appearances alone are not definitive; the listed ancillary workup and competing conditions must be considered.","hist":"Multifocal necrotizing dermal vasculitis and perivascular inflammation, lymphohistiocytic infiltrates and possible intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies.","role":"best"},{"name":"Bovine herpesvirus 2 pseudo-lumpy skin disease","supports":"May cause generalized nodular skin lesions in cattle.","against":"Herpesvirus 2 rather than capripoxvirus; usually more superficial and often self-limiting.","hist":"Multifocal epidermal necrosis and herpesviral cytopathic changes rather than deep capripox-associated nodules.","role":"less"},{"name":"Dermatophilosis","supports":"Crusted nodules and thickened wet-weather skin may look similar.","against":"Paintbrush crusts with branching bacterial filaments rather than deep full-thickness necrotic nodules.","hist":"Exudative neutrophilic epidermitis with segmented Dermatophilus organisms.","role":"less"},{"name":"Bovine papillomatosis","supports":"Raised cutaneous masses can occur.","against":"Papillomas are chronic exophytic epithelial growths, not acute febrile nodules with sitfasts.","hist":"Orderly papillary epithelial hyperplasia with fibrovascular cores.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s72-bps","lecture":"72","title":"Calf with target-like raised-rim lesions inside the mouth","species":"Weaned dairy calf","history":"Several calves have mild oral discomfort, circular red papules and shallow erosions on lips, muzzle, tongue and buccal mucosa. A milker reports a transient papule on a finger. No widespread hoof vesicles or severe herd fever is reported.","gross":"Circumscribed rounded slightly raised red oral papules with central superficial erosion and pale/elevated rim.","histo":"Proliferative ballooning degeneration of epithelial cells, cytoplasmic eosinophilic poxviral inclusion bodies, crusting and surface ulceration.","best":"Bovine papular stomatitis (parapoxvirus)","morph":"Oral mucosa: multifocal proliferative viral papular stomatitis","mechanism":"Parapoxvirus infects oral keratinocytes to induce papular proliferation and focal epithelial injury. Human contact lesions are possible; gross vesicular-disease differentials warrant attention.","tests":"PCR when etiologic confirmation or regulatory exclusion is needed; assess herd for genuine vesicular lesions and contact state animal health authorities for any suspicion of FMD or vesicular stomatitis.","ref":"Lecture 72 slide 12 (bovine papular stomatitis)","system":"Skin","options":[{"name":"Bovine papular stomatitis (parapoxvirus)","supports":"The specified host, lesion distribution, history and diagnostic histopathologic pattern make this the leading diagnosis.","against":"Gross appearances alone are not definitive; the listed ancillary workup and competing conditions must be considered.","hist":"Proliferative ballooning degeneration of epithelial cells, cytoplasmic eosinophilic poxviral inclusion bodies, crusting and surface ulceration.","role":"best"},{"name":"Foot-and-mouth disease","supports":"Cattle may develop oral erosions and contagious outbreaks.","against":"Typically vesicles that rupture and commonly affect feet and teats; a vesicular outbreak requires immediate official assessment rather than inference from photographs.","hist":"Epithelial vesiculation with hydropic degeneration; organism confirmation essential.","role":"less"},{"name":"Pseudocowpox","supports":"Zoonotic bovine parapoxvirus causing papules and erosions.","against":"More classically teat lesions in cows and human milker's nodules; agents require testing.","hist":"Parapoxviral ballooning and cytoplasmic inclusions; not reliably differentiated by H&E.","role":"less"},{"name":"Bovine viral diarrhea mucosal disease","supports":"Oral erosions can occur with systemic illness.","against":"Typically immunocompromised persistently infected cattle with widespread erosions/diarrhea, not mild papular raised-rim lesions.","hist":"Mucosal epithelial erosion/necrosis, not the same parapox proliferative pattern.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s72-seneca","lecture":"72","title":"Pig with snout and coronary-band vesicles","species":"Finishing pig in commercial herd","history":"Several finishing pigs suddenly develop vesicles and erosions of the snout, interdigital skin and coronary bands with lameness. A few nursing piglets elsewhere on the farm have unexplained mortality.","gross":"Vesicles or ruptured superficial erosions on snout, lips, oral mucosa, coronary band and distal feet.","histo":"Intraepidermal hydropic degeneration and vesiculation with variable neutrophilic inflammation and ulceration; histology is not virus-specific.","best":"Senecavirus A vesicular disease","morph":"Skin and mucosa: multifocal acute vesicular dermatitis/stomatitis","mechanism":"Senecavirus A infection injures epithelium and causes a syndrome clinically indistinguishable from major transboundary vesicular diseases without laboratory testing.","tests":"STOP routine handling of suspect vesicular disease and contact responsible animal health authorities immediately; exclude FMD and other reportable vesicular diseases with approved testing before attributing to Senecavirus A.","ref":"Lecture 72 slide 21 (Senecavirus A; not the historical swine vesicular disease virus)","system":"Skin","options":[{"name":"Senecavirus A vesicular disease","supports":"The specified host, lesion distribution, history and diagnostic histopathologic pattern make this the leading diagnosis.","against":"Gross appearances alone are not definitive; the listed ancillary workup and competing conditions must be considered.","hist":"Intraepidermal hydropic degeneration and vesiculation with variable neutrophilic inflammation and ulceration; histology is not virus-specific.","role":"best"},{"name":"Foot-and-mouth disease","supports":"Nearly identical oral and foot vesicles in pigs.","against":"Cannot be safely excluded by clinical pattern; official assay and response required.","hist":"Vesicular epithelial degeneration is nonspecific.","role":"less"},{"name":"Vesicular stomatitis","supports":"Pigs, horses and cattle may show vesicles/erosions.","against":"Clinical indistinguishability requires official testing and epidemiology.","hist":"Nonspecific vesicular epithelial lesions.","role":"less"},{"name":"Historical swine vesicular disease virus","supports":"Similar swine lesions may be described in older references.","against":"Distinct enterovirus; Senecavirus A is a different virus and the name should not be conflated.","hist":"Vesicular dermatitis requiring virus-specific molecular testing.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s72-canker","lecture":"72","title":"Horse hoof frog with proliferative cauliflower-like horn","species":"Adult draft horse","history":"A horse kept in wet conditions develops chronic hoof odor and a soft friable overgrowth arising from the frog, extending into bars and sole. Gentle debridement exposes bleeding papillomatous tissue and white filamentous horn.","gross":"Hyperplastic friable soft white vegetative papillae and horn in frog/sole; may bleed easily.","histo":"Pronounced papillomatous epidermal hyperplasia, dyskeratosis/parakeratosis and variable suppurative inflammation in abnormal hoof horn.","best":"Equine canker (proliferative pododermatitis)","morph":"Hoof: chronic papillomatous proliferative pododermatitis","mechanism":"Abnormal keratinization and chronic mixed inflammation produce proliferative moist horn; infectious contributors and environmental factors are implicated but a single universally causal organism has not been proven.","tests":"Examination of lesion depth and hoof structures, histopathology for uncertain lesions, evaluation of management/hygiene and specialist farrier/veterinary treatment.","ref":"Lecture 72 slide 42 (equine proliferative pododermatitis / canker)","system":"Skin","options":[{"name":"Equine canker (proliferative pododermatitis)","supports":"The specified host, lesion distribution, history and diagnostic histopathologic pattern make this the leading diagnosis.","against":"Gross appearances alone are not definitive; the listed ancillary workup and competing conditions must be considered.","hist":"Pronounced papillomatous epidermal hyperplasia, dyskeratosis/parakeratosis and variable suppurative inflammation in abnormal hoof horn.","role":"best"},{"name":"Equine thrush","supports":"Wet unsanitary feet, foul odor and frog pathology overlap.","against":"Thrush destroys and undermines frog tissue with black exudate, whereas canker proliferates papillary horn.","hist":"Necrotizing pododermatitis rather than papillomatous epidermal proliferation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Hoof keratoma","supports":"Abnormal focal horn mass can occur.","against":"Keratoma is localized keratin mass under hoof wall, not friable diffuse papillomatous frog proliferation.","hist":"Concentrically organized keratinized tissue without marked inflammatory papillae.","role":"less"},{"name":"Exuberant granulation tissue","supports":"Friable proliferative bleeding tissue may form after hoof injury.","against":"Granulation tissue has richly vascular fibroblastic stroma without marked abnormal epithelial horn papillae.","hist":"Fibrovascular proliferation with capillaries and fibroblasts.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s72-pdns","lecture":"72","title":"Growing pig with purple skin plaques and renal failure","species":"12-week-old grower pig","history":"A group of growing pigs develops fever and well-demarcated irregular dark purple skin patches on hind limbs, abdomen and perineum. Affected pigs are lethargic; kidneys are enlarged with cortical petechiae and the urinalysis suggests renal injury.","gross":"Multifocal red-purple to black crusting cutaneous plaques, often hindquarters and ventrum; enlarged pale kidneys with petechiae.","histo":"Necrotizing leukocytoclastic vasculitis with fibrinoid vessel-wall injury and thrombosis in skin; renal glomerulonephritis and interstitial inflammation.","best":"Porcine dermatitis and nephropathy syndrome (PDNS)","morph":"Skin and kidney: systemic necrotizing vasculitis with glomerulonephritis","mechanism":"Immune-complex-associated small-vessel injury causes dermal hemorrhage and kidney damage; PCV2 association is epidemiologic, not evidence that infection by itself causes every case.","tests":"Histopathology of skin and kidney, PCV2/herd assessment and exclusion of septicemic viral/bacterial disease by approved testing when indicated.","ref":"Lecture 72 slide 45 (PDNS)","system":"Skin","options":[{"name":"Porcine dermatitis and nephropathy syndrome (PDNS)","supports":"The specified host, lesion distribution, history and diagnostic histopathologic pattern make this the leading diagnosis.","against":"Gross appearances alone are not definitive; the listed ancillary workup and competing conditions must be considered.","hist":"Necrotizing leukocytoclastic vasculitis with fibrinoid vessel-wall injury and thrombosis in skin; renal glomerulonephritis and interstitial inflammation.","role":"best"},{"name":"Erysipelothrix rhusiopathiae septicemia","supports":"Purple or diamond-shaped skin lesions and systemic illness overlap.","against":"Erysipelas classically has characteristic geometric dermal infarcts and may cause arthritis/endocarditis without the same immune-complex glomerulonephritis pattern.","hist":"Bacterial septic vasculitis with thrombosis, possible arthritis and vegetative endocarditis.","role":"less"},{"name":"African swine fever","supports":"Fever, hemorrhage and skin cyanosis may mimic.","against":"Serious reportable disease requires immediate official exclusion whenever epidemiologically plausible.","hist":"Widespread hemorrhagic lymphoid/systemic pathology, distinct agent testing.","role":"less"},{"name":"Classical swine fever","supports":"Purple skin, fever and renal petechiae possible.","against":"Cannot rule out by photo alone; official testing and biosecurity when suspected.","hist":"Systemic hemorrhagic and lymphoid lesions with variable vascular changes.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-notoedres","lecture":"73","title":"Cat with thick crusted pinnae and contagious head itch","species":"Outdoor kitten in multi-cat rescue","history":"Several cats develop severe itching beginning at the ear margins and spreading across the face and neck. The most affected kitten has adherent yellow-gray crusts and thickened skin; superficial scrapings contain round burrowing mites.","gross":"Exuberant yellow-gray adherent crusts over pinna margins, forehead, face and neck with alopecia, excoriation and lichenification.","histo":"Acanthosis and hyperkeratosis with burrows containing round Notoedres mites or eggs within stratum corneum; superficial perivascular/eosinophilic inflammation.","best":"Notoedric mange (Notoedres cati)","morph":"Skin: severe hyperkeratotic parasitic dermatitis with intraepidermal mites","mechanism":"Highly contagious burrowing Notoedres mites damage cornified skin and evoke intense hypersensitivity inflammation.","tests":"Superficial skin scrapings for mites and eggs, examine in-contact cats; treat associated animals as indicated, and assess zoonotic exposure risk.","ref":"Lecture 73 slide 9 (notoedric mange)","system":"Skin","options":[{"name":"Notoedric mange (Notoedres cati)","supports":"The specified host, lesion distribution, history and diagnostic histopathologic pattern make this the leading diagnosis.","against":"Gross appearances alone are not definitive; the listed ancillary workup and competing conditions must be considered.","hist":"Acanthosis and hyperkeratosis with burrows containing round Notoedres mites or eggs within stratum corneum; superficial perivascular/eosinophilic inflammation.","role":"best"},{"name":"Sarcoptes scabiei","supports":"Burrowing mites with severe pruritus and crusting.","against":"Species epidemiology and mite morphology differentiate; cats can rarely acquire Sarcoptes.","hist":"Burrows with mites and crusting dermatitis, nonspecific without organism morphology.","role":"less"},{"name":"Feline mosquito-bite hypersensitivity","supports":"Ear tips and face can become intensely crusted.","against":"Seasonal bite exposure and eosinophilic lesions without burrowing mites.","hist":"Eosinophilic dermatitis without intraepidermal mites.","role":"less"},{"name":"Pemphigus foliaceus","supports":"Pinna and facial crusting can be striking.","against":"Subcorneal acantholytic pustules, not multiple burrowing mites or contagious cluster.","hist":"Subcorneal neutrophilic pustules with acantholytic cells.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-scalyleg","lecture":"73","title":"Chicken with lifted leg scales and thick crusts","species":"Backyard chicken","history":"Several adult chickens have progressive thickening and elevation of shank/toe scales, crusting and difficulty perching. The lesions extend under scales and mites are found on skin scraping.","gross":"Raised thickened leg scales, whitish-gray proliferative crusts and deformity of distal legs and toes in severe cases.","histo":"Pronounced orthokeratotic hyperkeratosis with epidermal thickening and small burrowing Knemidokoptes mites within crust/epidermal tunnels.","best":"Knemidokoptes mutans scaly-leg mange","morph":"Distal leg: chronic severe hyperkeratotic mite-associated dermatitis","mechanism":"Burrowing knemidokoptid mites proliferate beneath avian leg scales, stimulating chronic keratin accumulation and distortion of normal scale attachments.","tests":"Inspect and microscopically examine scrapings from affected scales; investigate flock contacts and enclosure hygiene.","ref":"Lecture 73 slide 15 (Knemidokoptes mutans listed; additional gross details from veterinary ectoparasite reference)","system":"Skin","options":[{"name":"Knemidokoptes mutans scaly-leg mange","supports":"The specified host, lesion distribution, history and diagnostic histopathologic pattern make this the leading diagnosis.","against":"Gross appearances alone are not definitive; the listed ancillary workup and competing conditions must be considered.","hist":"Pronounced orthokeratotic hyperkeratosis with epidermal thickening and small burrowing Knemidokoptes mites within crust/epidermal tunnels.","role":"best"},{"name":"Avian bacterial pododermatitis / bumblefoot","supports":"Lameness and thick crusting distal leg lesions can occur.","against":"Bumblefoot usually centers on plantar footpad pressure lesions rather than raised shank scales.","hist":"Necrosuppurative plantar pododermatitis and abscesses.","role":"less"},{"name":"Chicken leg papillomatosis","supports":"Proliferative keratotic leg lesions could resemble warty scales.","against":"Mites within tunnels and flock-associated scale elevation favor knemidokoptosis.","hist":"Papillomatous epithelial hyperplasia without mites.","role":"less"},{"name":"Traumatic or nutritional hyperkeratosis","supports":"Distal leg scale thickening possible.","against":"Demonstrated mites and progressive contagious flock pattern favor parasitism.","hist":"Hyperkeratosis without burrowing arthropods.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s74-ten","lecture":"74","title":"Dog with widespread epidermal sloughing after drug exposure","species":"Adult dog","history":"Days after starting a new medication, a dog develops fever, mucosal erosions and widespread painful fragile epidermis that detaches in broad sheets. Early biopsies show confluent keratinocyte death rather than isolated pustules.","gross":"Extensive epidermal detachment, large erosions and severe mucosal involvement; life-threatening fluid loss and infection risk.","histo":"Confluent full-thickness epidermal necrosis with minimal to moderate lymphocytic interface infiltrate; no primary acantholytic pustule pattern.","best":"Toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN)","morph":"Skin: severe confluent full-thickness epidermal necrosis","mechanism":"Severe cytotoxic keratinocyte injury—frequently drug-associated—causes widespread epidermal loss with barrier failure and systemic morbidity. Stevens–Johnson syndrome and TEN comprise a spectrum distinct from typical erythema multiforme.","tests":"Emergency clinical management, medication timeline, fresh-lesion biopsy and exclusion of staphylococcal exfoliative disease, burns and other bullous disorders.","ref":"Lecture 74 slide 20 (TEN and erythema multiforme distinction)","system":"Skin","options":[{"name":"Toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN)","supports":"The specified host, lesion distribution, history and diagnostic histopathologic pattern make this the leading diagnosis.","against":"Gross appearances alone are not definitive; the listed ancillary workup and competing conditions must be considered.","hist":"Confluent full-thickness epidermal necrosis with minimal to moderate lymphocytic interface infiltrate; no primary acantholytic pustule pattern.","role":"best"},{"name":"Erythema multiforme","supports":"Medication/infectious triggers and targetoid erythematous lesions overlap.","against":"Typical EM has individual apoptotic keratinocytes/satellitosis, not widespread confluent full-thickness epidermal necrosis.","hist":"Lymphocytic interface dermatitis with scattered single-cell apoptosis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Pemphigus vulgaris","supports":"Mucosal erosions and blisters overlap.","against":"Suprabasal acantholysis leaves basal-cell tombstones; TEN is full-layer necrosis.","hist":"Suprabasal intraepidermal clefts with acantholytic keratinocytes.","role":"less"},{"name":"Severe superficial bacterial exfoliative dermatitis","supports":"Diffuse epidermal peeling and inflammation possible.","against":"More superficial toxin-associated split rather than extensive full-thickness necrosis; organism-specific context matters.","hist":"Superficial subcorneal epidermal split and bacterial lesions.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s74-atopy","lecture":"74","title":"Young dog with chronic itchy paws and ears","species":"2-year-old dog","history":"A dog develops recurrent seasonal pruritus of the paws, muzzle, ventrum and ear pinnae, with intermittent otitis and greasy interdigital plaques. Flea prevention has been inconsistent and cytology currently reveals Malassezia.","gross":"Excoriated interdigital and flexural erythema, periocular/facial rubbing and chronic lichenification with secondary otitis; no pathognomonic gross lesion.","histo":"Superficial perivascular eosinophilic and lymphocytic dermatitis with spongiosis and variable epidermal hyperplasia; a nonspecific hypersensitivity reaction pattern.","best":"Canine atopic dermatitis with secondary Malassezia overgrowth","morph":"Skin: chronic pruritic superficial hypersensitivity dermatitis with secondary yeast proliferation","mechanism":"Environmental allergen sensitization and defective skin-barrier function contribute to chronic pruritus and inflammation; secondary yeast and bacterial infections amplify the cycle.","tests":"Clinical diagnosis after excluding ectoparasites, flea allergy, food-responsive dermatitis and infection; use cytology for secondary microbes. Serum IgE tests alone do not establish atopic dermatitis.","ref":"Lecture 74 slides 26–27 (canine atopic dermatitis)","system":"Skin","options":[{"name":"Canine atopic dermatitis with secondary Malassezia overgrowth","supports":"The specified host, lesion distribution, history and diagnostic histopathologic pattern make this the leading diagnosis.","against":"Gross appearances alone are not definitive; the listed ancillary workup and competing conditions must be considered.","hist":"Superficial perivascular eosinophilic and lymphocytic dermatitis with spongiosis and variable epidermal hyperplasia; a nonspecific hypersensitivity reaction pattern.","role":"best"},{"name":"Flea allergy dermatitis","supports":"Intense pruritus and secondary infection can occur.","against":"Typically caudal dorsum/tail-base emphasis, although overlap exists and flea control remains essential.","hist":"Superficial eosinophilic hypersensitivity inflammation, nonspecific.","role":"less"},{"name":"Sarcoptic mange","supports":"Contagious intensely pruritic dog with often few mites.","against":"Ear margin and elbow/hock lesions plus contact history favor scabies; perform mite evaluation.","hist":"Variable burrowing mites and nonspecific hypersensitivity dermatitis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Cutaneous adverse food reaction","supports":"Can cause chronic ear, paw and ventral pruritus and identical histology.","against":"Food trial is needed when suspected; distribution and biopsy do not distinguish food allergy from atopy.","hist":"Superficial perivascular dermatitis with variable eosinophils, indistinguishable from atopic reaction.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s71-pillowfoot","lecture":"71","title":"Cat with soft doughy swollen pawpads","species":"Adult cat","history":"A cat has gradually enlarged soft footpads with white crisscross striae and occasional fissuring. Mild lameness develops after one pad ulcerates. Serum proteins show hyperglobulinemia.","gross":"Diffusely swollen compressible doughy pawpads, sometimes violaceous, with pale striations and ulceration.","histo":"Dense predominantly plasmacytic dermal/subcutaneous inflammation, sometimes Russell bodies, with variable hemorrhage or ulceration.","best":"Feline plasma-cell pododermatitis","morph":"Pawpads: severe plasmacytic pododermatitis","mechanism":"An immune-associated plasma cell-rich infiltrate expands pad tissues and may disrupt the epidermis; hypergammaglobulinemia and other immune conditions can coexist.","tests":"Biopsy/cytology when atypical; rule out bacterial infection and neoplasia, assess systemic conditions and evaluate renal disease as indicated.","ref":"Lecture 71 slide 40","system":"Skin","options":[{"name":"Feline plasma-cell pododermatitis","supports":"The species, lesion distribution, microscopic pattern, and associated clinical findings most strongly support this diagnosis.","against":"Gross lesions alone are insufficient; targeted tests and exclusions remain necessary.","hist":"Dense predominantly plasmacytic dermal/subcutaneous inflammation, sometimes Russell bodies, with variable hemorrhage or ulceration.","role":"best"},{"name":"Bacterial pododermatitis","supports":"Lameness, painful swollen pad and ulceration can occur.","against":"Purulent infection and neutrophils predominate rather than a diffuse plasmacytic infiltrate.","hist":"Suppurative cellulitis with bacteria.","role":"less"},{"name":"Feline eosinophilic granuloma","supports":"Pads can be swollen or ulcerated in unusual allergic lesions.","against":"Eosinophilic granulomatous inflammation and collagen degeneration, not plasma cell-dominant expansion.","hist":"Eosinophilic granulomatous dermatitis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Cutaneous lymphoma","supports":"Infiltrative nodules can affect extremities.","against":"Monomorphic atypical lymphoid cells rather than mixed mature plasma cell infiltrates.","hist":"Atypical neoplastic lymphoid sheets.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s71-xanthoma","lecture":"71","title":"Bird with yellow cutaneous plaques and elevated lipids","species":"Obese pet bird","history":"A bird eating a high-fat seed-heavy diet develops yellow irregular raised plaques near wing and abdominal skin. A biopsy reveals lipid-rich foamy macrophages and cholesterol clefts.","gross":"Yellow to orange plaques and nodules in skin, sometimes ulcerating and becoming friable.","histo":"Expansile aggregates of foamy macrophages, lipid droplets and cholesterol clefts with giant-cell reaction and fibrosis.","best":"Cutaneous xanthoma","morph":"Skin: lipid-laden xanthogranulomatous dermatitis / xanthomatous lesion","mechanism":"Local accumulation of lipid-laden macrophages is associated with dyslipidemia and lipid metabolism disturbances; the lesion is not automatically malignant.","tests":"Histopathology, nutritional assessment and lipid/liver disease workup as appropriate.","ref":"Lecture 71 slide 27","system":"Skin","options":[{"name":"Cutaneous xanthoma","supports":"The species, lesion distribution, microscopic pattern, and associated clinical findings most strongly support this diagnosis.","against":"Gross lesions alone are insufficient; targeted tests and exclusions remain necessary.","hist":"Expansile aggregates of foamy macrophages, lipid droplets and cholesterol clefts with giant-cell reaction and fibrosis.","role":"best"},{"name":"Lipoma","supports":"Soft masses may be yellow.","against":"Mature adipocytes form a neoplasm rather than foamy dermal macrophage aggregates.","hist":"Well-differentiated mature adipocytes.","role":"less"},{"name":"Cutaneous fibrosarcoma","supports":"Birds can develop firm skin masses.","against":"Spindle-cell neoplasia does not show diffuse lipid-laden macrophages with clefts.","hist":"Atypical spindle cells and mitoses.","role":"less"},{"name":"Bacterial granuloma","supports":"Inflammatory nodules and ulceration may occur.","against":"Infection-associated suppurative inflammation and organisms, rather than cholesterol clefts, predominate.","hist":"Heterophilic granuloma and possible organisms.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s72-fhvskin","lecture":"72","title":"Cat with erosive facial dermatitis and corneal disease","species":"Adult cat with recent stress","history":"A cat develops asymmetric crusted facial ulcers around the medial canthus and nose after a stressful move. The cat also has conjunctivitis and corneal inflammation.","gross":"Ulcerative facial dermatitis, crusts and erosions centered around periocular and nasal regions, occasionally nasal planum.","histo":"Necrotizing ulcerative dermatitis with variable eosinophils; viral nuclear changes may be inconsistent. Definitive etiologic attribution needs clinicopathologic correlation.","best":"Feline herpesvirus-1 associated ulcerative dermatitis","morph":"Face: necrotizing ulcerative viral-associated dermatitis","mechanism":"FHV-1 recrudescence or infection injures facial/ocular epithelium; viral detection may indicate carriage, so positive PCR alone does not prove skin causation.","tests":"Skin biopsy and FHV-1 PCR/IHC on lesion tissue when indicated, ophthalmic assessment, and exclusion of fungal and eosinophilic disease.","ref":"Lecture 72 slide 19","system":"Skin","options":[{"name":"Feline herpesvirus-1 associated ulcerative dermatitis","supports":"The species, lesion distribution, microscopic pattern, and associated clinical findings most strongly support this diagnosis.","against":"Gross lesions alone are insufficient; targeted tests and exclusions remain necessary.","hist":"Necrotizing ulcerative dermatitis with variable eosinophils; viral nuclear changes may be inconsistent. Definitive etiologic attribution needs clinicopathologic correlation.","role":"best"},{"name":"Feline eosinophilic granuloma complex","supports":"Facial ulcers and eosinophilic infiltrates can overlap.","against":"Concurrent herpetic ocular/respiratory disease increases FHV suspicion; histology and viral lesion testing needed.","hist":"Eosinophilic collagen-degenerative dermatitis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Dermatophytosis","supports":"Periocular scale and crusts in cats possible.","against":"Hair invasion by dermatophyte rather than necrotizing ulcerative pattern.","hist":"Hyphae and arthroconidia in hair.","role":"less"},{"name":"Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma","supports":"Ulcerated nasal/face lesion can occur.","against":"More often progressive sun-exposed neoplasia with invasive squamous nests.","hist":"Infiltrative atypical squamous epithelial cells.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s72-grooming","lecture":"72","title":"Dog with painful dorsal pustules after grooming","species":"Adult dog","history":"Within two days of grooming and a bath, a dog becomes febrile and painful with multiple draining pustules and nodules along the dorsum. The grooming equipment or dilution water may have been contaminated.","gross":"Painful deep dorsal follicular nodules, pustules, crusts and draining tracts, sometimes systemic illness.","histo":"Ruptured follicles with neutrophilic and pyogranulomatous inflammation extending into dermis; bacterial rods may be found.","best":"Post-grooming bacterial furunculosis, often Pseudomonas aeruginosa","morph":"Skin: multifocal deep suppurative and pyogranulomatous furunculosis","mechanism":"Follicular trauma plus contaminated water/shampoo permits invasive opportunistic bacterial infection and deep follicular rupture.","tests":"Cytology and bacterial culture with susceptibility testing from deep lesions; assess systemic illness and obtain grooming exposure history.","ref":"Lecture 72 slide 33","system":"Skin","options":[{"name":"Post-grooming bacterial furunculosis, often Pseudomonas aeruginosa","supports":"The species, lesion distribution, microscopic pattern, and associated clinical findings most strongly support this diagnosis.","against":"Gross lesions alone are insufficient; targeted tests and exclusions remain necessary.","hist":"Ruptured follicles with neutrophilic and pyogranulomatous inflammation extending into dermis; bacterial rods may be found.","role":"best"},{"name":"Pyotraumatic dermatitis","supports":"Acute moist painful skin can follow self-trauma.","against":"Usually superficial surface erosions from self-trauma, not deeper follicular rupture and fever.","hist":"Superficial exudative dermatitis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Sterile eosinophilic furunculosis","supports":"Acute nodules/ulcers and follicular destruction can resemble infectious disease.","against":"Often muzzle after insect exposure with marked eosinophilia and no causal bacteria.","hist":"Eosinophilic follicular destruction.","role":"less"},{"name":"Dermatophilosis","supports":"Dorsal crusts and wet exposures overlap.","against":"Paintbrush exudative crusts with segmented filamentous bacteria rather than deep post-grooming follicular infection.","hist":"Superficial neutrophilic dermatitis with Dermatophilus filaments.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-onchocerca","lecture":"73","title":"Horse with ventral skin changes and ocular irritation","species":"Adult horse","history":"A horse exposed to biting midges develops ventral and neck pruritic lesions, patchy depigmentation and occasional ocular inflammation. Microfilariae are identified in skin snips.","gross":"Patchy scale, alopecia, erosions and pigment changes of ventral abdomen, neck and head; sometimes associated ocular lesions.","histo":"Perivascular to interstitial eosinophilic inflammation with occasional slender filarial microfilariae in dermis; chronic epidermal hyperplasia.","best":"Onchocerca cervicalis-associated dermatitis","morph":"Skin: filarial eosinophilic dermatitis","mechanism":"Adult worms occupy the nuchal ligament; microfilariae in skin and vector-associated inflammation produce dermatitis, with possible ocular involvement.","tests":"Appropriate skin snips and microscopy/PCR where available, ocular examination and differentiation from insect hypersensitivity.","ref":"Lecture 73 slide 22","system":"Skin","options":[{"name":"Onchocerca cervicalis-associated dermatitis","supports":"The species, lesion distribution, microscopic pattern, and associated clinical findings most strongly support this diagnosis.","against":"Gross lesions alone are insufficient; targeted tests and exclusions remain necessary.","hist":"Perivascular to interstitial eosinophilic inflammation with occasional slender filarial microfilariae in dermis; chronic epidermal hyperplasia.","role":"best"},{"name":"Culicoides hypersensitivity","supports":"Same vector exposure, pruritus and ventral lesions.","against":"Allergic histology is nonspecific; actual dermal microfilariae support Onchocerca infestation.","hist":"Eosinophilic perivascular dermatitis without parasites.","role":"less"},{"name":"Stephanofilaria dermatitis","supports":"Filarial dermatosis can cause ventral plaques.","against":"Primarily cattle/ruminants and horn-fly-associated sites, not nuchal-ligament adults in horses.","hist":"Filarial eosinophilic dermatitis with different organism.","role":"less"},{"name":"Chorioptic mange","supports":"Pruritic crusted equine skin can occur.","against":"Typically distal limbs with surface mites, rather than ventral microfilariae.","hist":"Superficial mites and epidermal hyperkeratosis.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s73-cpl","lecture":"73","title":"Draft horse with progressive thick folded lower limbs","species":"Adult feathered draft horse","history":"A draft horse develops increasingly swollen distal limbs with thick skin folds, nodules, ulcers and recurrent secondary infections. The process progresses over years despite intermittent treatment for pastern mites.","gross":"Bilateral progressive distal limb enlargement, deep skin folds, fibrosis, verrucous nodules and secondary crusting.","histo":"Marked dermal fibrosis and chronic lymphatic dilation/remodeling with inflammation, epidermal hyperplasia and secondary infection.","best":"Chronic progressive lymphedema of draft horses","morph":"Distal limb: chronic fibrosing lymphedematous dermatitis","mechanism":"Inherited and environmental contributors impair lymphatic drainage and skin elasticity; chronic edema fosters fibrosis, hyperkeratosis and secondary microbial/parasite complications.","tests":"Clinical distribution and breed, exclude other limb edema/obstructive conditions, examine/treat secondary Chorioptes and infections.","ref":"Lecture 73 slide 39","system":"Skin","options":[{"name":"Chronic progressive lymphedema of draft horses","supports":"The species, lesion distribution, microscopic pattern, and associated clinical findings most strongly support this diagnosis.","against":"Gross lesions alone are insufficient; targeted tests and exclusions remain necessary.","hist":"Marked dermal fibrosis and chronic lymphatic dilation/remodeling with inflammation, epidermal hyperplasia and secondary infection.","role":"best"},{"name":"Chorioptic mange alone","supports":"Feathered legs with mite-associated pruritic crusts.","against":"Mites may coexist but do not fully account for progressive fibrosing limb enlargement and deep folds.","hist":"Superficial mite-associated dermatitis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Bacterial cellulitis","supports":"Painful swelling and skin fibrosis may follow infection.","against":"Usually more acute/asymmetric; long-standing bilateral fibrotic progression points to lymphedema.","hist":"Neutrophilic subcutaneous inflammation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Equine pastern dermatitis","supports":"Crusting and hyperkeratosis are common.","against":"This is a reaction syndrome, not necessarily the primary progressive lymphatic disorder.","hist":"Variable dermatitis depending underlying etiology.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s74-vkh","lecture":"74","title":"Akita with uveitis followed by facial depigmentation","species":"Young adult Akita","history":"A dog experiences bilateral painful uveitis followed weeks later by symmetrical depigmentation of the muzzle, lips and eyelids and white hair in affected areas.","gross":"Bilateral ocular inflammation with later symmetric leukoderma and leukotrichia at periocular, muzzle and lip sites.","histo":"Lymphohistiocytic interface/periadnexal dermatitis and melanocyte injury with pigmentary incontinence; ocular tissues show uveitis.","best":"Uveodermatologic (VKH-like) syndrome","morph":"Skin: melanocyte-targeting lymphohistiocytic dermatitis with concurrent bilateral uveitis","mechanism":"Immune-mediated melanocyte destruction involves ocular uveal melanocytes and skin, often ocular signs before cutaneous changes.","tests":"Urgent ophthalmic assessment, skin biopsy at active margin and exclusion of infectious uveitis or vitiligo alone.","ref":"Lecture 74 slide 21","system":"Skin","options":[{"name":"Uveodermatologic (VKH-like) syndrome","supports":"The species, lesion distribution, microscopic pattern, and associated clinical findings most strongly support this diagnosis.","against":"Gross lesions alone are insufficient; targeted tests and exclusions remain necessary.","hist":"Lymphohistiocytic interface/periadnexal dermatitis and melanocyte injury with pigmentary incontinence; ocular tissues show uveitis.","role":"best"},{"name":"Vitiligo","supports":"Can cause symmetric leukoderma without primary itch.","against":"Does not itself cause severe bilateral uveitis.","hist":"Loss of epidermal melanocytes with limited inflammation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Discoid lupus erythematosus","supports":"Nasal depigmentation and interface dermatitis occur.","against":"Typically localized nasal architectural loss and photosensitivity, rather than initial bilateral uveitis.","hist":"Lymphocytic interface dermatitis of nasal planum.","role":"less"},{"name":"Systemic lupus erythematosus","supports":"Multisystem immune disease can cause skin and ocular findings.","against":"Look for nephritis, joint disease and cytopenias; melanocyte-specific pattern more suggestive of uveodermatologic syndrome.","hist":"Variable vasculitis/interface dermatitis and immune-complex pathology.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"s74-nodular-renal","lecture":"74","title":"German shepherd with many firm skin nodules and renal masses","species":"Middle-aged German shepherd dog","history":"A German shepherd has numerous firm cutaneous nodules over limbs and trunk; abdominal imaging identifies bilateral multicystic renal masses.","gross":"Multiple firm dermal nodules with associated renal cystadenomatous or carcinomatous masses.","histo":"Dermal densely collagenous fibrous nodules; renal lesions display epithelial cysts with possible malignant features.","best":"Hereditary renal cystadenocarcinoma and nodular dermatofibrosis syndrome","morph":"Skin: multifocal nodular dermatofibrosis associated with renal cystic neoplasia","mechanism":"Inherited FLCN-associated syndrome links renal epithelial neoplasia and paraneoplastic-like dermal fibrous nodules.","tests":"Skin and renal tissue histopathology, renal imaging, pedigree review and genetic investigation where available.","ref":"Lecture 74 slide 46","system":"Skin","options":[{"name":"Hereditary renal cystadenocarcinoma and nodular dermatofibrosis syndrome","supports":"The species, lesion distribution, microscopic pattern, and associated clinical findings most strongly support this diagnosis.","against":"Gross lesions alone are insufficient; targeted tests and exclusions remain necessary.","hist":"Dermal densely collagenous fibrous nodules; renal lesions display epithelial cysts with possible malignant features.","role":"best"},{"name":"Cutaneous fibrosarcoma","supports":"Firm fibrous skin nodules may resemble one lesion.","against":"Usually an individual malignant fibrous mass, not dozens of nodules with bilateral renal cystic tumors.","hist":"Atypical invasive spindle-cell neoplasm.","role":"less"},{"name":"Multiple histiocytomas","supports":"Dogs may have multiple raised skin masses.","against":"Histiocytic round cells differ from mature fibrous dermal nodules.","hist":"Round-cell histiocytic proliferations.","role":"less"},{"name":"Generalized cutaneous xanthomatosis","supports":"Multiple skin nodules possible.","against":"Foamy macrophages and lipid disorders rather than fibrous nodules with renal tumors.","hist":"Foamy histiocytic lipid accumulations.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r75-seminoma","lecture":"75","system":"Male repro","title":"Cryptorchid dog with bulging pale testicular mass","species":"Older cryptorchid male dog","history":"An older dog with an abdominally retained testis has a pale soft solid testicular mass that bulges when the tunica is cut. No feminization or symmetric alopecia is evident.","gross":"Solid soft pale gray-white testicular enlargement, bulging cut surface, unlike small yellow greasy interstitial nodules.","histo":"Sheets or lobules of large uniform-to-pleomorphic germ cells with vesicular nuclei and prominent nucleoli, divided by delicate fibrous septa, often with lymphocytes.","best":"Testicular seminoma","morph":"Testis: germ-cell neoplasm (seminoma)","mechanism":"Neoplastic germ cells expand testicular parenchyma. Cryptorchidism predisposes to certain testicular neoplasms and retained heat impairs spermatogenesis.","tests":"Testicular histopathology and inspection of both testes; evaluate regional nodes if malignant behavior suspected.","ref":"Lecture 75 slides 25–26","options":[{"name":"Testicular seminoma","supports":"Species, gross distribution and microscopic pattern collectively provide the strongest diagnosis.","against":"Definitive etiology and behavior require appropriate sampling and confirmatory testing.","hist":"Sheets or lobules of large uniform-to-pleomorphic germ cells with vesicular nuclei and prominent nucleoli, divided by delicate fibrous septa, often with lymphocytes.","role":"best"},{"name":"Sertoli cell tumor","supports":"Can arise in a cryptorchid testis and expand it.","against":"Endocrine feminization and tubular neoplastic Sertoli cell pattern favor this tumor.","hist":"Palisading Sertoli-like cells arranged in tubules/cords.","role":"less"},{"name":"Leydig interstitial-cell tumor","supports":"Older dogs may have circumscribed testicular nodules.","against":"Often smaller soft yellow greasy nodule rather than bulging white germ-cell mass.","hist":"Polyhedral eosinophilic/vacuolated interstitial cells.","role":"less"},{"name":"Testicular lymphoma","supports":"Pale soft infiltrative testicular mass can mimic seminoma.","against":"Lymphoid cell phenotype and infiltrative diffuse architecture distinguish lymphoma.","hist":"Sheets of atypical lymphocytes replacing tissue.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r76-feline-hyperplasia","lecture":"76","system":"Mammary","title":"Young queen with sudden dramatic enlargement of several mammary glands","species":"Young intact queen after progestin exposure","history":"An intact female cat develops striking bilateral mammary enlargement over weeks after exposure to progesterone-like hormones. Multiple glands are firm but not infiltrative; the skin may ulcerate due to tension.","gross":"Rapid dramatic often multicentric firm diffuse mammary enlargement, sometimes ulcerated from stretch.","histo":"Marked non-neoplastic proliferation of mammary ducts and stromal tissues, with relatively orderly epithelial/myoepithelial structures.","best":"Feline mammary fibroadenomatous hyperplasia","morph":"Mammary glands: diffuse progesterone-associated fibroadenomatous hyperplasia","mechanism":"Progesterone-related mammary growth hormone/IGF-1 signaling drives rapid exaggerated lobular ductal and stromal proliferation, not primary carcinoma.","tests":"Reproductive and drug history, biopsy when atypical, assess pregnancy status; differentiate from inflammatory mammary disease and malignancy.","ref":"Lecture 76 slide 18","options":[{"name":"Feline mammary fibroadenomatous hyperplasia","supports":"Species, gross distribution and microscopic pattern collectively provide the strongest diagnosis.","against":"Definitive etiology and behavior require appropriate sampling and confirmatory testing.","hist":"Marked non-neoplastic proliferation of mammary ducts and stromal tissues, with relatively orderly epithelial/myoepithelial structures.","role":"best"},{"name":"Feline mammary carcinoma","supports":"Mammary masses and ulceration possible.","against":"Usually discrete invasive masses in older cats rather than synchronous hormone-related rapid multi-gland enlargement.","hist":"Invasive atypical mammary epithelial nests/tubules.","role":"less"},{"name":"Acute mastitis","supports":"Painful enlarged mammary glands and skin changes may occur.","against":"Fever, abnormal milk and neutrophilic exudation favor mastitis over orderly proliferative hyperplasia.","hist":"Acute neutrophilic mastitis with possible bacteria.","role":"less"},{"name":"Mammary fibroadenoma","supports":"Benign glandular/stromal proliferation shares some morphology.","against":"Often a discrete neoplastic mass, whereas this is rapid hormone-responsive diffuse hyperplasia.","hist":"Circumscribed mammary gland/stroma proliferation.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r77-brucella-bovine","lecture":"77","system":"Abortion","title":"Late bovine abortion with fibrinous placenta and fetal pneumonia","species":"Late-gestation bovine fetus","history":"Several pregnant cattle abort in late gestation; the placenta has thickened exudative intercotyledonary membranes and partially necrotic cotyledons. A fetal lung section shows severe inflammatory consolidation.","gross":"Tan-yellow mucoid to fibrinous intercotyledonary placentitis with necrotic cotyledons, fetal edema and pneumonia; lesions overlap other infectious abortions.","histo":"Necrosuppurative placentitis with mixed inflammatory response and fetal bronchopneumonia; organisms may be visible but cannot reliably be speciated by H&E.","best":"Brucella abortus-associated bovine abortion","morph":"Placenta: severe necrosuppurative placentitis; fetus: suppurative bronchopneumonia","mechanism":"Brucella localizes to gravid uterine and placental tissues and disrupts placental exchange with fetal infection; abortion tissues carry major zoonotic and herd risks.","tests":"Strict biosecurity and official reporting as required; laboratory-guided placental/fetal specimens, Brucella-specific PCR/culture/serology under authorized conditions. Do not culture suspect agent in an ordinary teaching lab.","ref":"Lecture 77 catch-up PPT slides 32–33 speaker notes","options":[{"name":"Brucella abortus-associated bovine abortion","supports":"Species, gross distribution and microscopic pattern collectively provide the strongest diagnosis.","against":"Definitive etiology and behavior require appropriate sampling and confirmatory testing.","hist":"Necrosuppurative placentitis with mixed inflammatory response and fetal bronchopneumonia; organisms may be visible but cannot reliably be speciated by H&E.","role":"best"},{"name":"Coxiella burnetii","supports":"Necrotizing placentitis, abortion clusters and zoonotic hazards overlap.","against":"Coxiella often has very high intracellular trophoblast bacterial load; specific PCR/IHC required.","hist":"Trophoblast-rich necrotizing placentitis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Chlamydia abortus","supports":"Severe late placentitis possible in ruminants.","against":"More typical sheep/goats; necrotizing placentitis still overlaps histologically.","hist":"Necrotizing trophoblast inflammation with intracellular organisms.","role":"less"},{"name":"Bovine mycotic abortion","supports":"Thick leathery membranes and fetal pneumonia/skin lesions possible.","against":"Fungal hyphae and fetal mycotic dermatitis versus bacterial Brucella process.","hist":"Necrotizing fungal placentitis with tissue-invasive hyphae.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"e78-glaucoma","lecture":"78","system":"Eye","title":"Dog with enlarged painful globe and optic-disc cupping","species":"Adult dog","history":"A dog develops ocular pain, corneal edema and progressively enlarged globe. Tonometry demonstrates sustained elevated intraocular pressure; the retinal nerve fiber layer and optic disc are damaged.","gross":"Buphthalmos with corneal edema, angle changes, possible lens displacement; chronic optic-disc cupping and retinal atrophy.","histo":"Optic nerve head axonal loss/cupping, retinal ganglion degeneration, iridocorneal angle remodeling and possible ciliary body atrophy.","best":"Chronic glaucoma","morph":"Eye: chronic pressure-associated retinal and optic-nerve degeneration","mechanism":"Aqueous outflow impairment raises IOP, injures retinal ganglion axons and stretches ocular tissues. Decide primary versus secondary only after identifying an acquired cause.","tests":"Tonometry, gonioscopy/angle assessment and complete ophthalmic evaluation for uveitis, neoplasia or lens luxation.","ref":"Lecture 78 slides 18–22","options":[{"name":"Chronic glaucoma","supports":"Species, gross distribution and microscopic pattern collectively provide the strongest diagnosis.","against":"Definitive etiology and behavior require appropriate sampling and confirmatory testing.","hist":"Optic nerve head axonal loss/cupping, retinal ganglion degeneration, iridocorneal angle remodeling and possible ciliary body atrophy.","role":"best"},{"name":"Retrobulbar mass with exophthalmos","supports":"Eye can appear protruding and red.","against":"Exophthalmos displaces the globe without necessarily enlarging it; retropulsion and imaging distinguish.","hist":"Retrobulbar mass/inflammation rather than globe expansion.","role":"less"},{"name":"Anterior uveitis","supports":"Pain, redness and corneal edema overlap.","against":"Often low intraocular pressure early and miosis; secondary glaucoma can develop later.","hist":"Uveal inflammatory cells with possible synechiae.","role":"less"},{"name":"Lens luxation without glaucoma","supports":"Pain and lens displacement can occur.","against":"Luxated lens can cause secondary glaucoma; elevated IOP and optic nerve damage indicate glaucoma as current syndrome.","hist":"Displaced lens with or without secondary angle obstruction.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"e78-ibk","lecture":"78","system":"Eye","title":"Summer cattle outbreak of painful corneal ulcers","species":"Pastured calves in summer","history":"Multiple calves develop tearing, blepharospasm and corneal opacity. Face flies are abundant; some ulcers become deep, vascularized or perforate.","gross":"Suppurative conjunctivitis, corneal edema and central ulceration, occasionally perforation.","histo":"Ulcerative neutrophilic keratoconjunctivitis, corneal stromal edema and possible intralesional Gram-negative coccobacilli.","best":"Infectious bovine keratoconjunctivitis associated with Moraxella bovis","morph":"Eye: acute ulcerative suppurative keratoconjunctivitis","mechanism":"Moraxella-associated ocular surface injury plus UV, fly transmission and other cofactors produce corneal epithelial damage and rapidly progressive ulceration.","tests":"Ocular examination and careful culture/PCR interpretation with lesion context; assess Moraxella species, UV exposure and herd fly pressure.","ref":"Lecture 78 slide 33","options":[{"name":"Infectious bovine keratoconjunctivitis associated with Moraxella bovis","supports":"Species, gross distribution and microscopic pattern collectively provide the strongest diagnosis.","against":"Definitive etiology and behavior require appropriate sampling and confirmatory testing.","hist":"Ulcerative neutrophilic keratoconjunctivitis, corneal stromal edema and possible intralesional Gram-negative coccobacilli.","role":"best"},{"name":"Bovine infectious keratoconjunctivitis due to Moraxella bovoculi or other coinfections","supports":"Ocular discharge and ulcers may overlap.","against":"Detection alone does not always establish causality; species-specific assay plus lesion epidemiology required.","hist":"Similar ulcerative keratoconjunctivitis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Traumatic corneal ulcer","supports":"Mechanical injury causes unilateral painful corneal defects.","against":"Herd-wide outbreak with face-fly pressure favors infectious etiology.","hist":"Corneal epithelial loss and secondary neutrophilic inflammation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Infectious bovine rhinotracheitis","supports":"Can produce conjunctivitis and ocular inflammation.","against":"Respiratory signs and lack of classic severe corneal ulcers favor IBR, though mixed disease exists.","hist":"Conjunctival necrosis/inflammation with herpesviral infection.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"ear79-ceruminous","lecture":"79","system":"Ear","title":"Older cat with invasive external ear canal glandular mass","species":"Older cat","history":"An older cat has chronic unilateral otitis, canal obstruction and a firm irregular mass arising in the external ear canal. Histology demonstrates atypical ceruminous epithelial tubules invading local stroma.","gross":"Irregular canal-occluding mass with possible ulceration and invasion into surrounding ear canal tissue.","histo":"Atypical ceruminous apocrine epithelial tubules, nests or solid areas with stromal invasion, mitoses and variable luminal secretion.","best":"Ceruminous gland adenocarcinoma","morph":"External ear: infiltrative ceruminous gland carcinoma","mechanism":"Malignant glandular epithelial proliferation obstructs and invades canal tissues, leading to secondary otitis and possible nodal or distant metastasis.","tests":"Histopathology, imaging of bulla if invasion suspected and regional lymph node/systemic staging.","ref":"Lecture 79 slides 15–16","options":[{"name":"Ceruminous gland adenocarcinoma","supports":"Species, gross distribution and microscopic pattern collectively provide the strongest diagnosis.","against":"Definitive etiology and behavior require appropriate sampling and confirmatory testing.","hist":"Atypical ceruminous apocrine epithelial tubules, nests or solid areas with stromal invasion, mitoses and variable luminal secretion.","role":"best"},{"name":"Ceruminous gland adenoma","supports":"Benign glandular tumor may obstruct the canal.","against":"No stromal invasion and limited cytologic atypia favor adenoma.","hist":"Well-differentiated noninfiltrative ceruminous glands.","role":"less"},{"name":"Feline inflammatory polyp","supports":"May cause canal obstruction and unilateral otitis.","against":"Typically younger cats with a pedunculated fibrovascular inflamed polyp connected to middle ear.","hist":"Inflamed fibrovascular stroma and benign epithelial covering.","role":"less"},{"name":"Aural cholesteatoma","supports":"Can cause aggressive middle ear mass and bony destruction.","against":"Keratin-filled squamous-lined cyst rather than invasive apocrine tubules.","hist":"Laminated keratin and squamous epithelial cyst lining.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r75-leydig","lecture":"75","system":"Male repro","title":"Old dog with a tiny greasy yellow testicular nodule","species":"Older intact male dog","history":"A small soft well-demarcated yellow nodule is discovered in the testis at necropsy, without signs of feminization.","gross":"Small yellow greasy expansile nodule, sometimes multiple; normal tubules displaced at margin.","histo":"Well-differentiated polygonal interstitial endocrine cells with eosinophilic/vacuolated cytoplasm in expansile sheets or cords.","best":"Leydig (interstitial) cell tumor","morph":"Testis: circumscribed interstitial endocrine-cell neoplasm","mechanism":"Proliferation of Leydig cells produces a typically slow expansile nodule; tumor origin does not necessarily imply a clinically active androgen or estrogen syndrome.","tests":"Histopathology to distinguish endocrine from germ or Sertoli lineage; assess concurrent testicular lesions.","ref":"L75 slides 23,26","options":[{"name":"Leydig (interstitial) cell tumor","supports":"Species, signalment, lesion morphology and microscopic pattern provide the most consistent diagnosis.","against":"The diagnosis is not verified by gross appearance alone; use the specified confirmatory test and consider competing causes.","hist":"Well-differentiated polygonal interstitial endocrine cells with eosinophilic/vacuolated cytoplasm in expansile sheets or cords.","role":"best"},{"name":"Seminoma","supports":"Common in older dogs; solid testicular growth.","against":"Usually pale gray-white bulging germ-cell mass rather than small yellow greasy nodule.","hist":"Large neoplastic germ cells in lobules, often lymphocytes.","role":"less"},{"name":"Sertoli cell tumor","supports":"Testicular mass in older or cryptorchid dog.","against":"Firm pale fibrous mass and tubular/palisading cell pattern; feminization can occur.","hist":"Sertoli-type tubules and cords in fibrous stroma.","role":"less"},{"name":"Focal testicular degeneration","supports":"Can cause altered testicular consistency and color.","against":"Atrophic/fibrotic tubules, not organized expansile Leydig-cell sheets.","hist":"Seminiferous tubular atrophy and fibrosis.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r75-bph","lecture":"75","system":"Male repro","title":"Intact dog with symmetric enlarged prostate and tenesmus","species":"Older intact male dog","history":"An older intact male dog has ribbonlike stools and tenesmus; the prostate is bilaterally enlarged and nonpainful with small cysts.","gross":"Symmetric prostatic enlargement with nodular glandular hyperplasia and occasional cyst formation.","histo":"Proliferative glands with tall epithelial lining and variable stromal hyperplasia, without invasive atypia.","best":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia","morph":"Prostate: diffuse to nodular benign glandular-stromal hyperplasia","mechanism":"Androgen-dependent prostate growth with age enlarges the gland, compressing rectum and occasionally predisposing to cystic secretion retention.","tests":"Rectal examination and imaging; evaluate urine/prostatic specimens if inflammation suspected; biopsy when neoplasia is a concern.","ref":"L75 slide 30","options":[{"name":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia","supports":"Species, signalment, lesion morphology and microscopic pattern provide the most consistent diagnosis.","against":"The diagnosis is not verified by gross appearance alone; use the specified confirmatory test and consider competing causes.","hist":"Proliferative glands with tall epithelial lining and variable stromal hyperplasia, without invasive atypia.","role":"best"},{"name":"Prostatic carcinoma","supports":"Enlarged prostate may cause tenesmus and urinary obstruction.","against":"Often irregular/asymmetric and invasive with cytologic atypia, although gross overlap exists.","hist":"Invasive atypical epithelial glands/nests with desmoplasia.","role":"less"},{"name":"Bacterial prostatitis","supports":"Prostatic enlargement and lower urinary signs can occur.","against":"Pain, fever and suppurative cytology/culture favor infection.","hist":"Suppurative inflammatory infiltrates and gland destruction.","role":"less"},{"name":"Prostatic abscess","supports":"Focal cavity and systemic illness may cause palpable enlargement.","against":"Pus-filled cavity rather than symmetric hyperplastic acini.","hist":"Necrosuppurative cavitating inflammation.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r75-bcanis","lecture":"75","system":"Male repro","title":"Stud dog with unilateral epididymitis and scrotal licking","species":"Adult intact male dog","history":"A breeding male dog develops enlarged tender epididymal tissue, infertility, chronic scrotal dermatitis and progressive ipsilateral testicular shrinkage.","gross":"Enlarged inflamed epididymis with atrophic testis and reactive scrotal skin; possible orchitis or prostatitis.","histo":"Mixed chronic-active epididymitis with sperm extravasation granulomas, fibrosis and adjacent seminiferous tubular loss.","best":"Brucella canis-associated epididymitis/orchitis","morph":"Epididymis: chronic-active epididymitis with secondary testicular degeneration","mechanism":"Brucella infection targets reproductive tissue, causing inflammatory obstruction and sperm leakage; bacteremia and venereal/abortion-associated spread pose zoonotic risk.","tests":"Appropriate serology plus confirmatory PCR/culture through qualified lab, herd/kennel investigation and zoonotic precautions.","ref":"L75 slide 21","options":[{"name":"Brucella canis-associated epididymitis/orchitis","supports":"Species, signalment, lesion morphology and microscopic pattern provide the most consistent diagnosis.","against":"The diagnosis is not verified by gross appearance alone; use the specified confirmatory test and consider competing causes.","hist":"Mixed chronic-active epididymitis with sperm extravasation granulomas, fibrosis and adjacent seminiferous tubular loss.","role":"best"},{"name":"Escherichia coli ascending epididymitis","supports":"Bacterial reproductive infection can cause pain/swelling.","against":"Urinary tract source and culture rather than Brucella-specific diagnostics.","hist":"Suppurative epididymitis with bacterial colonies.","role":"less"},{"name":"Testicular torsion","supports":"Pain, edema and secondary atrophy possible.","against":"Acute hemorrhagic infarction of testis rather than chronic epididymal inflammation.","hist":"Diffuse coagulative necrosis and hemorrhage.","role":"less"},{"name":"Sperm granuloma after obstructive injury","supports":"Inflamed epididymal mass and sperm leakage possible.","against":"A localized lesion may lack infectious evidence and chronic systemic reproductive infection.","hist":"Sperm-centered foreign-body giant-cell reaction.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r76-follicular-cyst","lecture":"76","system":"Female repro","title":"Cow with persistent estrus and anovulatory ovarian cyst","species":"Adult dairy cow","history":"A cow has repeat estrous behavior and no palpable corpus luteum. An ovarian structure has a thin wall and fluid-filled lumen persisting beyond normal follicular lifespan.","gross":"Large thin-walled fluid-filled ovarian cyst; no robust luteal wall.","histo":"Follicle-like cyst lined by flattened granulosa/theca cells; little to no luteinization.","best":"Follicular ovarian cyst","morph":"Ovary: persistent anovulatory follicular cyst","mechanism":"Disordered hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian feedback and failure of ovulation permit a follicle to persist; estrogenic effects can alter estrous behavior.","tests":"Serial ultrasound, progesterone testing and clinical reproductive timeline; differentiation from a luteal cyst is functional as well as structural.","ref":"L76 slides 7–12","options":[{"name":"Follicular ovarian cyst","supports":"Species, signalment, lesion morphology and microscopic pattern provide the most consistent diagnosis.","against":"The diagnosis is not verified by gross appearance alone; use the specified confirmatory test and consider competing causes.","hist":"Follicle-like cyst lined by flattened granulosa/theca cells; little to no luteinization.","role":"best"},{"name":"Luteal cyst","supports":"Fluid-filled ovarian structure with reproductive dysfunction.","against":"Thicker luteinized wall and progesterone effect may be detected.","hist":"Luteinized granulosa/theca wall.","role":"less"},{"name":"Granulosa-theca cell tumor","supports":"Persistent estrus and ovarian enlargement can occur.","against":"Solid or multicystic neoplastic ovarian mass with hormonal abnormalities and inhibin/AMH support, not one simple follicular cyst.","hist":"Neoplastic granulosa/theca cells, sometimes Call–Exner bodies.","role":"less"},{"name":"Normal preovulatory follicle","supports":"Thin-walled fluid-filled ovarian structure is physiologic.","against":"Transient cycle stage rather than a persistent anovulatory cyst.","hist":"Normal orderly follicular wall.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r76-metritis","lecture":"76","system":"Female repro","title":"Postpartum cow with fetid enlarged uterus and fever","species":"Recently calved dairy cow","history":"A cow three days postpartum is febrile and toxemic with fetid reddish-brown uterine discharge after retained membranes.","gross":"Markedly enlarged flaccid edematous uterus, foul exudate, friable congested wall and variable serosal fibrin.","histo":"Acute neutrophilic and necrotizing inflammation extends through multiple uterine wall layers; bacterial proliferation may be abundant.","best":"Acute puerperal metritis","morph":"Uterus: severe diffuse necrosuppurative transmural metritis","mechanism":"Ascending bacterial contamination after calving, retained placenta and tissue damage allow deep uterine infection; endotoxin drives systemic illness.","tests":"Postpartum history, physical examination and assessment for systemic illness; differentiate from endometritis limited to mucosa and closed-cervix pyometra.","ref":"L76 slide 21","options":[{"name":"Acute puerperal metritis","supports":"Species, signalment, lesion morphology and microscopic pattern provide the most consistent diagnosis.","against":"The diagnosis is not verified by gross appearance alone; use the specified confirmatory test and consider competing causes.","hist":"Acute neutrophilic and necrotizing inflammation extends through multiple uterine wall layers; bacterial proliferation may be abundant.","role":"best"},{"name":"Endometritis","supports":"Postpartum infertility and uterine inflammation can overlap.","against":"Typically restricted primarily to endometrium with less severe systemic toxemia.","hist":"Superficial endometrial neutrophilic inflammation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Pyometra","supports":"Pus accumulates in uterine lumen.","against":"Often prolonged retained purulent contents with a functional corpus luteum in cows rather than acute severe puerperal wall involvement.","hist":"Luminal neutrophilic pus and chronic endometrial changes.","role":"less"},{"name":"Uterine rupture with peritonitis","supports":"Postpartum toxemia and fibrinous serosa can occur.","against":"Visible wall defect and peritoneal contamination distinguish traumatic rupture.","hist":"Full-thickness tearing and fibrinopurulent serositis.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r76-feline-mammary-ca","lecture":"76","system":"Mammary","title":"Cat with firm ulcerated mammary nodule and enlarged nodes","species":"Older female cat","history":"A cat develops a fast-growing firm mammary mass with ulceration and palpable regional nodes, contrasting with multicentric hormonally stimulated enlargement in young queens.","gross":"Firm invasive poorly mobile mammary nodule, ulceration and possible regional lymphatic spread.","histo":"Atypical malignant mammary epithelial tubules/solid nests invading stroma, frequent mitoses and potentially lymphovascular invasion.","best":"Feline mammary carcinoma","morph":"Mammary gland: invasive epithelial carcinoma","mechanism":"Malignant mammary epithelial proliferation invades local tissues and can spread via lymphatics; feline mammary masses have a high probability of malignancy.","tests":"Excisional histopathology with margins, regional-node evaluation and thoracic staging.","ref":"L76 slide 46","options":[{"name":"Feline mammary carcinoma","supports":"Species, signalment, lesion morphology and microscopic pattern provide the most consistent diagnosis.","against":"The diagnosis is not verified by gross appearance alone; use the specified confirmatory test and consider competing causes.","hist":"Atypical malignant mammary epithelial tubules/solid nests invading stroma, frequent mitoses and potentially lymphovascular invasion.","role":"best"},{"name":"Fibroadenomatous mammary hyperplasia","supports":"Rapid mammary enlargement possible.","against":"Younger intact/progestin-exposed queen with diffuse multiple gland enlargement and orderly noninvasive tissue.","hist":"Orderly fibroadenomatous proliferation without destructive invasion.","role":"less"},{"name":"Mastitis","supports":"Inflamed painful mammary tissue and ulceration can occur.","against":"Systemic infection and neutrophilic exudate rather than invasive neoplastic epithelium.","hist":"Suppurative mammary inflammation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Benign mammary adenoma","supports":"Well-circumscribed glandular mass can occur.","against":"Lacks stromal and vascular invasion and marked atypia.","hist":"Noninvasive glandular epithelial proliferation.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r76-mycoplasma-mastitis","lecture":"76","system":"Mammary","title":"Dairy herd with contagious agalactia and recurrent mastitis","species":"Lactating dairy cow","history":"Multiple cows develop refractory mastitis, sharp reduction in milk production and abnormal secretions; multiple quarters may be involved despite standard antibiotic approaches.","gross":"Variable glandular swelling and watery or flocculent milk; chronic cases may have mammary fibrosis.","histo":"Variable chronic-active interstitial and intralobular mastitis, glandular loss and fibrosis; routine histology is not etiologically specific.","best":"Mycoplasma-associated bovine mastitis","morph":"Mammary gland: chronic-active mastitis with secretory loss","mechanism":"Contagious milking-associated Mycoplasma infection and difficult organism clearance can cause long-term gland injury and agalactia.","tests":"Aseptic milk collection for targeted Mycoplasma culture/PCR and herd-level transmission control; do not identify Mycoplasma by H&E pattern alone.","ref":"L76 slide 42","options":[{"name":"Mycoplasma-associated bovine mastitis","supports":"Species, signalment, lesion morphology and microscopic pattern provide the most consistent diagnosis.","against":"The diagnosis is not verified by gross appearance alone; use the specified confirmatory test and consider competing causes.","hist":"Variable chronic-active interstitial and intralobular mastitis, glandular loss and fibrosis; routine histology is not etiologically specific.","role":"best"},{"name":"Staphylococcus aureus mastitis","supports":"Contagious recurrent and chronic mastitis overlaps.","against":"Culture distinguishes; Staph can form microabscesses and fibrotic foci.","hist":"Suppurative multifocal abscessing mastitis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Coliform E. coli mastitis","supports":"Milk abnormality and severe mastitis possible.","against":"Often abrupt endotoxemic severe mastitis rather than multi-cow chronic refractory agalactia.","hist":"Acute necrosuppurative mastitis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Streptococcal mastitis","supports":"Contagious mastitis and elevated somatic cells possible.","against":"Definitive microbiology distinguishes organism; lesions are not specific.","hist":"Neutrophilic ductal and lobular mastitis.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r77-hydrallantois","lecture":"77","system":"Pregnancy","title":"Cow with rapidly enlarging abdomen and watery placental fluid","species":"Late-pregnant cow","history":"During late pregnancy the dam's abdomen distends rapidly and fluid sampling identifies large volumes of watery allantoic fluid; fetus may be compromised.","gross":"Marked expansion of allantoic sac with large watery volume and altered placentomal attachments.","histo":"Placental structural changes vary; no specific inflammatory agent or pathognomonic histology required.","best":"Hydroallantois","morph":"Pregnancy: excessive allantoic fluid with placental dysfunction","mechanism":"Disordered placental exchange and allantoic fluid homeostasis create severe distention; unlike hydroamnios, the excess watery fluid is in the allantoic compartment.","tests":"Ultrasonography and fluid/placental compartment recognition; differentiate from multiple pregnancy, ascites and hydroamnios.","ref":"L77 slide 21 speaker notes","options":[{"name":"Hydroallantois","supports":"Species, signalment, lesion morphology and microscopic pattern provide the most consistent diagnosis.","against":"The diagnosis is not verified by gross appearance alone; use the specified confirmatory test and consider competing causes.","hist":"Placental structural changes vary; no specific inflammatory agent or pathognomonic histology required.","role":"best"},{"name":"Hydroamnios","supports":"Severe abdominal distention in pregnancy can occur.","against":"Excess viscous amniotic fluid, often related to impaired fetal swallowing or defects.","hist":"No unique histology; identifies fluid compartment.","role":"less"},{"name":"Maternal ascites","supports":"Massive abdominal distention is possible.","against":"Fluid is free in peritoneal cavity, not within fetal membranes.","hist":"Dependent serosal changes depending cause.","role":"less"},{"name":"Twins / multiple gestation","supports":"Abdominal enlargement can be marked.","against":"Additional fetuses and placental distribution visible on imaging.","hist":"Normal fetal membranes with more than one fetus.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r77-lepto","lecture":"77","system":"Abortion","title":"Late-gestation equine abortion with icteric fetus","species":"Pregnant mare","history":"A mare aborts late in gestation after possible exposure to contaminated water and wildlife urine. The fetus has icterus and hepatomegaly; placental edema and hemorrhage are variable.","gross":"Icteric fetus, yellow-mottled enlarged liver, hemorrhagic foci and possibly edematous placenta; gross lesions may be absent.","histo":"Nonspecific fetal hepatitis, nephritis and placentitis, sometimes spirochetes detected by specialized methods; H&E does not reliably identify species.","best":"Leptospira-associated abortion","morph":"Fetus and placenta: abortion with hepatic/renal inflammatory injury suggestive of leptospirosis","mechanism":"Hematogenous transplacental dissemination of zoonotic leptospires can infect fetus and placenta and disrupt viability.","tests":"Fetal/placental PCR and appropriate paired serology/MAT with laboratory guidance; PPE for abortion materials.","ref":"L77 slides 39–40","options":[{"name":"Leptospira-associated abortion","supports":"Species, signalment, lesion morphology and microscopic pattern provide the most consistent diagnosis.","against":"The diagnosis is not verified by gross appearance alone; use the specified confirmatory test and consider competing causes.","hist":"Nonspecific fetal hepatitis, nephritis and placentitis, sometimes spirochetes detected by specialized methods; H&E does not reliably identify species.","role":"best"},{"name":"Equine herpesvirus 1 abortion","supports":"Late-term abortion and fetal liver lesions possible.","against":"Classic fetal pulmonary edema/inclusions and EHV PCR distinguish infection.","hist":"Fetal multifocal hepatic necrosis with herpesviral intranuclear inclusions in suitable lesions.","role":"less"},{"name":"Ascending bacterial placentitis","supports":"Late abortion with placental inflammation possible.","against":"Cervical star ascending distribution and culture rather than icteric leptospiral context.","hist":"Suppurative placentitis concentrated near cervical star.","role":"less"},{"name":"Equine twin-related placental insufficiency","supports":"Noninfectious late abortion may occur.","against":"Avillous apposition zones and two fetuses; no etiologic infectious evidence.","hist":"Placental insufficiency without pathogen-specific lesions.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"r77-fescue","lecture":"77","system":"Pregnancy","title":"Mare with prolonged pregnancy and agalactia on fescue pasture","species":"Late-pregnant mare","history":"A mare grazes endophyte-infected tall fescue and develops prolonged gestation, scant mammary development and a large placenta; foal is weak or stillborn.","gross":"Thick heavy edematous placenta, poor lactation and prolonged gestation; fetal compromise varies.","histo":"Nonspecific placental edema and altered maturation without characteristic infectious inclusions.","best":"Endophyte-infected tall fescue reproductive toxicosis","morph":"Placenta: edema and placental insufficiency with abnormal gestational endocrinology","mechanism":"Ergot alkaloids from endophyte-infected fescue suppress prolactin and disrupt reproductive endocrine/vascular function, producing agalactia and prolonged gestation.","tests":"Pasture/exposure history, reproductive timeline and exclusion of infection; maternal prolactin where relevant.","ref":"L77 slide 24","options":[{"name":"Endophyte-infected tall fescue reproductive toxicosis","supports":"Species, signalment, lesion morphology and microscopic pattern provide the most consistent diagnosis.","against":"The diagnosis is not verified by gross appearance alone; use the specified confirmatory test and consider competing causes.","hist":"Nonspecific placental edema and altered maturation without characteristic infectious inclusions.","role":"best"},{"name":"Equine herpesvirus 1","supports":"Placental abnormality and stillbirth possible.","against":"Evidence of transplacental viral fetal infection and EHV PCR supports infection.","hist":"Fetal necrotizing lesions and intranuclear inclusions.","role":"less"},{"name":"Twin-related placental insufficiency","supports":"Poor placental exchange and late loss possible.","against":"Two conceptuses with characteristic avillous placental contact zones.","hist":"Noninflammatory placental exchange loss.","role":"less"},{"name":"Ascending placentitis","supports":"Placental thickening and loss possible.","against":"Focal cervical-star exudation and suppurative inflammation.","hist":"Neutrophilic placentitis.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"e78-kcs","lecture":"78","system":"Eye","title":"Dog with thick ocular discharge and dry dull cornea","species":"Middle-aged dog","history":"A dog has chronic mucoid ocular discharge, recurrent superficial ulcers and a dull poorly wetted corneal surface; Schirmer tear test is low.","gross":"Thick ropy discharge, conjunctival hyperemia, vascularized/pigmented dry cornea and superficial epithelial erosion.","histo":"Chronic superficial keratitis, squamous metaplasia of conjunctiva and goblet-cell changes with secondary ulceration.","best":"Keratoconjunctivitis sicca (KCS)","morph":"Eye: chronic desiccation-associated keratoconjunctivitis","mechanism":"Loss of adequate aqueous tears compromises lubrication and antimicrobial defenses, creating inflammatory epithelial injury and secondary pigmentation/vascularization.","tests":"Schirmer tear testing and full ophthalmic exam; evaluate immune-mediated lacrimal damage, drug-related causes and neurogenic disease.","ref":"L78 slide 32","options":[{"name":"Keratoconjunctivitis sicca (KCS)","supports":"Species, signalment, lesion morphology and microscopic pattern provide the most consistent diagnosis.","against":"The diagnosis is not verified by gross appearance alone; use the specified confirmatory test and consider competing causes.","hist":"Chronic superficial keratitis, squamous metaplasia of conjunctiva and goblet-cell changes with secondary ulceration.","role":"best"},{"name":"Simple bacterial conjunctivitis","supports":"Discharge and redness overlap.","against":"Normal aqueous tear production and less chronic corneal pigmentation favor conjunctivitis.","hist":"Conjunctival inflammation with limited corneal desiccation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Entropion","supports":"Corneal ulcer and discharge can occur.","against":"Eyelid inversion/hair rubbing is the primary lesion; Schirmer may be normal.","hist":"Focal frictional ulcerative keratitis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Chronic superficial keratitis (pannus)","supports":"Vascularization and pigmentation of cornea occur.","against":"Adequate tears and distinctive superficial immune corneal distribution differ.","hist":"Superficial lymphoplasmacytic corneal infiltrate.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"e78-feline-iris","lecture":"78","system":"Eye","title":"Cat with progressive dark iris pigmentation and glaucoma","species":"Older cat","history":"Patchy iris pigmentation gradually coalesces and thickens; the pupil becomes distorted, and the eye develops secondary high intraocular pressure.","gross":"Diffuse irregular iris darkening and thickening, possible distortion of pupil and invasion of drainage angle.","histo":"Atypical melanocytes infiltrate iris stroma, potentially ciliary body and sclera, with mitoses and vascular invasion influencing prognosis.","best":"Feline diffuse iris melanoma","morph":"Iris: infiltrative melanocytic neoplasm","mechanism":"Neoplastic melanocytes expand within iris and may obstruct aqueous drainage, contributing to secondary glaucoma and potential metastasis.","tests":"Serial ophthalmic photography, tonometry, specialist evaluation and histopathology if globe removed; distinguish from benign iris melanosis.","ref":"L78 slide 46","options":[{"name":"Feline diffuse iris melanoma","supports":"Species, signalment, lesion morphology and microscopic pattern provide the most consistent diagnosis.","against":"The diagnosis is not verified by gross appearance alone; use the specified confirmatory test and consider competing causes.","hist":"Atypical melanocytes infiltrate iris stroma, potentially ciliary body and sclera, with mitoses and vascular invasion influencing prognosis.","role":"best"},{"name":"Feline iris melanosis","supports":"Pigmented spots may look similar.","against":"Flat, noninfiltrative melanocytic increase without cellular invasion and progressive distortion.","hist":"Benign increased melanocytes without destructive invasion.","role":"less"},{"name":"Anterior uveitis with pigmentation","supports":"Iris changes and glaucoma possible.","against":"Inflammatory cells and synechiae rather than infiltrative atypical melanoma.","hist":"Lymphoplasmacytic uveitis.","role":"less"},{"name":"Iris cyst","supports":"Dark intraocular spot can appear.","against":"Smooth cystic cavity rather than expanding pigmented iris tissue.","hist":"Thin epithelial-lined fluid cyst.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"e78-phacoclastic","lecture":"78","system":"Eye","title":"Rabbit with ruptured lens capsule and granulomatous uveitis","species":"Young rabbit","history":"A rabbit has focal cataract, intense anterior uveitis and imaging evidence of focal lens capsule rupture; history suggests Encephalitozoon-associated lens infection.","gross":"Focal or diffuse white cataract and disrupted lens capsule with severe ocular inflammation.","histo":"Granulomatous or pyogranulomatous inflammation centered on exposed lens proteins at capsule rupture, with lens debris.","best":"Phacoclastic lens-induced uveitis","morph":"Lens/uvea: granulomatous uveitis secondary to capsule disruption","mechanism":"Lens protein escapes a breached capsule and stimulates a vigorous immune/foreign-body response; unlike phacolytic disease, capsule rupture is central.","tests":"Slit-lamp/ocular ultrasound, assess lens capsule and consider E. cuniculi evidence in rabbits; correlate histology.","ref":"L78 slide 43","options":[{"name":"Phacoclastic lens-induced uveitis","supports":"Species, signalment, lesion morphology and microscopic pattern provide the most consistent diagnosis.","against":"The diagnosis is not verified by gross appearance alone; use the specified confirmatory test and consider competing causes.","hist":"Granulomatous or pyogranulomatous inflammation centered on exposed lens proteins at capsule rupture, with lens debris.","role":"best"},{"name":"Phacolytic uveitis","supports":"Cataract-associated immune inflammation overlaps.","against":"Lens capsule is largely intact with protein leakage rather than overt rupture.","hist":"Lens-related uveitis without clear capsular tear.","role":"less"},{"name":"Primary infectious uveitis","supports":"Ocular inflammation and granulomas possible.","against":"Infection might initiate capsule injury, but lens protein exposure explains the lens-centered reaction.","hist":"Granulomatous uveitis with infectious evidence as appropriate.","role":"less"},{"name":"Primary glaucoma","supports":"Pain and corneal edema can occur.","against":"Elevated IOP and optic nerve injury rather than rupture-centered lens inflammation.","hist":"Pressure damage to drainage angle and retina.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"ear79-chondritis","lecture":"79","system":"Ear","title":"Young cat with bilateral curled painful pinnae","species":"Two-year-old cat","history":"Both ear flaps become swollen and painful with reduced flexibility, curled margins and variable crusting; no clear external-ear canal infection.","gross":"Bilateral thickened distorted erythematous pinnae with loss of cartilage firmness.","histo":"Lymphoplasmacytic to granulomatous inflammation of auricular cartilage with altered cartilage matrix staining and degeneration.","best":"Auricular chondritis","morph":"Pinna: bilateral inflammatory degenerative chondritis","mechanism":"Immune-associated cartilage inflammation damages auricular supporting cartilage, leading to swelling and deformation rather than a simple hematoma cavity.","tests":"Histopathology when indicated, exclude traumatic auricular hematoma and infectious chondritis, examine other cartilaginous structures.","ref":"L79 slide 11","options":[{"name":"Auricular chondritis","supports":"Species, signalment, lesion morphology and microscopic pattern provide the most consistent diagnosis.","against":"The diagnosis is not verified by gross appearance alone; use the specified confirmatory test and consider competing causes.","hist":"Lymphoplasmacytic to granulomatous inflammation of auricular cartilage with altered cartilage matrix staining and degeneration.","role":"best"},{"name":"Aural hematoma","supports":"Sudden thick swollen pinna occurs.","against":"Fluid-filled cavity associated with head shaking versus diffuse cartilage inflammation.","hist":"Dissecting hemorrhage and organizing fibrovascular tissue.","role":"less"},{"name":"Otitis externa","supports":"Pain, scratching and crusts possible.","against":"Ear canal-centered inflammation, not a diffuse bilateral cartilage-centered lesion.","hist":"Surface canal epidermal inflammation and gland changes.","role":"less"},{"name":"Auricular infarction","supports":"Crusty ear tips and necrosis possible.","against":"Ischemic distal margin change and vascular thrombosis rather than diffuse chondritis.","hist":"Vascular thrombosis and ischemic necrosis.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"ear79-dentigerous","lecture":"79","system":"Ear","title":"Foal with draining cystic swelling at base of ear","species":"Young horse","history":"A foal has a persistent draining opening near the base of the pinna. Imaging detects toothlike mineralized material in a congenital cystic cavity.","gross":"Periauricular cyst containing irregular developing toothlike structures, sometimes draining a sinus.","histo":"Squamous epithelial-lined cyst with odontogenic dental tissues including dentin/enamel-related structures.","best":"Equine dentigerous cyst","morph":"Periauricular region: congenital odontogenic cyst","mechanism":"Ectopic tooth-forming tissue within a developmental cyst produces persistent cystic swelling and discharge.","tests":"Imaging to define dental structures and anatomy; histopathology after excision confirms odontogenic tissue.","ref":"L79 slide 14","options":[{"name":"Equine dentigerous cyst","supports":"Species, signalment, lesion morphology and microscopic pattern provide the most consistent diagnosis.","against":"The diagnosis is not verified by gross appearance alone; use the specified confirmatory test and consider competing causes.","hist":"Squamous epithelial-lined cyst with odontogenic dental tissues including dentin/enamel-related structures.","role":"best"},{"name":"Auricular abscess","supports":"Draining fistula and swelling can resemble cyst.","against":"Suppurative cavity without organized odontogenic tooth structures.","hist":"Neutrophil-rich abscess capsule.","role":"less"},{"name":"Branchial cleft cyst","supports":"Congenital periauricular cyst possible.","against":"Does not contain dental tissues.","hist":"Epithelial cyst lining without odontogenic differentiation.","role":"less"},{"name":"Aural inflammatory polyp","supports":"Ear-associated mass and discharge possible.","against":"Inflammatory fibrovascular polyp rather than mineralized ectopic tooth structures.","hist":"Polypoid mucosa with inflammatory stromal infiltrate.","role":"less"}]},{"id":"ear79-bovis-otitis","lecture":"79","system":"Ear","title":"Calf with otitis media and neurologic cranial nerve signs","species":"Young calf","history":"A calf from a respiratory disease outbreak develops unilateral drooping ear, head tilt and purulent material within a tympanic bulla; other calves have pneumonia.","gross":"Tan-yellow inspissated exudate filling middle ear/bulla, possibly bone remodeling and adjacent inflammatory spread.","histo":"Suppurative to chronic-active otitis media, possible osseous involvement and extension toward cranial nerves/brainstem.","best":"Mycoplasma bovis-associated otitis media","morph":"Middle ear: severe suppurative otitis media in calf","mechanism":"Respiratory-associated spread to the middle ear can injure local nerves and, in severe disease, extend to adjacent structures. Similar gross lesions can have several bacterial agents.","tests":"Tympanic bulla sampling and Mycoplasma PCR/culture when appropriate; examine lungs and differentiate Histophilus, Pasteurella and Trueperella.","ref":"L79 slide 18","options":[{"name":"Mycoplasma bovis-associated otitis media","supports":"Species, signalment, lesion morphology and microscopic pattern provide the most consistent diagnosis.","against":"The diagnosis is not verified by gross appearance alone; use the specified confirmatory test and consider competing causes.","hist":"Suppurative to chronic-active otitis media, possible osseous involvement and extension toward cranial nerves/brainstem.","role":"best"},{"name":"Histophilus somni-associated otitis media","supports":"Calf otitis, brainstem signs and pneumonia can overlap.","against":"Organism identification and concurrent vascular/neuro lesions distinguish.","hist":"Suppurative middle ear inflammation, occasionally vascular injury.","role":"less"},{"name":"Pasteurella multocida otitis media","supports":"Respiratory association and middle ear exudate overlap.","against":"Requires bacteriology; gross lesions alone cannot separate.","hist":"Suppurative otitis media.","role":"less"},{"name":"Aural cholesteatoma","supports":"Bulla lesion can be destructive and cause neurologic signs.","against":"Keratinizing squamous epithelial cyst with laminated keratin, not primarily purulent middle ear infection.","hist":"Keratin-filled cyst with epithelial lining and bone reaction.","role":"less"}]}]};
